      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>FORTUNE-TELLER FINED. Amusing Evidence in a Warrington</head>
        <p>FORTUNE-TELLER FINED. Amusing Evidence in a Warrington</p>
        <p>APPENDICITIS AND LOVE.</p>
        <p>Warrington police have joined in the crusade against fortune-tel lets and the prosecution of "Madame Eloise" (Elizabeth Gertrude Kilner) afforded no small amount of amusement at the Police Court on Monday. The prosecution was under the Vagrancy Act which pro- scribes the "using of subtle craft or device to deceive or impose upon certain of his Majesties subjects," Madame being charged with imposing upon people by the use of palmistry, three informations being laid against her. Mr. Stanley Wilson appeared to prosecute, Mr. Browne representing the defendant, who pleaded not guilty. Mr. Wilson stated that the defendant had for some time been practising as a palmist and clairvoyant at her residence, 5, Thelwall-lane, the penalty for which was imprisonment for not more than three months, or a fine not exceeding £25. On the 2nd March the house was watched between the hours of 6.15 p.m., and 9.15, when 23 females were seen to enter. The following day the house was again watched and six females were seen to enter. He quoted those figures to show that the defend- ant had been patronised by a good many foolish people. When arrested on Saturday night by Superintendent 31onaghan, Madame said she had never told fortunes, but only read the palms of those who visited her. P.C. Hardy gave evidence as to having watched the defendant's house and seen a largo number of females enter. _</p>
        <p>Mr Browne: Have you not seen some of the highest people in Warrington visit the house? Witness : I have seen some very respectable peeple there.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: Have you not seen a carriage and pair there?</p>
        <p>7 Witness : No, sir. Catherine Rylatt, wife of a member of the Warrington Police force, visited defendant on the 2nd of March and had her hand read. She was told she had had a pro- posal of marriage when she was 26 and would have another when she reached the age of 20 yelps. Asked if she knew a man under Government who wore a uniform she replied in the affirmative. Further questioned she said that man was not dear to her. Serious illnesses were foretold. Madame further informed her that she had a relative who was seriously ill and, said witness, "She advised me if I had that relative insured to keep up the payment." For her services Madame Eloise received the sum of Is.— the fee charged in every case. In answer to Mr. Browne Mrs. Rylatt said she did not believe what the defendant told her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>ADVICE ON LOVE AFFAIRS.</head>
        <p>ADVICE ON LOVE AFFAIRS.</p>
        <p>Ellen Wood (17) Morley-street,a bright, prepossessing young lady learnt as the result of having her hand read, that there was a particular boy whom she liked very much, but that she must not have him, but must keep him at a distance. She would have a proposal of marriage before she was twenty which she must not accept, being told to wait for a similar offer which would be made when she reached the age of 22. Her matrimonial prospects, as foretold by the defendant, were not, however, particu- larly alluring, as trouble arising out of the intemperance of relatives was predicted. Miss Wood received the further not particularly pleasant information, that unless she took care "she would have appendicitis like the King and Princess Victoria," and was also told that she must abstain from eating pastry and sweets. She also learnt that she would have five children, and that she must not marry a drunkard, as if she did she would fret herself to death. "A QUEER-TEMPERED MAN •</p>
        <p>On the 3rd March the wife of Detective Clough -visited the defendant, and learnt that she had 9. husband who was a "queer tempered man with whom not every- one could live, but that she understood him." Trouble with relations was foretold but a little consolation was afforded by the defendant predicting that Mrs. Clough would become prosperous and that her relations would look up to her.</p>
        <p>Thi;called forth the remark from Mr. Wilson that Detective Clough might soon expect the promotion he deserved. Elizabeth Deakin, a domestic servant, whose hand the defendant read, was informed that she had two lovers— one of whom she knew—and that it would be her own fault if she did not marry between the ages of 20 and 25 On the 30th January,saidMr. Wilson, Mrs. Williamson 145, Longford street, visited the defendant and was told she would shortly flit,that she would do well in business and that she would have trouble with one of her daughters. Miss Williamson, who accompanied her mother was told that she would receive a letter from a soldier which :vould contain a valuable present,and that she would marry between the ages of 20 and 22. These people, submitted Mr. Wilson, had been imposed upon and practically robbed by the defendant. After hearing the evidence the defendant on the advice of Mr. Browne, withdrew her plea of not guilty. Mr. Browne said some 12 months ago the defendant was advised by his late partner not to attempt to pre- dict the future though unfortunately she had apparently not followed that advice. When people visited her she asked them if they would have their characters read by phrenology on palmistry. She had made a study of phrenology and for two or three years had practised at Blackpool without interruption from the police. Phrenology was perfectly legal and the defendant would' give an undertaking that in the future she would prac- tise nothing else. He would suggest that upon that undertaking the imposing of a small fine would meet the case. She worked to maintain her husband who was suffering from consumption. The Chief Constable: Her husband is a tradesman in full work.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: He is consumptive. The magistrates retired for a few minutes and upon their return the Mayor announced that they had decided to fine the defendant £2O and costs (223 Oa. in all) or in default two months imprisonment. Mr. Wilson applied for a fee of 10s. 6d. for each witness and for the advocate's fee. The magistrates intimated that they had allowed Bs. for the witnesses which they considered sufficient. Mr. Browne asked that the defendant should be allowed time in which to pay the fine as her husband was not working on full time. The Chief Constable opposed the application and the magistrates decided to leave the matter in his hands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>CHRONIC BILIOUSNESS CURED. Another Bile Bean Triumph. 20 YEARS OF ILLNESS ENDED.</head>
        <p>CHRONIC BILIOUSNESS CURED. Another Bile Bean Triumph. 20 YEARS OF ILLNESS ENDED.</p>
        <p>Another case of long-standing biliousness having been cured by Bile Beans is reported. The subject is Mrs. Clara Perry, of 108, Bucknall New-road, Hanley, who says : " For nearly twenty years I suffered from biliousness and allied ailments. I used to vomit a quantity of nasty green bile, and when these attacks were on I was fearfully dizzy and weak. All was confused. My eyes would be dimmed, and palpitation would be very bad. One of the many doctors I have tried told me I had bilious fever.</p>
        <p>dc • Eight or nine doctors I tried in St. Helens and Hanley, and all they could do was to give me relief for a day or so, when I should be as bad as ever again. Sometimes when I had been to the doctor's surgery my biliousness would be so bad that I became quite blind for a time. I was unable to see anything ! I also had liver complaint—a family ailment. I spent pounds on doctor's bills; and for ten months I was an out-patient at the North Staffordshire Infirmary. They said at last they could give me no more medicine, as it did me no go2d- About this time I saw Bile Beans advertised, and my husband recommended me to try them. I said to him in despair. What is the good? I can get no better.' He pressed me to give Bile Beans a trial, and at last I did so. I was very bad in bed at the time, but soon after I began with the Beans I felt a marked im- provement. I persevered with them' and soon the sick- ness began to leave me. I naturally continued with their use, and I kept to them until I had taken thirteen Choir Social. boxes. The result is that they have restored me to perfect health, and all the old complaints are now There was a good attendance of the members of the ended. Whereas my food would not remain on my Church of the Sacred Heart choir at the first annual stomach, I can now heartily enjoy my meals, and never social, which was held in the Dmids Hall, Sankey- feel ill-effects afterwards. ' Mr. Perry heartily corroborated his wife's statement, street, on Tuesday evening. A capital programme of saying that Bile Beans had really worked a miracle in sow"'n had been prepared by the choirmaster, Mr. J. her case. ~.•-ibbins, all items being giveh in an able manner by The medicine which worked this wonderful cure is the members of the choir. Mr. George Coggins gave Purely vegetable, and is capable of curing the most several Irish comic and nigger songs, and was difficult and stubborn wises of biliousness, indigestion, heartily applauded. Refreshments were served during anmmia, constipation, piles, debility., headache, liver: the intervals, and dancing was kept up with spirit and kidney disorders, nervousness, pimples, face sores, 1 until half-past eleven. Master W. Hai diman was the blood impurities, and all female ailments. All chemists stock Bile Beans at Is. 1111. or 2s. 9d.2.5. 9d.(bOZ contail three times 11d. size). Ayold substitute; accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>RENEWED PROVISIONALLY AT THE ADJOURNED SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RENEWED PROVISIONALLY AT THE ADJOURNED SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>The Question of Compensation,</p>
        <p>The consideration of the renewal of the George Hotel licence was the chief business at the adjourned Licensing Sessions held at the Police Court on Tuesday. The effect of the Licensing Committee's decision is that the hotel will remain open until the Compensa- tion kuthority has assessed the rate of compensation to be paid in accordance with the new Act. April 30th is the earliest date on which the authority can meet so that several months will probably elapse before the matter is finally setded.The members of the Licens- ing Committee present on Tuesday were Mr. John Fairclough (chairman), Alderman Francomb, Dr. Fox, Messrs. C. White, J. Geddes, and H. Roberts, while the other magistrates on the Bench were :—Alder- man Smethurst, Alderman Bennett, Messrs. C. S. Burgess, J. W. Hayward, E. Woods, E. J. Guest, S. Wright, W. Roberts, L. W. Reynolds, H. Thornton, and C. Jackson Holmes.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he wished it to be understood that the business was entirely confined to the Licensing Committee.</p>
        <p>APPLICATION FOR A BILLIARD LICENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Browne applied on behalf of prank Berry, of the Glassmaker's Arms, Brick-street, for a billiard licence. Mr. Berry, he said, had kept the house for five years. Previously he was a licencee in the borough for about eleven years and no offence whatever had been alleged against him. All he wanted was permission to have a semi-billiard table. There was no provision of that kind in the neighbourhood either in the direction of the Barracks or down Church-street. The room was quite suitable, being 16ft. 41n. long and 12ft. 61n. wide and lift, high. The Chief Constable had intimated that he would not oppose the application. The Chief Constable.: That is so, your Worships. We have examined the room and it fulfills the requirements am far as the police are concerned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>GEORGE HOTEL. STRONG OBJECTIONS TO A RENEWAL.</head>
        <p>THE GEORGE HOTEL. STRONG OBJECTIONS TO A RENEWAL.</p>
        <p>IMr- Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk) said he appeared on behalf of Mr. Thomas Halton, licencee of the George and Dragon Hotel, and also on behalf of the Corporation, who were the owners of the premises, to ask for a renewal of the licence. The Bench would be well aware of the circumstances in connection with the licence and the property in general, and he did not pro- pose to enter into details at that point. Mr. Thomas Halton was called and gave formal evi- dence in support of the application. Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, said it would be within the recollection of the macistrates that he made an applica- tion at the annual Licensing Sessions for the matter of the renewal of the licence to be allowed to stand over in order that he might give the necessary notice of objection on behalf of certain persons for whom he appeared. Since then notice of objection had been I served on behalf of Mr. Francis Dixon, the Rev. J. F. Matthews, and Mr. Thomas Dale, and in addition to those gentlemen he appeared on behalf of several others, including the Rev. C. Harvey Cook, minister of the Unitarian Church in Cairo-street. The grounds of objection urged by his clients were :—(1) that the licence is not required to meet the wants of the neighbourhood ; (2) That the existence of the licence renders the number of licensed houses in the neighbour- hood excessive ; (3) That having regard to the change in the character of the neighbourhood during the last five years owing to the demolition of property the con- tinuance of the licence is undesirable; (4) That in the general interests of the public the renewal of the licence is not desirable. In support of the first clause he would point out that the district was not a residential one,but was practically a district of lock-up shops,and he thought when their worships considered the surrounding neighbourhood they wolld agree that the licence was not necessary. With regard to the second ground of objection, he would ask their worships to stand, in imagination, at Market Gate, and look at the number of licensed houses in Buttermarket street, Horsemarket street, Sanke y street, and other off-streets. He thought they would be of opinion that 'there were quite sufficient houses to meet the requirements of the neighbourhood. The magistrates were quite entitled, apart from the evidence he would call, to take into consideration their own local knowledge. He might have been able to say something with regard to the structural condition of the premises but he did not wish to urge that as a ground of objection. The notice stated that a renewal was undesirable, but he would go a step further and say it was impossible to continue the licence under the circumstances. The Corporation had made arrangements for the taking down of property on that side of Bridge- street and for the rebuilding of certain shops. The Chairman: I think we might dispose of that part. We all know about that. Mr. Steel then dealt with the contention that it was desirable in the general interests of the public that the licence should not be renewed. He would remind their worships that as a Licens- ing Bench they were a body interposed between the publican and the public for the protection of the public, and he would emphasise that point. They had already had an application before the Bench for the transfer of the licence to another part of the town. Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons, Ltd., had the selection 1 from the whole town, and they chose what, in their I opinion, was the best site. The Bench, however, decided that they would not allow a transference of the licence. When he made the application for an adjournment, it was suggested that their Worships should consider the question of transfering the licence to a house, the owner of which was prepared to give £l,OOO for the licence. He submitted that that was not a matter which ought to come within their consideration. The Bench had from time to time decided that a full licence was not required for the particular neighbourhood in which the house was situated—Bank Quay. The objectors did not wish the Bench to do an injustice to anyone. The new Act gave their Worships power to grant compensation to a licence holder and to the owners. Therefore the Corporation would be entitled to com- pensation and by refusing a renewal the Bench would not be doing them wrong or injury. He submitted they would be doing a great benefit to the public in whose interest they were expected to act. The Rev. J. F. Matthews was called and said he had subscribed his signature to the notice of objection. He objected to the renewal on the grounds stated by Mr. Steel. Mr. Thomas Dale gave similar formal evidence. Mr. Wilson said the Corporation were in a difficulty with regard to the licence. The only question in which they were concerned was that of compensation. Whether they obtained a payment from Messrs. Walker for the licence or receive 4 compensation in accordance with the new Act mattered little to them, but he was instructed to say that they could not afford, unless they were compelled, to allow the licence to lapse. They had purchased the premises at an enormous cost and they thought the ratepayers were justly entitled to compensation from some direction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>DECISIONS.</head>
        <p>THE DECISIONS.</p>
        <p>The Licensing Committee had a private consultation and on their return into Court, the Chairman said the application for a billiard licence for the Glassmakers, Arms was refused. The licence of the George Hotel would be renewed provisionally and the considera- tion of compensation would be referred to the Compen- sation Authority.</p>
        <p>St. Mary's Dance. About 180 eouples were present at the annual dance in connection with St. Mary's (R. C.) Church which was held in the Parr Hall on Monday evening. Admirable arrangements had been made, and dancing commenced at 8 and was kept up with enthusiasm until 2.30. The music was in the capable hands of Mr. R. Whitfield's band, and the selection of pieces gave general satisfaction. Messrs. P. Delaney, C. Wells, J. W. Bibby, and T. Rigby ably discharged the duties of M.C. Ladies of the parish had charge of the refreshments which gave every satisfactions The success of the dance was in no small measure due to the following committees which were responsible, for the arrangements : Laciies,Mrs. P. Bate(president), Mesdames Walker, Lowe, Lightburn, Corneilly, Wells, Delaney, Bate, Titmas, G. Dolan, Reardon, Toole. T. Curran, 0. Toole, T. Rigby, H. Rigby, Talon, J Dolan, Boyle and Rabbit, and the Misses Wallington' Wells, Robinson, Caldwell, Bibby, Kiernan, Qurran; Hazlehurst, Dolan and M. Riey. Gentlemen: Rev. J. McGrath (chairman),Rev. }. D'Arcy (vice-chairman and treasurer), Messrs. W. Madden, E. Caldwell, G. Dolan, 0.11. Wells, J. Devlin, H. Rigby, jun., P. Bate T. McCann, A. J. Titmas, J. Mather, if. J. Knight, J. Rabbit, T. Rigby, T. Curran, H. Rigby, G. Johnson, P. Delaney, 0. Toole, C. H. Unsworth, J. Dolan and G. T. Bibby, the energetic hon. secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meetin,, of the Ramcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>KNUTSFORD LIGHT RAILWAY. The Light Railway Commissioners intimated that they would hold a public inquiry at the Town Hall War- rington on Tuesday, March 14th, as to the expediency of granting the applicatiox which had been made to them for an order to authorise the above named light railway,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>HELSBY WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>HELSBY WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>At a special meeting of the 'Parochial Coln- mittee and a committee from the District Council it was reported that the specifications for the waterworks (No 1) contract had been examined and reduced to £270 by making certain alterations. Mr. Hugh Davies had reduced his tender to £270 and the committee recom- mended its acceptance. Mr. Guest thought other tenderers should have been approached and asked to revise their tenders. If that had been done the work would probably have been done for £250, as originally three lower tenders had been submitted than the one it was proposed to accept. The minutes were approved. FARMER COMPENSATED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward reported that the Isolation Hospital committee recommended the payment of £26 to the tenant of the land upon which the hospital was built as compensation. The recommendation of the committee was approved. -- PARENTS TO RE PROSECUTED. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer reported that cases of scarlet fever had occurred at Norley. The parents of the chil- dren had not reported the outbreak even though a rash had been noticeable, and after he visited the house the children were alio% ed to go out playing in the roadway. It was decided to prosecute the parents for their neglect to notify the outbreak of the disease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>ROADNV_AY IN DANGER.</head>
        <p>ROADNV_AY IN DANGER.</p>
        <p>M., Mounfleld stated that in consequebee of the dredging of the Iriver, the Council's road at Walton Arches was in danger. The sides of the road had already collapsed, and unless something was done the roadway itself would be damaged. It was decided to write to the Manchester Ship Canal Company calling their attentieu to the matter.</p>
