      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>UP-TO-DATE BODICE.</head>
        <p>UP-TO-DATE BODICE.</p>
        <p>Loose bodies are a thing of the past, and it is now decreed that smart women must wear clothes that follow closely the lines of the figure. We are told that the waists of to-day are much smaller than those of yesterday. This is only partially true. The cut of the up-to-date bodice, with its long point in front and tightly-drawn drapery or care- fully arranged lines, tends to make the waists appear smaller than they did in pouching and full bodices. Nevertheless, it is to be feared that the</p>
        <p>new order of fashion will tend to tight-lacing oh the part of many foolish and misguided women. Our sketch shews a draped bodice having the bust drapery caught up by rows of tiny velvet bows. The rucked sleeves end in a tight lace cuff, and are ornamented with tiny bows similar to those on the bodice. The yoke, collar, and belt are of fine lace, latticed and strapped with narrow bands of the plain material. A bodice of this description is, of course, a thing apart from the blouse slip, since it must be made ou a well-boned, attached lining.</p>
        <p>TEE HUSBAND AS HELP.</p>
        <p>In the Young 717ontait Miss Mary Halliday dis- cusses the problem suggested by the difficulty of getting servants, "How to manage without a maid." In her view the husband should be pressed into the service. "He should clean the boots, carry the coals, and clean the knives in the morn- ing, protecting his hands by wearing an old pair of gloves the while he performs his 'chores. Some women are prejudiced on this point (and some men). but surely a manly man will take matters into his own hands. There is a comradeship in working together for, a common end that brings pleasure in its wake. I know a man who looks back upon the servantless period, when he and his wife between them managed the work of their house, as almost the 'pleasantest in his life." Itscrem. _ _</p>
        <p>CHICKENS A Ls TURINOISE. —Single, draw, and truss two fat spring chickens, put a piece of butter inside each, cover the breasts with slices of lemon, then lard with slices of fat bacon, tie up the chickens in cloths, and put them in a stewpan with an onion stuck with two cloves. Cover with boiling white stock, hying to the boil again, and cook for forty minutes. Meanwhile, chop an onion, ctit 20z. of raw ham into dice, and fry these a light brown in 20z.„0f butter in a stewpan; then add 11b. of rice; fry this lightly, then add a quart of the chicken broth, and cook till the rice is done; dry it thoroughly, toss it in a little freshly oiled butter, then make a bed of it on a hot dish, arrange the cut-up chickens on it, pour well. iltoilnced WNW 6411G8 crreri and serve,</p>
