      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>LIBERAL MEETING AT STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL MEETING AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>THE SHORTCOMINGS OF THE GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES BY MR. W. H. LEVER AND MR. H. ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Liberals are actively continuing the advocacy of the candidature of Mr. King in the Knuts- ford Division, and under the auspices of their Associa- tion a meeting was held in the Providence schoolroom on Tuesday evening. Despite the very inclement state of the weather there was a large attendance, over which Mr. Josiah Longland presided, being supported by Messrs. W. H. Lever (Port Sunlight), IL Roberts, W. H. Sherburn, A. H. Dunn, J. Mounfield, E. Lloyd-Jones, and the Rev. J. S. Hughes.</p>
        <p>The Chairman at th; outset apologised for the non-at- tendance of Mr. A. H. Crosfleld who had been announced to speak. Mr. Crosfleld was fully intending to be present but had been unexpectedly called away to London upon important business,and regretted very much that he would be unable to attend. He would like to say a word or two as to the argument advanced by protectionists that the consumer would not pay any duty which would be imposed upon goods coming into this country. A few weeks before hersaw a letter in a weekly journal from a gentlemen who had made enquiries in New York as to the prices of certain articles which could be obtained at grocers' shops: He divided his argu- ments into three heads, goods manufactured in England, American products, and goods imported both into England and America. 11l tile first case good.s manufactured in England sold in London for 10s. 1.00. and in New, York for 16s, 6d. Goods of American manufacture sold in London for 10s. 3d. and in7America for us. sd. Other goods imported both into England and America, which put the countries upon exactly the same footing, the London price was 17s. 40. and the New York price 245. 9d. That showed most conclusively with regard to that particular article at any rate whether the duty was payable by the consumer or by the producer. In a recent contract for some iron work required in Madrid the English price quoted was 10s. 6d. per ton exclusive of duty, and 16s. 11d, inclusive of duty. The price quoted by Spanish firms who were competing was only 2s. 6d. per ton less than the English price. From that it would be seen that those for whom the work was done bad to pay not only the cost of iron but also the costtof the duty. (Applause.) We had. had</p>
        <p>THE BEOS4iNING OF PROTECTION in our experience of the sugar convention. At the present time sugar had so advanced in price that taking an average family of five persons it was costing £2 a year for sugar for that family more than it cost before the convention was entered upon. If the price of sugar was so affected it was evident that there would be a similar increase in the price of other things and that not only the workingman but the middle class would feel the increased cost of living acutely. (Applause.) That therefore was a matter upon which it behoved everyone to think very seriously and when they had considered the matter seriously he had very little doubt that the difficulty would be solved in favour of a continuance of free trade and that they would work and vote for the candidate who supported free trade— (applause)—and not for Mr. Egerton who had upon every available opportunity consistently advocated pro- tection. (Applause.) Mr. W. H. Lever, who was received with applause, congratulated the Liberals of [the Knutsford division upon their selection of a candidate whom he was per- fectly sure they would return with an overwelming majority et the generrl election. (Applause). When the present Government was placed in power they did not include in their programme the policy which they have adopted since, bad they done so they would not have had the confidence of the country. (Applause). The only candidates entitled to the confidence of the people were chose having at heart the interests of their particular constituency as a division and of the empire as a whole. It was upon the latter that the conserva- tives claimed they were entitled to confidence, but they overlooked the fact that it was during the last 50 years that the empire had made the greatest strides, and that for two thirds'of the period the Government had been in the bands of the Liberals. Through the mismanage- ment of a Conservative government we' had lost our best colony—the United States—which had now become our greatest competitor all over the world. The Conserva- tive Government claimed the confidence of the people but if the people compared their promises at the election in 1900 with theirl performances since then the country would have no reason totioubt the result of the next election. (Hear, hear). .</p>
        <p>OLD-AGE PENSIONS were promised, but that promise WAS not carried out on account of the great expense which the scheme would involve, whereas they had spent more in grants in aid than would have been sufficient te provide for old-age pensions. 'limy would, however, never get a really representative Government of the people, fo: the people end by the people until members of Parliament were paid, and their election expenses paid by the people. The present system under which a candidate bad,tb face a heavy initial expense, and if elected had to face the expense of living in London, was an altogethet wrong system, and would only be altered by a Liberal Government, and be made possible for a candidate to be selected by the neople unfettered by any consideration whatever. (Applause.) The policy of protection NVB 9 proposed as means of indirect taxation upon goods consumed by the masses of the people which would result in the payment by the working man of more than his fair share of taxa- tior, moreover the adoption of a system of protection would lead to the formation of trusts and to the corrup- tion of ourpolitical life which at the present time was i the purest n the world. The tobacco Industry had en- joyed protection to • the extent of is. per pound with the result that a tiust had bean formed, foreign tobaccos of the commoner type were prevented from entering the country and every time a man bought half- an-ounce of toba co he paid three farthings for the benefit of a certain number of wealthy men who eontrolled the English trade. Mr. Lever illustiAted the inaccuracy of the statement that because our imports exec 3ded our exports trade was bad, and showed that in all countries which. were progressing the imports exceeded the exports, and that the contrary was boticed in the case of decaying rations. We were raising annually an enormous sum in imperial and local tavation and it was because something must be done to raise that sum that alteration in our fiscal policy was prorosed.Mr. Lever proceeded to illustrate the inequality of the present system of taxation showing how, while a workingman had virtually to give 10 weeks' work every year to pay his rates and taxes the upper classes had to give only three or four weeks. The Protectionist proposals instead of relieving the working man would increase his burden and would decrease the burden of those better able to pay the taxes. Was the working man going to take it lying down or VMS he going to make the millionaire by limns of a</p>
        <p>GRADUATED SYSTEM OF TAXATION bear his fair burden ? (Applause.) aianufacturers were told they were to get their raw materials free and be would like to illustrate the inequality of the protec- tionist proposals. The greatest industq of the country was farming and the faimer's raw materill %vim his land and if he was to be treated fairly all restrictions upon his land should be removed and that he should be given security on all improvements on his land. (Hear, hear.) The taxation of land values was another question which required attrition and a proper system of taxation introduced. (Applause.) Retaliation had been tried and had failed., Moreover all countries under a system of protection were retaliating against other countries which put up tariffs' and we had not yet seen a single country on its knees. In all protected countries with the exception of the United States wages were lower than they were in our country and even in the United States they were being gradually reduced while in free trade England they were being ialsed. The more they considered the scheme the more they would realize what a fraud it was, and that it was simply a means to make the men eaning 20s. per week pay still more than he did at present, eud more than his fair share of the coritry's tsxation. That was not the way to make our Empire the marvel of the world. That could be done only bya just system of taxation, under which every man was equal, and in proposing that, the Liberal was the true Imperialist, and by seeking t 6 put the burden of taxation upon the working man the Conservative was the " Little Englander." (Applause.) Mr. Sherburn moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Lever for his address, and pointed out that in eveiy case where a works had been brought up to date the mann acturer had prospered.</p>
        <p>SIR. M. ROBEIiTS AND THE SUGAR COXVENTION. Mr. H. Roberts seconded and remarked that there was one matter which claimed the attention of every work- ing man,and that was that at the present time we had in the conntry a great number of unemployed. That question was one which in his opinion was quite within the Lange of practical politics. The Corservative Government profeesed to have a strong desire to pre- vent aliens coming into this country and swelling the ranks of the unemployed, and yet at the same time that Government had aided and abetted in procuring the services of 40,0C3 chinamen to compete with white labour—(applause)—at a time when white men were seeking labour and s:sking it honestly in a British colony purctssed with British money and British blood. (Applause.) Rsferring to the sugar question he said the more he considered that question the more he MS satisfied that there wes more than sufficient food for reflection and that every elector who considered it would be compelled to vote for the free trade candidate at the next election. This also affected the question of the unemployed. He did not knew whether they understood the whole of the aspects of the sugar convention. Briefly it was that for many years sugar was produced from the beetroot in Germany end Fiance and the Governments of those two countries, anxious to promote the exportation of sugar, gave bornties to the producer with the result that the English purchaser could get his sugar cheaper than the German or French_ men. By reason of this cheap sugar our confectionery</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>BAND OF HOPE FESTIVAL</head>
        <p>THE BAND OF HOPE FESTIVAL</p>
        <p>A Pretty Scene.</p>
        <p>The following description of the entertainment at this festival on Thursday of last week had to be omitted from our last issue owing to great pressure on space : Although the crowning of the Temperance Queen was as usual the great event of the evening, there were several entirely new departures. Nearly the whole of the children were in character, and an 'effective opening of the entertainment was a procession which presented an exceedingly pretty scene. There were nursery rhyme notables galore, while the dresses of many of the girls were bedecked with flowers of pleasing variety. Great merriment was caused by a I very diminutive soldier who wls followed by a portly John Bull, and there was a host of ludicrous figures, such as clowns, coons, and niggers. A band of Italian girls with bright costumes and ribbons jingled tam- bourines, and the effect as the almost endless chain of characters moved across the platform was kaleidoscopic. When the children had taken their seats on the platform gallery the mass of colour provided a charming spectacle. The retiring Queen (Elsie Jones), attended by guards, beefeaters heralds, and several dignitaries was escorted to the throne, and the chorus "The cause of temperance " was given with fine spirit. After a proclamation by heralds the Queen-elect (Lily Kendrick) appeared, and the retiring queen having vacated the throne the Coronation ceremony was performed with fitting ceremony. The queen was also presented with the garland and sceptre and ascended the throne, the revels then being commenced. A very meritorious item was the "Call to the flowers " for which the youthful performers had been trained by Mrs. Davenport and Miss Wilson. The soloist (May Isherwood) possesses a very sweet voice and in response to her call and a wave of the wand, tiny tots representing familiar blooms ran to her side and were presented to the Queen. The most striking and highly successful features of the evening were the dances. A band of lads and lasses tripped round the maypole and the ribbons were speedily plaited. Great credit is due to their trainers, Mrs. Whitmore, Miss Sankey and Mr. R. P. Williams. A series of national dances for which the boys and girls had been trained by Miss Ethel Spinks took the audience by storm, so to speak. The children, pleasingly costumed, gave dances typical of England, Wales, Ireland, and Scotland in such a ulever and graceful way as to call forth the highest praise. In the English dance the limelights considerably enhanced the effect. Each dance had to be repeated and Miss Spinks must have felt gratified at the success of her pupils. A Grecian drill by girls trained by Miss Spinks was another unique feature and was executed in highly creditable style. The costumes bad been Made by Miss Taylor. Harry Griffiths, a promising singer with a sweet voice of good range, sang 'The Longshoreman" with fine gusto, and Lena Duckworth gave a piquant rendering of "The pig- tail and the fan." Another soloist was Tom Smith who acquitted himself well in the small part allotted to him. Master Tom Barton, Cert. R.A.M., very skilfully and tastefully played a violin solo, while a select choir contributed a feeling interpretation of "Don't forget the old folks."</p>
        <p>Master Tom Barton's juvenile orchestra was in attend- ance and rendered efficient service. The chorus was under the able conductorship of Mr. A. Spruce; Mr. F. Podmore, M.1.U.M., presided at the organ; and Master Clatworthy acted as assistant organist.</p>
        <p>PRIZES PEESBNTED. During an interval book prizes were presented to the following members for their efforts in disposing of tickets :—May Isherwood, Lily Kendrick, Barbara Hol- ryod, Hilda Spruce, Lily Richardson, Arthur Whitfield, Eunice Downes,' Gerald Doncaster, Elsie Gray, Annie Lawless, Thomas Richardson, Elsie Hewitt, Rachael Peacock, Florrie Burton, Edith Davies, Emma Hamblett, and Sarah Forsyth. The thanks of the children to the Chairman and all who had assisted were appropriately expressed by a herald.</p>
        <p>OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>Praise must be accorded to the following officials for their excellent work :—Committee, Messrs. D. T. Dale, G. Whitfield, R. P. Williams, J. Monks. W. Wilkes, Mesdames Whitmore and Davenport; director of elocu- tion, &amp;c., Mr. G. Whitfield ; hon. secretary, Mr. F. Gandy ; hon. assistant secretaries, Messrs. Henry Woolley and It E. Jones; secretary of ladies' auxiliary, Miss E. Gibbs.</p>
