      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>115, SANKEY STREET,</head>
        <p>115, SANKEY STREET,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Volunteer Cyclists at Supper.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Volunteer Cyclist Company held their annual supper and smoking con- •-ert at the Drill Hall on Saturday evening an excellent repast was served by Mrs. Atkinson. The subsequent proceedings were of an enjoyable character, speech- making being pleaeantly intersi :reed with songs by Major Fairclough, a recitation by Lieutenant Picton, and songs by several of the sergeants and privates. , Mr. Bodell and Lance Corporal Glover were particu- larly successful. Captain Hash., us. gave a brief review of the past year's work r- r ^Airing that the whole of the season had b •n most satisfactory, and the men worked so well in camp as to rr 3elve special commendation from the Brigad'er. (Hear, hear). Having reached that standard, he hoped they would live up to it and do their best to become the best Cyclist Company, and it was possible the best com- pany in camp. As they knew, they were, unfortu- nately, about to lose the set vices of Sergeant-Major Rawlinson and he wished to propose his health. (Cheers). It would be impossible for him to say sufficient abuut his good work, and therefore, he would say nothing. (Laughter and cheers). The toast was received with musical honours, Cheers being giyen both for the Sergeant-Major and Mrs Rawlinson In rising to reply the .:..rgeant-Major was received with renewed Cheers. He said he thanked all present most sincerely for the very kind 'manner in which the toastiof his health bad been drunk. He felt his severance from the battalion yeli much in.deed, as when he joined it he intended to remain with it many years. He trusted the Cyclist Company would con tinne to do good work. Other toasts were submitted including the health of the office's and of Colonel- Sergeant Jones and the Chairman, both of which were heartily drunk. Mr. Hazlehurst acting as accom- panist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WARRINGTON MEN GASSED. -- -- Tragic Occurrence in a Sewer.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MEN GASSED. -- -- Tragic Occurrence in a Sewer.</p>
        <p>A CARTER'S HEROISM.</p>
        <p>A Warrington coroner's jury on Wednesday recom- mended for the Royal Humane Society's medal, a carter named John Stockton, 13, Helaby-street, for his heroic conduct in extricating from a manhole in Ellesmere- street, thre 3 men who had been gassed. On the morn- ing of that day a number of Corporation employees were engaged clearing out the sewer. One, George Pugh, was working down number 2 manhole when he was gassed and had to come to the surface. His place was taken by Frank Donaghue who, after work- ing for a few minutes fell suddenly uncon- scious. A fellow workman Richard Bretherton made a gallant attempt to rescue him from his position but foundthe task beyond his strength, Donoghue being some sixteen stone in weight. Other workmen came on the scene and Michml Mitchell went to the assist- ance of Donoghue. He too was overcome by the gas and rendered unconscious. Thomas Baxter Farrel- street, undaunted by the fate which Mitchell had met, entered the man-hole with the object of rescuing his comrades and was in turn overcome by the gas. The other men hesitated to follow and were trying to get , the men out of their dangerous position with hooks when Stockton appeared on the scene. With great bravery he at once entered the man-hole but being "gassed" had to return to the surface. He recovered in a few minutes and displayed unexampled pluck by again going to the assistance of the three men. This time he was successful, and one by one the men were taken out, Stockton re-entering the manhole three times to attach a rope to their bodies. Unfortunately so far as Baxter was concerned his assistance came too late, as he expired a few minutes after being brought to the surface. The other two men were removed to the Infirmary and have since recovered, and there is little doubt that they owe their lives to Stockton's heroism. Such briefly were the facts detailed before Mr. Jones (deputy coroner) at the inquest which he held on Wed- nesday, of what is, happily, a unique accident in the history of the Borough. At the inquest one of the principal- witnesses was Mr. Thomas Longdint borough surveyor, who Produced instructions which he had given to the men who were cleaning the sewers. He also reduced a copy of section of the "Public Health Acts Amendment Act" which prescribed that I "Every person who turns or permits to enter into any sewer of a local authority any chemical refuse or other liquid which, either alone or in combination with the sewage, causes a nuisance or is dangerous or injurious to health, shall be liable to a penalty not exceeding ten 15,9unds,and to a daily penalty not exceeding five pounds." xte had personally served copies of the Act upon all Works in the town, together with a letter calling atten- tion to the frequent complaints received by the Paving and Sewerage Committee of the Corporation of dele- terious matters being turned into the sewers, and asking that strict injunctions should be given to workmen to exercise their utmost care, so as to prevent any pro- ceedings having to be taken under the Act. The only firms which tapped the Ellesmere-street sewer were the Firth Company, Limited, and Locker's. The Coroner: Will the Firth Company turn obnoxi- ous gas into the sewer? Mr. Longdin : I think it will be proved that on this • day they turned acid into the sewer. Continuing, he said he and the Town Clerk had visited the Firth Corn- , rgtny's works and were informed that a quantity of spent hydrochloric acid had been turned into the sewer a short time before the accident. . .</p>
        <p>In answer to Mr. Steel, who appeared for the relations he said Baxter when he entered the manhole was not covered by the regulations to workmen. He considered Baxter's conduct had been very heroic. (The jury "Hear, hear.") . Mr. Ruddock, the public analyst for the borough said he had examined a sample of the gas taken from the sewer shortly after the accident and found it contained a large qvantity of carbonic gas, twenty times the the quantity containel in the ordinary sir. lie had made experiments end found that hydrochloric said, fluxed with matter taken from the sewer, generated sulphuretted, hydrogen and carbonic acid. The Coroner: Then the turning of such acid into the sewer would be likely to be injuriors and dangerous to health. .</p>
        <p>Witness : Csrtainly. Unsworth : Do you consider the turning of spent acid into the sewer would be dangerous. Witness: My experience hos been that acid is lan out in varying strengths. Mr. Unsworth : Do not other firms in town turn acid into the sewers? _ _</p>
        <p>Mr: Etid-do—ck Some of them do. Mr. Unsworth : We are not doing anything other 13ou1e have not done? Mr. Ruddock : No.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : But that will not justify you. Mr. Unsworth : I quite admit that, but it is some- thing in our favour. Dr. Peacock spoke to having been called to the scene.</p>
        <p>IHe applied artificial restoration, unfortunately in Baxter's case without success. The men smelt strongly I I of sulphuretted hydrogen. . Evidence was given by a number of workmen bearing out thp details of the accident as given above. _ .. . . . . .</p>
        <p>Geoi-ge Pugh said the gas canie into the man-hole very suddenly. Samuel Ellison, foreman over the sewer men stated thatall precautions against accident were carried out. He was not present when the accident occurred. Had he been there he would not have allowed Donoghue to enter the man-hole after Pugh had been gassed. The workmen in their evidence were agreed that the Corporation could not do more than they were doing to protect them. Mr. Wilson remarked that Stockton's heroic conduct would be prope,ly acknowledged by the Corporation. The Coroner: He acted very bravely and is deserv- ing of recognition. Mr. Unsworth said he did not think there %dB any evidence against his clients as to their having done wrong. The Coroner: I era going to tell the jury that there is. Someone from the works admitt ed it. It is a , natural inference that acid was turned into the sewer. I</p>
        <p>Mr. Unsworth declined to call any evidence. Addressing the jury the coroner said that although they had no direct evidence they had indirect evidence that the Firth Company had turned acid into the sewer. Mr. Unsworth could have called rebutting evidence and had not done so and the jury may therefore say that acid was turned into the sewer—acid which in combination with the contents of the sewer became injurious rid dangerous to health, and aril&amp; caused Baxters death. With reference to Stockton he felt sure the jury would like to commend his bravery in attempting to save Baxter's life.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict that Baxter's death was caused by suffocation due to the presence of acid in the sewer. The jury assumed that the acid was turned into the sewer by the Firth Company though there was no direct evidence to show that. They were of opinion that no blame attached to the Corporation. They expressed their admiration of Stockton's heroic conduct and asked the coroner to recommend him for the Royal Humane Society's letter. Mr. Wilson, who was present on behalf of the Cor- poration, said that the Corporation heard of the accident with feelings of deep regret, and he publicly acknow- ledged the spirit and courage displayed by the deceased man in his brave attempt to save his fellow workmen. He would like to express his sympathy with the widow and family of Baxter in their sad bereavement. The Corporation would also take into consideiation the bravery shown by Mitchell and Bretherton. Mr. Steel, who appeared on b:thalf of the relatives of the deceased, hoped the Corporation's sympathy would take a tangible form, as Baxter left • a wife and five children unprovided for. Mr. A. J. Willett was present at the inquest on bebqlf of the Accident Insurance Corporation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>L</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 11, 1905.</p>
        <p>(ALL EIGHTS Iticsaaysnj</p>
        <p>anyone to say pow o' -- " Ask Banks. He's aeon it." "Eh ? What has he seen r' eagerly.</p>
        <p>"Banks won't toll,"</p>
        <p>4:6l4—biicirenimow.',' went on the other rather impaticutiy, " a feilo..v slimy can't be- lieve all that rot you hcar about the tiling. Why it's got, about that it's the ghost of a gigantic Kafir with a bloody hand, sod Some say without a head. As if impale coati!l see whether a Kafir's hand was bloody or not in this dark, or even in the moonlight. El_ " I suppose not.'! The rest of the fatviilw, hielarttg It,se, had retired, bnt, lured by the beauty of the night, Welland sat on. Then it struelc hint that Brid- son's laconic answers pointed to a distaste for the subject. However, he would natice one more at- tempt.</p>
        <p>"Well, it's my belief that the thing's easily accounted for." he said. " Probably in certain lights there's a weird or startling shadow which scares the horses. As for the riders—well, funk does the rest."</p>
        <p>" Why don't you go and see for yourself?" said the other, knooking the ashes out of his pipe and rising. . _ _</p>
        <p>"I Lave a couple of times, and it was no good."</p>
        <p>" Well, try again. The third time's lucky, they say." The very words Rose had used, thought young Welland. He answered:</p>
        <p>"By George, I will! I'll go back that way this very night."</p>
        <p>* * r</p>
        <p>The ' moon, nearly at half, shone down upon the broad stripe of the fire-line, bathing it, and the great spread of forest, in the silvern glory of an African summer night. The path, which was steep and rather rough, ran now on one side of the stripe, now on the other, and mostly in the shaded gloom of tall yellow wood trees. A dead silence brooded, emphasised rather than dis- turbed by the occasional " ha-ha-haa " of a hunting jackal far away upon the plain beneath, and this, and the sombre ghostliness of the forest depths on either side began tq affect Welland un- consciously, for all his fine healthy animal strength and sturdy British scepticism. " The third time's lucky " had been the words quoted both by Bone and her father. Would it prove so in this case, and was be destined to see— well, whatever there was to be seen f Some people might think it ituything but lucky, he thought, grimly. If nothing happened he would be disappointed : if something did--His nerves tingled at the thought, as he paced his horse down the shadowy path ; and every now and again the animal would cock its oars, and snuff suspiciously at some sight or sound within the black depths, unseen, unheard by its rider. Ha—there was the critical point. He was drawing near it now. A shadow darkened the path. Glancing upward he observed that a long streamer of filmy cloud was drifting across the moon. And now a great rock jutted out into the path but a short way in front. Welland drew his revolver from the holster and placed it ready to hand in his side pocket ;-3then, shortening the reins in his left hand SO as to have his horse well by the head, he advanced, eyes and ears keenly : alert, as he drew nearer and nearer to the critical</p>
        <p>spot. Immediately beyond the jutting rock the path took a sudden trend inward, right into the black darkness of the overhanging trees—and then— the rider felt his steed suddenly shiver and snort, while he himself was conscious of some awful form of peril hanging over him. Then the ani- mal came to an abrupt standstill—so .abrupt in- deed, that it •would have unseated him but that lie had been in some measure prepared—and stood trembling in every limb. His nerves all Welland strove with voice and touch to soothe the terrified horse, but all without avail— and then—he hardly knew how it happened. Something dropped upon him as be sat in his saddle—dropped• down upon him from above—a Something which he could not see; a vast, awful, crushing weight, bearing Lim with stun- ning shock to the earth. * * * * * • • •</p>
        <p>Bridson, having said good-bye to his departing guest, returned to the stable and cut up another bundle of forage for his own horse. Then, taking a look round upon the beauty of the night, he de- cided it was time to go to bed. But as he ascend- ed the steps of the stoep surprise awaited him. For the front door had opened, and there, -framed within the portal in the moonlight, stood Hose. She was in her nightdress, and her white face and distended eyes bore every indication of terror and distress.</p>
        <p>" Oh, father," she began, before he could speak. " Quick, saddle up and go after Harry." "What on earth's the matter asked Bridson amazed.</p>
        <p>"Ho has gone down the fire-line." " Well, what if he has? • He's been down it before."</p>
        <p>"Oh. quick—go—go ! or you will be too late !" cried the girl pressing her hands to her eyes in an anguished manner. "My dream—my dream !" • I:rideen, like many a man of few Words, wte prompt and decisive in his* actions. He glanced for a moment at his daughter concernedly. Then ke said :</p>
        <p>" I'll go, dear. Get my gun and cartridge. while I Wilt, up, then go back to bed." In less than ten minutes from this occurrence, mounted and armed, he was striking into the fire-line. His gun, a combination one, was load- ed with a bullet in one barrel, and a heavy charge of " loopers " in the other, and he had two or three extra cartridges in his pocket besides. As he sent his horse as swiftly as was safe down the rough path, a conviction grew upon Bridson that his errand was destined to achieve a greater result than the mere humouring of the anxiety of his favourite daughter. He listened intently for any sound of the man he was follow- ing, but if snob there were, it was drowned in the noise made by his own horse's hoofs. The great red rock rose before Lim in the moonlight, and In —his steed began to back and refuse. Bridson, realising that here, if anywhere, was his aid re- quired, slid to the earth. The instant he did so, the horse, jerking the bridle from his hand, turned and trotted away up the fire-line in flue direction of his stable.</p>
        <p>Bridson let him go, then, keen, alert, holding his gun ready, he approached the rock. A amnia of trampling, a squeal, and a rush of hoofs fell upon his ears. And then, as he rounded the corner, a sight met his eyes that might have caused tbp. nerve of a less resolute man to fail him. Beneath the overhanging trees the moonligh fell in a dim silver network. Something dark and indistinct lay upon the ground, apparently strug- gling, and over it, also discernible in the dim light, there towered a Shape--huge, black, gi- gantic—in an attitude that was terrible Hll,l threatening in the extreme. Controlling hi s nerves, Bridson took quick aim and fired. The result was strange. The Thing, whatever it was, elood motionless for.a moment, then seemed to rise upward, and mingling with the overhang- ing trees, it disappeared from his sight. "That you, Harry ?" he said, in a quick, low voice. "Not hurt, are you I)" The recumbent figure picked itself up slowly, then stood forth in the moonlight. It was Wel- land, hut there was a strange, dazed look upon his face in which it WKS evident to the one spec- tator he was striving bard to crush out an ex- pression of downright fear. " Wait a minute," said Brideon, who bad struck a listening attitude—" Ah !" A faint rustling was audible in the depths of the forest. Pointing his gun in the direction of this, Bridson fired his smooth bore barrel, and the heavy charge of " loopera " cut through the undergrowth with a vicious " zip-zip." Then there was silence.</p>
        <p>" That's all we can do to-night," said Bud- son who, the other noticed, had slipped a pair _of fresli cartridges into his gun the moment he had Bred. "Now—what's happened ?"' "God knows. I don't," answered the younger Mattilk an seriousness. "It you hadn't come when you did, in less than another minute 1 should Lave been it dead man. Look.'l BridsOn did look, mita then astonishment indeed was his portion. The face turned towards hut in the moonlight, hal a swollen and livid look, as that of a man suffering from semi-strangula- tion, and on the throat were the distinct ;narks of the lingers and thumb of a gigantic human hand. " Heavens on earth What was it r- he said. The look of fear came back into Welland's face, end be glanced uneasily over his shoulder. " Whatever it was, ghost, men or devil," lie answered, " I was simply a ,child in its grip, and you know Pm pretty powerful at anything in the way of a rougli•and-ttunble myself." " Where's your horse ?" said Bridson. " Gone to blazes, right away, I should think. E hadn't time to pull the pistol on—on whatever it was. I say, 'though, Mr. Bridson—what on earth brought you here F" " My horse for one thing, but he's gone back home again. But it was Rose who started me oti lifter you. 7</p>
        <p>" Rose?" yes." Ilea he briefly expliOned</p>
        <p>WOilt • Ste MIA kaillply Nivea my life, that's all," said the young man feelingly. "So it appears. Anil now we'd better put our best foot toremost, and get back home. She'll get an awful schrek if she happens to see that fool of a horse come back without me." This, luckily, did not happen, for they cap- tured the runaway steed when near home. Rosa had hurriedly dressed, and was waiting for them, bur the two men had ligretA tilitt she rdiould not be told all particulars. It Wildt suffieient that Harry's horse had shied at something, and had thrown him, bruising hint rather. If slit sus- pected there was more underlying this—especial- ly as her father enjoined upon her to preserve strict silence on the isithject—she was a good girl, and a sensible one, and forebore to question them.</p>
        <p>. - "Now, Harry, we must get to the bottom of this affair," said Bridson, as the two of them drew near the scene of the struggle early next morning. " You stand, back here while I ex- amine the spoor." Very different now was the aspect Of things t 3 when last these two stood here but a few hours back.. The newly risen sun was flooding the earth with light—and bright-winged birds, flit- ting from spray to pray, rendered the air glad with melody. Long and carefully did Bridsou scrutinize the ground. " 1 own I'm puzzled," he said, shakinur his head. " There's no sign or trace of a human foot—except your own bootinarks, Harry. But the marks of the scragging -you got are material enough at any rote'. Ak!" An idea had struck bins—a strive, weird, al- most impossible idea. The boughs of the over- hanging trees swept down within five feet -of his bead, and now,, looking up, he noticed that some Of the smaller twigs growing out of them were broken. And' the thing, whatever it was, bad eeemed to disappear nipeord. Thou young Welland hal an inspiration—to wit that they should go and examine the spot in the direction of which Drama' had fired in the dark. They soon gained this, aud then a quick exclamation escaped them, and they looked at each other eagerly. On the ground was 11 great splash of blood. ' " We'll run the mystery to earth at last," cried Welland excitedly. " Ste. There are snore of them !"</p>
        <p>There were, and there was something else be- sides—the print of a footmark to wit—of a naked foot —and that of enormona size -and then an- other and another. Vliutcwer lad been stricken here had retreated, but from the quantity of blood could not go far. " Ghosts don't bleed, anyhow," said Welland, as eagerly, yet ever on the alert, they followed the tracks. Eaeh was armed with a double gun, and it they held in readineas, 'am .on and on through the shaded forest glom the blood tracks led them, farther and farther in. Suddenly, young Welland halted, almost springing back. "Good heavens ! Look at that !" he cried.</p>
        <p>Under a great yellow wood tree, -and -propped up against it, Nat the the form of a truly gigantic Kafir. A frightful expression of hate mid agony was stamped upon the 13onntensmee ; reflecting in the glazed di.tesiding eyeballs and hared teeth ; and the naked body was covered with blood. "Hs stone dead. Harry. Don't fire," said Bridson. It WM indeed so. His buckshot had found its billet in the course of that random shot. " What on e irth —why, what would be the fel- tow's reason for attacking people The war has been over for sonic time,' said Harry, gazing with a sort of horrible rumination at the fearful _</p>
        <p>" My tistion iM lis wss it who'd suNiked awsy hill) to livn by himself," said Bridson. •• Probably he was otOy daugerows at certain times."</p>
        <p>" 1.11 answer for it lie was last night," said the younger anau. feeling his own throat signifi- cantly. " See why it it he never left any spoor, I Harry Why, he juinnvl up a tree, and cliinhed along the tree lops until ha pulged it site to come down. And now —we'll just Nast) iliti here, end say nothing chola it. I think we both know how to keep our oiVII enittivel, eli I.' We don't want any enquiries I,ld. If he's ever found, they'll only think In wits in of those killed in the war—there are severil of them king Hbout the forest even yet.. Ditt —" he addril, with a spice of grim humour, as thee hulled away, " I. think somehow, the ghost of Brid-ain's has taken its last walk. Wit never walk ngain." And it never did : lint it was not until some years later that Harry Welland told his wife (nee Rose Bride* the reason why.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>GHOST OF BRIXION'S FIRE-LINE</head>
        <p>THE GHOST OF BRIXION'S FIRE-LINE</p>
        <p>BERTR A. 31 MITFORD (Author of " The. Gun-Runnert" sc.)</p>
        <p>The situation ot Bridson's homestead was. beautiful in the extreme. „ Right up ou *thigh ridge Of the Kranturtinge it stood, sunk .in• eyrie-like hollow, the front of which looked down on .a bold droop of mountain side, in whose green kloofs the grass grew abundant and sweet, causing Bridson's cattle at Stook Fair tip° to be eagerly looked out for and bought up. Here a , rolling panorama of ironbound cliff and deep reesses, black with forest, deligked the eye ; hut it was On the other side of the ridge that the eittlook was truly mag- nificent, for a gentle aso•nt .of about a hunched yards behind the homestend brought you to ,ut point where, standing for the first time, a 30 rt of Involuntary gasp cseaped you. For it seemed is though the world lay out- spread at your feet—and sueli a world. Plains, undulating and wooded, Oahe.; level and bare, here and there the gleam el a river glimpsed in the deep bend of its valley bottom, and some far Dff rend, tho loom of Um dark bush-line seeming almost fiat by reason of distance, ',occanionaly- a white speck where the sun lit upon the white- washed walls of n farmhouse—the blue smoke of n grass tire or two mingling with the haze of misty distance. Leading dawn to all this, the slope of the ridge fell in -bold sweep, but all black and green and gold with the vast unbroken for- . ?lit which covert .d the monntsin. side. Here you might wander for miles and miles in the cool gloom of gigantic y ellow wood frees, every sound hushed and mysterious in the t wilight silence, the flight of a crimson-winged louri •or two dart- ing a flash of colour through the trailing monktby ropes.' Miles, did we say F Why, for days you might wander in those forest. recesses, perchance if you .were newly arrived and inexperienced, never to emerge, ea intle«l had happened to, ttt. any rate, one unlucky wight, some time before. This poor man, a Journeyman stonemason by trade, and unumed to the ways of the country, had become lost in this particular forest tract, and it was not until Le bad been missiog for over a year that his bones were found, quite by accident, not very far from Bridson's homestead. This had taken place right on the edge of a stripe or belt, %In which the trees had been cut away, and which intersected the forest-clad slope from base to summit in R broad, wavy band. This was in fact a " lire-line" the raison d'etre of which was to arrest the further spread of any accidental conflagration. There were three or four others in the forest at intervals, the handiwork of Banks, the, Forest Offleer, au importation from India, where he had served for a good many years in the Caine capacity. For the forest on that side of the range was not (iridium's pro- perty, but Crown laud.</p>
        <p>But popular report could not leave the poor stonemason to rest in peace. It was said that he " walked "—that off- certain 'tights his ghost might be met hovering round the spot where his remains bad been found." Bridson's Fire-line," as it was called, came to have a bad name. It became a locality to avoid, which was inconveni- ent, for it constituted a short cut to Bridson's farm, for all but wheeled traffic : and it was sig- Meant of the force with which its minister repu- tmtion fastened on the public mind, that people preferred to take the triggon road on the other mide of the ridge, which added miles to their way, rather than go up or down it at time any after dark.</p>
        <p>Alter a time the stonemason's ghost gave way to an apparition far more frightful and appalling, though vague. Banks, the Forest Oflleer, jeered end scoffed at the whole thing, even when one of the neighbours who bad been visiting Brideon, came to signal grief in the fire-line while return- ing home at night. He had not returned home, but his horse had, and that in a pitiable state of sweat and terror, and on search being made, the rider was discovered with a badly fractured hip, and other injuries. Something had come right at him out of the gloom of the forest Areas, he declared, something black and gigantic—but—a Something that was not mortal—to that he could swear to the end of his life—and his horse be- coming unmanageable, and he hints-elf entirely unnerved, he had been thrown, and what with the shock of the event,. and the pain of his in- juries, bad become unconscious. "Old Begg had been having a glass or two of 'clop' more than he ought," had been Banks' jeering com- mentary on that effair, and also on two or three others akin to it, though not so serious in their results. And then there came a time when Banks did not jeer any more : on the contrary, ha avoided the topic with a carefulness which in one hitherto less sceptical might have looked uncom- monly like something else. It must not be supposed, however, that this sort of thing was allowed to go uninvestigated. After certain of these occurrences Bridson and his neighbours had made a most thorough and ex- haustive search on and :ironed the spot—always the saine—whereou they were supposed to have taken place. This had comprised a large section of the adjacent forest, and hearing in mind that these were men born slid brzsl in the veldt, and whose, practised eyes not the most minute indica- tion of spoor could have escaped, the utter futil- ity of their investigational del but add to the mystery said dread which, enshrouded the whole business. More than once, too, a party, well , armed, had concealed thenetelvee within view of the spot, towards eundown, and watched the , whole night through, but whether it were that there was safety in umebere no manifestation of any sort occurred to disturb—or reward—their vigil. a « a</p>
        <p>* * •</p>
        <p>Rose Bridson and her nowt; were standing on the high ridge overlooking the vast panorama we have described, and right at the point where the fire-line began. Rose was a pretty girl of twenty, with wavy golden hair and large blue eyes, in short a sweet and wholesome Anglo-Saxon type. Harry Welland was An English importation— rather a recent one at that—and was gaining colonial experience on an adjoining stook run ten or a dozen miles away. He was a fine young fellow, well looking and, athletic withal, and keen on every form of outdoor sport: and having said this much it is scarcely necessary to add that for the reputed spectre .of Bridson's fire-line he pro- fessed the profoundest contempt.</p>
        <p>" How is it the thing never shone up when I'm on band, eh, Rose " he was saying, by no means for the first time. " Why, because ' the joker, whoever it is, knows that I don't 'take any banky-panky. I'd choke the life out of him AS a beginning, and if that didn't do, I'd shoot. Rather !"</p>
        <p>"Yes, dear, I know you would," said the glancing at him admiringly, and thinking he looked as if he could do it too. " But—" " But what r' " Nothing. I • was only going to say that I wish You'd leave the whole thing alone."</p>
        <p>" There's nothing to leave alone. It's all skit- tles. I've been allong the place often enough during the day time, and twice at night, and I've seen nothing." " The third time's lucky,' " quoted Rose, and immediately wished she hadn't, as be re- joined : " Well, I'll chance the third time. I'll try it to- night. Where is it the thing is said to show up ?" " Where the great red rock stands out on to the-line. We pointed it out the other day, you remember, when we were riding up. But, dear- est, don't go out of your war to Meddle with the thing. Don't, just to please me." "Why, Rosie, thmight you Colonial people were a bard-headed, practical crowd ; and here you are, as superstitivs as a lot of old women." " Now, Harry, don't you abuse colonials. I won't have it."</p>
        <p>" Abtitge them ? Not much. Traven't I given prompt and practical proof of the very biggest Rind of appreciation of them ?" he answered, with it siAmitietuit look into her bluo eyes, which more than mado his peace. The sun laid sot. and the briefest of African twilight was falling upon the forest-clad slope, whose now coin r. face the light band of the distance. Almost as soon as the pair regained the homestead supper was ready. Bridson, a tall, dly stamp of man, was more of a listener than a talker, but his family more than made up for paternal deficiency, in that they all chattered at once. Three boys in the hobbledehoy and school stage—as mauy girls ditto, besides Rose, who was the eldest—there was no taciturnity around that festive board.</p>
        <p>_ "I say, Mr. Bridsou," said young Welland, as be and his prospective father-in-law were smoking a final pipe together, " do you really be- lieve in this fire-line affair ? I never can get</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Junior Conservative Association.</head>
        <p>Junior Conservative Association.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the members of the War- rington Junior Conservative Association was held at the Club, Orford-lane, on Monday evening, Mr. Roger C. Parr, presiding—On the motion of Mr. E. Draper, seconded by Mr. J. W. Kay, Mr. R. C. Parr was un- animously re-elected president of the Association. Other officers elected were, Committee :—Messrs W. LiWilliams, S. Thorley, H. Hayes, J. S. Bate, J Seed, J. Strettle, W. C. Brooks, T. Richardson, W. Spence, and H. B, Houghton; Club Committee :—Messrs. W. L. Williams, J. Stretde, J. Seed, S, Thorley, W. C. Brooks and W. Gibson. The following resolution was moved by the Councillor Fulton Smith and carried. That the members assembled at the annual meeting of the Warrington Junior Conservative Asso- ciation, desire to express their complete confidence in his Majesty's Government and the policy they are pursuing.'</p>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>----- i AK ordinary watch contains about 150 pieces, but complicated repeaters, chronographs, Sm., as many as 800, and in one case 975 pieces.</p>
        <p>Expitnnotarrs shew that Röntgen rays and radium both arrest the growth of seedling plants. The rays first accelerate the germination of dry bean and turnip seeds, then check it.</p>
        <p>M. MirsocaAn has been able, thanks to the rare and pure atmosphere on the summit of Mont Blanc, to observe the darkening of the bright rays in the solar spectrum caused by absorption of the rays in the solar atmosphere.</p>
        <p>•Ax aluminium respirator has been invented for breathing in mercurial vapours. The air passes through powdered aluminium, which absorbs the mercury before it goes to the lungs. The device is used in the mercury mines of Monte Amiata.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PERFEGT BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE PERFEGT BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>AND OTHER NEWS FOR LADY READERS.</p>
        <p>THE Blouse has come to stay. Other fashions come and go: but the blouse keeps on. Attractive, variable for all figures, suitable for home and party wear alike, it cannot be beaten. Dressmakers find no fault with the blouse fashion. Any complaint they make is against dressmaking itself. "I bad to give up the work," said one skilful needlewoman. "The doctor said I was not strong enough to work at all. It is a wonder I am alive now.</p>
        <p>"I was apprenticed to the dressmaking business," continued the speaker—s Miss Alice Dunn, of 82, Meadow-road, Fenti- man-road, SouthLam- beth, London—" but soon had to give it up because of bilious- ness and anemia, which had taken a firm hold of me. To go upstairs made me completely exhaus- ted. I was seen by three different doc- tors, and was at home for six months, during which time I became</p>
        <p>DESCRIPTI, Our illustration sla Wit a charming blouse of white silk, trimmed with folds of lace insertion. high uecla d bond of tucked silk and stitched upon band of silk down the front, edged with face frills. The sleeves are tucked from shoulder to elbow,- with the fulness gathered into sacs cuffs.</p>
        <p>so feeble that my ; mother thought the end was not far off. The pains in my chest were terrible, and when I coughed I used to ' spit blood. I could not sleep, and my appetite was wretched. Mother now says it was like death-watching, , for she never expected I would recover. "It was suggested that I should try Dr. Williams pink pills. My friends sent for a box, and very soon after, for the first time for years I began to feel an ' appetite for food. By the time I had finished the first , box I felt sure the pills were curing me. I persevered, steadily taking the pills, and before long everyone could see that I was absolutely cured. These pill are invalu- able, and if I have a suspizion of a headache I take a , pill, and thus keep quite well." Miss Dunn who undoubtedly looks a picture of health added that her sister, who is seventeen years of age had also been cured of anmia by Dr. Williams' pink te pills for pale people. "Publish this testimonial for , the good it may do," said Miss Dunn in conclusion. , Loss of appetite is a sign that food is not digesting. I Weakness and bile naturally follow. New blood is the cure. Mothers know why they and their daughters need nem blood more than men; but men can beantemic, I too. The weak back, easy fatigue, lack of interest in I life, indigestion, and general nervous weakness whic,h ' trouble both men and women, need new blood to cure ; them, and these pills make new blood. Nerves that need new blood to feed them show their need in neuralgia, fits, St. Vitus' dance, eventually paralysis, ! locomotor ataxy, and utter breakdown. But Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people can cure all these things—the genuine pills. It is important to avoid substitutes, and purchasers should take care to see the full name, as above, on the wrapper, before paying.</p>
