      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>RADIUM EXPERIMENTS.</head>
        <p>RADIUM EXPERIMENTS.</p>
        <p>Dr. Jules Rehns finds by experiment on himself that radium bromide, while discolouring and peeling off the skin, does not injure the eye. Radium should not, he thinks, be applied to persons indiscriminately by physicians, as it affects different skins differently. He did not find Any anaesthetic action of radium on the nerves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WIRES AND WEATHER.</head>
        <p>WIRES AND WEATHER.</p>
        <p>An observer draws attention to the humming of telegraph and telephone wires as a sign of the weather. It appears that the humming of wires running east and west presages a fall of temperature often ten or more hours before it is marked by the thermometer; and, on the other hand, the humming of north and south wires foretells a rise of temperature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>PRODUCTION OF ALCOHOL.</head>
        <p>PRODUCTION OF ALCOHOL.</p>
        <p>In Germany 80 per cent., and in Austria 60 per cent, of the alcohol made is from the potato. Russia makes 50 per cent. and France 20 per cent. from this root. Treacle and beetroot yield 75 per cent. of French alcohol, and the remaining 5 per cent, is from wines and fruits. In the United Kingdom, Spain, and Italy, alcohol comes chiefly from grain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>PAGOSCOPE.</head>
        <p>THE PAGOSCOPE.</p>
        <p>The "pagoscope" is a white frost alarm for horticulturists, made by Bernel Bourette, 36, Rue de Poitou, Paris. It consists of a wet and a dry bulb thermometer on a frame, with a needle shew- ing the reading of the wet bulb, and indicating by its attitude whether there will be frost or not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HOW THE MOON SOLIDIFIED.</head>
        <p>HOW THE MOON SOLIDIFIED.</p>
        <p>Has the moon solidified from the centre to the circumference, or the reverse? Lunar photographe have led M. Loewy, director of the Observatory of Paris, and M. Puiseux to think that solidification has taken place from the circumference to the centre. The original solid crust, according to their views, has been broken up and displaced by still molten nucleus. Lord Kelvin and others hold by the contrary hypothesis, at least as regards the earth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>EXPERIMENTS WITH CALCIUM.</head>
        <p>EXPERIMENTS WITH CALCIUM.</p>
        <p>MM. Moissan and Chavanne hare made experi- ments with the calcium prepared in notable quantities at the Bitterfeld works. The electrical conductivity of silver being taken at 100, that of calcium is only 15-6 at a temperature of 20deg. Centigrade. Its fusing point is 810 deg. C. Its density is 1-548. Calcium makes a crystalline amalgam with mercury, stable in dry air at ordinary temperatures, absorbing neither ' oxygen nor nitrogen. It slowly decomposes water, and is an energetic reducer, acting in a regular manner. It will be useful in organic chemistiy for reductions in a liquid neutral or feebly alkaline.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>MOVING WALL OF SAND.</head>
        <p>A MOVING WALL OF SAND.</p>
        <p>The Alamogordo or Tularosa desert, east of tho Organ Mountains, New Mexico, is a sandy plain of the Jura-Trias and early cretaceous strata of America, including the "red beds" of marl, clay, salts, and gypsum. It is traversed by lava beds, now in ruins, and eroded valleys. It is dotted with sand dunes and marshy "sinks." The flora is like that of other south-western deserts in the United States, but modified somewhat by local conditions. A white wall of gypsum sand is mov- ing eastward at one part and overwhelming all vegetation, which cannot, like the yucca radiosa, the mesquite, and sumac struggle against its advance. The green tufts of the yucca can be seen rising over white drifts 201 t. high.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>PLANTS THAT CAUSE DISEASE.</head>
        <p>PLANTS THAT CAUSE DISEASE.</p>
        <p>Plants, as well as animals, cause diseases among men. M. Dubreuilh, in the Journal de Medicine, Bordeaux, reports cases of dermatitis due to prim- roses. Retzdorff has also signalled the case of a lady who, in the second half of October every year, had an eruption of eczema on the face and hands, which only disappeared with springtime, and was traced to China primroses (Primula obconzca), which she received as a present on her fête day (October 15th), and tended with her own hands until they were planted out in spring, when the malady left her. Another lady suffered from an eruption on the face and forearms from the China primrose. A third had a similar complaint from wearing a bouquet of this flower on her bosom. Primrose flowers are sometimes chewed and swallon ed, but the habit may produce an eruption of the mouth. It appears that the chrysanthemum has a like property. A lady spent six hours in cutting these flowers, and next day had fever, with an eruption on the face and arms. A café waiter bruised a leaf of Thuya cfrecidentalis between his fingers, and soon afterwards an eruption difficult to cure broke out. Itlo doubt, comments a writer in the Globe, these are special cases, peculiar to individuals, but the facts should be known. Such an eruption should be washed with alcohol, which dissolves the iritating matter, then with water and soap.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SPOTS ON Tlit SUN.</head>
        <p>SPOTS ON Tlit SUN.</p>
        <p>If the sun's disc be scanned from time to time, says the Scientific American, it will be found that sometimes there are spots and sometimes there are not. According to our present knowledge, these spots are projected by the descent of comparatively cool matter from the higher regions of the solar atmosphere, so that the more spots there are the greater will be the quantity of matter descending. Since this falling material is the result of previous up-rushes of highly-heated matter from the lower levels of the sun's atmosphere, it stands to reason that this spot phenomenon indicates great solar atmospheric disturbance, and, therefore, greater activity, and consequently more intense heating capacity. Thus, We arrive at the conclusion that the greater the number of the sun-spots the greater the solar activity, and, therefore, the hotter must be the,sun. Examined through a telescope of con- siderable magnifying power, a spot appears like an immense ragged hole in the crust of the solar surface. A spot is a cavity, and not an elevation. As the spot turns from us, the phenomenon is similar to . that of looking across the edge of a great shallow saucer, only that the outline is irregular, and that where the bottom should be there is nothing but the blackness of what seems an immeasurably deep chasm. These vast cavities are not solid things, and not properly to be com- pared even with masses of slag swimming on molten metal. They are rents in that bright cloudy curface of the sun which we call the photosphere, and through which we look down to lower regions. Their shape may be very rudely likened to a funnel with sides at first slowly sloping (revumbra), and then suddenly going down into the central darkness (umbra). This central dark- ness has its gradations of shade, for cloudy forma- tions may be seen very obscurely glowing far down its depths; solid bottom we never see. Near the edge of the sun's ditc the epots appear to rise up through the obscuring atmosphere and gather here and there in groups of hundreds to form -thite, cloud-like pataies (ficulce), which may soloetimes be seen even with a spyglass. Lookirkg straigh: into a spot through a very large telescope, this penumbra is seen to be made up a long white thanents twisted into curious, mpe-like forma- tions, while the central part is like a great flame ending in fiery spires. Over tfrzse hang what look lik- clouds, such as we somet*.ams see in our sky, I Ut more transparent than the finest lace veil, and b. v fig, not the fleecy loot- of our clouds, but the pearance of being ftl...si with almost infinitely dt feate threads of light. With all this there is something crystalline about the appearance of a soct, not Unlike frost figures on a window pane. Indeed, the intense whiteness of everything is suggestive of something, very cold.</p>
      </div>
