        <p>ItjNIVERSITY bCiiOOL, tiOUPtiPOKT. A MOST SUCCEMFUL BOARDING SCHOOL. FOR 801/N.</p>
        <p>W. HRNDRRSON. M.A., Principal, QAXONHOLMR, SOUTH PROMENADR, ST. ANNIIS.— kJ BOARDING SCHOOL FOR GIRLS. Illustrated Pro- oectus on application, —Principals, the Misses A.OHBURNER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>THE tactics of THE GOVERNMENT AND thepovernment MR. CHURCHILL'S MOTION. on Wednesday</p>
        <p>night were not heroic, but in desperate straits dignity is apt to be lost sight of. By deciding to move the previ- ous :question, Mr. BALFOUR and his colleagues played the old game of shirking the issue with rather less than the old skill. They might just as well have tagen the advice of one of their sup- porters in the Press and pretended that their vital point had nothing whatever to do with fiscal argument. The object of the Opposi- tion is to turn the Government out. What could be more "vital," therefore, than the "cardinal interests represented by " the Union and the Empire "F Unionists were entreated to vote without the slightest reference to the terms Of MT. CHURCHILL'S resolution. They were to bear in mind everything except the matter in debate. Why bother with preferential trading and protective taxation, in fact? "Give the country time to think, and when a General Election comes let the arguments for and against a departure from the principles of PEEL and COBDEN be submitted to its verdict, according to the matured convictions of the politicians who apply for its mandate to defend or overturn the existing system." Mean- while Parliament ought not to occupy itself. with the question at all. The country is to think, but the House of Commons is not to share in the thinking. This is the sort of thing which passes for argument with writers who wish to keep Mr. BALFOUR in office at any hazard. He has the glory of having invented the proposition that a question which has stirred a country for nearly two years may be discussed in newspapers and on platforms, and may decide the issue of bye-elections but ought not to be mentioned in the Legislature In the terms of the Ministerial amendment on Wednesday night, such a question ought not to be put in the Assembly which it most concerns</p>
        <p>If such a doctrine were accepted, it would reduce Parliament to a sham. A Government acting en Mr. BALFOUR'S principle might have no policy at all in any controversy that happened to be embarrassing. The Prime Minister might a " Ob, no, I cannot discuss that. The country must be left to think about it, and when a General Election comes I will let you hear what I think, but not till then." This is a flat denial of the functions of the House of Commons as a delibera- tive and consultative body. Mr. BALFouit virtually asserts the unheard of prerogative of declining to discuss any inconvenient subject on the plea that it does not concern the existing Parliament. The Unionists are told that they must not "destroy the only instrument by which the policy of Unionism and Imperialism can be vindicated and pursued." To vote for Mr. CHURCHILL'S resolution against pro- tective taxation on food, or to abstain from voting, said a Unionist journal, was to take the means to bring about "the triumph of the Home Ruler and the Pro-Boer." If Mr. Cauncnim.'s resolution, remarked the same article, had been " divested of party significance," everybody could have voted for it; for who wants to put protective taxation on food? Unless preferences means higher prices said Mr. CHURCHILL on Wednesday night, it is a sham, and we know quite well that it is intended to secure higher prices. But this is a point which has no interest for the Government. All they wish is to be regarded by Unionists as the champions who stand between the country and the awful things which must happen if the Liberals take office. This view of their responsibilities and capacities is not taken by those Unionists who see in Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S policy and Mr. BALFOUR'S connivance the. one real danger that threatens the Empire. As for the country, it is thinking with- out Mr. Benroun's help. Its sole desire is to have a change of Government.</p>
        <p>Quite a remarkable gathering was witnessed at the annual meeting of the Warrington Liberal Association on Wednesday. The large room at the Club was crowded almost to suffocation, and such unanimity and enthusiasm were displayed as augurs well for the prospects of the party at the election. As a rule a business meeting such as thanks does not appeal to the rank and file of an association, but on Wednesday the members of the Liberal Association proved themselves to be stirred by the spirit of the times. They are eager for the fray, and as scmebody remarked; a vote of this was really due to Mr. Chamberlain for the unity he had brought about in the ranks of the Liberal party. There is no possible doubt of the fact that he Liberals of Warrington are approach- ing the impending conflict in a far different spirit thai, on the last occasion in 1900. Then, the khaki election found many Liberals disinclined to give a vote which might relieve the Government of the responsibility of putting things straight, and there were many dissensions from the Progressive side. To-day the position is entirely different. The Government has not a ve.tige of credit left, and we believe that not only the Liberals, but the people of Warrington generally, are eager for an opportunity of reversing both local and national representation. Under such circumstances as these, offering the opport•inities of a generation, it is exceedingly gratifying to find local Liberalism so virile and aggressive in its new-found unity.</p>
        <p>Warrington Free Churchmen have cause to hold in pleasant remembrance the great gatherings of the National Free Church Council in Manchester this week, if only for the reason that they afforded an opportunity of hearing such conspicuous figures in the religious world as the miniaters who addressed the mass meeting in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening. The meeting was full of inspiration and encouragement and rarely have more stirring and eloquent appeals for a robust Church life and activity in all that makes for the common weal been heard by a Warrington audience. The commanding personage of the evening, all will readily concede, was the Roy. F. B. Meyer whose zeal for the uplifting :of the masses and purifying of public life compel the respect of great numbers who are not fortunate enough to come within his immediate influence. Every member of the audience must have been impressed by the speaker's intense earnestness not less than by his broadmindedness, while the address was illumined with bright flashes of humour. The whole of the speeches—and in fact the meeting itself—were evidence of the increasing unity among all twanches of Nonconformity. Lines of demarcation there must inevitably be, but, was strongly em- phasised, they need not preclude united action against the common foes of Christianity and the people.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Corporation has frequently proved itself to be thick-skinned. Neither a House of Commons Committee nor the Local Government Board can apparently reduce it to a state of fitting humility. There is a fine large spirit of independence abon. t a popularly-electad, unpaid local authority, which sometimes carries it beyond the bounds of prudence and decency. Little importance seems to have been attached officially to the smart rebuff from the Local Government Boarrl with regard to Warrington's cool way of obtaining sanction for the borrowing of money after t has actually been spent. At least, His Worship the Mayor does not appear to be much impressed with the enormity of the offence he has, presurnably, condoned. To his mind, the very proper rebuke from Whitehall is so much " red tape," and nothing more. The remark wa very injudicious and net befitting the dignity of the Mayor of the Borough. It ought to be obvious that the Government department which holds the purse strings is entitled to very respectful con sideratioo. The Education Committee has tried the Board of Education to the limit of endurance on the accommodation question and the grants were seriously endangered at one time. The Cor- poration ought to know, without inviting the lesson cf bitter experience, that the Local Govern- ment Board will not allow itself to be openly flouted, and it is due to the ratepayers that the unconstitutional and foolish policy of the past should:be abandoned. -A Something like lan hour and a half's heated discussion was required on Tuesday to settle the appoinment of a road-foreman and even then—to use a "bull "—the question was not settled. The- whole difficulty arose through the suggestion,freely voiced, that there was political favouritism afoot. It was almost touching to witness the distress of the Conservative members when their cruelly candid oppor_ents gave the committee's recom- mendation, in favour of the appointment of Mr. W. L. Williams, a political colour. The notion was new and distasteful, to them, of course. But quite apart from any political intrigue, which may or may not be justified, the Council did quite right to refer the matter back for further consideration. The qualifications of the two candidates who are favourites for the post were fully discussed and compared. In our view there was only one course open to an honourable body of men, and that course they took. Mr. Williams' experience was " theoretical," while that of Mr. Wareing was practical. One man is a qualified and expert road-forernaii and the other is not, though he may possess the requisite know- ledge. There is no doubt, therefofe, that it is the duty of the Committee to reconsider the matter. Unless other conditions are equal, no local prefer- ence should be shown. What the ratepayers expect is the appointment of the man whose training and experience beet fit him for the Co'</p>
        <p>- iuncjllor Pemberton's public motto is, should imagine, "Persistence," for he has a wood ml tenacity of purpose when once his interest E energy are aroused. Readers of the weekly pi cannot fail to have observed this themselves, they must also be well ewer. that, if there is thing more than another which he has otriven</p>
        <p>we ler- id</p>
        <p>mess</p>
        <p>and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
