      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <p>with might and main it is the right to inspect all documents of the Corporation. The fact that he has once more failed will, we think,entitle him to some Sympathy. Further than this we cannot go with Mr. Pemberton. He made out a. very good case from his point of view on Tuesday, but the Council never seriously entertained his proposal to alter Standing Order .37, for the simple reason that there were objec- tions of a most serious nature to what he proposed. The resolution in Councillor Pemberton's name would give him all the scope he desired, but it would also be liable to abuses which might have the gravest consequences. There are, unquestion- ably, papers in the possession of a Corporation which it would not be advisable to place in the hands of any Councillor, much less leave them Open to the inspection of all the ratepayers' repre- sentatives. The safeguard of secrecy is as necessary in regard to the contracts and negotia- tions of a Corporation as it is in the business of a private company. Councillor Zemberton must know this, and he can hardly expect that any variation of the standing orders which might result in the betrayal of trade and professional secrets is likely to ba entertained. We do not think he has beezi fairly treated in many of his endeavours to procure information, but this does not colter the fact that his motion on Tuesday was quite inadmis- Bible on the grounds of public policy.</p>
        <p>It will be a source of gratification to very many people—a large majority in fact—to learn that the Workhouse extension scheme has been defeer.4ii for twelve months. The outburst of public feeling against the expenditure has been such as to con- vince many members of the Board that the time is inopportune for putting any additional burden on the rates which can possibly be avoided, and the decision on Wednesday by fifteen votes to eight is Indicative of a considerable modification of opinion. The extension may;in many respects, 1.3 e urgently required and yet policy dictate that it is inexpedient. There is no doubt that the Workhouse can jog along in its present form with- out any danger of overcrowding. Tue effort to attain administrative perfection can well be post- poned to more prosperous times, and we are glad that the economists have triumphed. Credit is due to Mr. Wm. Hickman and the Property Owners' Association for having engineered the opposition to the scheme. A tight hand on the expenditure of public monies is necessary if municipal rating is to be reduced to a normal level, and the finances of the country brought to a healthier condition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Serious Accident.</head>
        <p>Serious Accident.</p>
        <p>A serious accident occurred at Culcheth Station yesterday. James Yates, West Leigh, had entered a railway carriage and was closing the window in the door furthest from the platform when the door opened and he fell on to the lines He sustained a fractured skull and was conveyed to the Warrington Infirmary. Was Dan Leno Ever at Warrington ? A correspondent writes to ask us if the late Dan Leno ever appeared at the Old Circus, Sankey-street, (the Empire) about 14 or 15 years ago, or when it was called the Gaiety, some 20 years ago. If any of our readers can furnish any information on this point we shall be obliged by their communicating with us.</p>
        <p>"PREMIER" CVCLES at 12 guineas with change Speed gear &amp;c., unequalled.—J. S. GARLA.ND, 51 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Mr. T. J. Down's Orchestra An enlarged photograph of the above band was on Wednesday last presented to Mrs. Down by the members as a memorial of their late esteemed Conductor. Mrs. Down has always taken a deep interest in the welfare of the band. The photograph was executed by Mr. Birtles, Sankey-street, in whose window it may be viewed during the next week.</p>
        <p>Warrington Ragged School and Mission. The Executive Committee beg to thank Mr. Frank Stringer for a gift of 130 lbs. of bread. This was dis- tributed on Thursday night to the deserving poor by Mr. Skelland. The Mission has been doing this work of feeding the hungry through the winter, especially the children, and make a strong appeal for help to enable them to still continue to relieve the distress which still exists, especially in the Bewsey district.</p>
        <p>ASK for CIINNDIGHA.M'S HIGH-CLISa MINERAL</p>
        <p>WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Nursing Association. The bazaar in aid of the District Nursing Associa- tion, organized by the Misses Whitney and Baskerville, recently held in Cairo-street schoolroom, has proved a greater success than was at first anticipated, and reflects the highest credit on all concerned. The sum handed over to the funds of the Asseciation is £22 16s. besides which, the promoters, wishing to give a tangible memento to the Home, have presented a handsome screen and lounge chair for the Nurses' sitting-room, and a beautiful American rocking chair for the Superintendent's sitting-room.</p>
        <p>AST{ for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Welsh Congregational Gathering. The annul congregational gathering in connection with the Welsh Church, Crosfielcl street was held on Thursday, when an excellent tea was followed by an enjoyable concert. At the tea some 100 persons were present and for the subsequent proceedings that number had been considerably augmented. Mr. H. Roberts presided. The contributors to an unusually entertaining programme were, without exception, highly successful in their efforts. They were Miss L. Williams who sang sweetly and displayed considerable histrionic talent in a recitation in Welsh, Mr. J. Morris the possessor of a rich baritone voice, Mrs. W. T. Williams, Mr. H. 0. Hughes, Mr. H. M. Ashton, Master, T. Barton and Mr. E. Wilkes, who in addition to contributing a pianoforte solo, acted as accom- panist. The proceedings closed, with the singing of the Welsh National hymn "Land of oar Fathers. "PREMIER" CYCLES at 7 guineas startling value.— J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.— Advt.</p>
        <p>Apollo Male Voice Choir's Success. The Warrington Apollo Male Voice Choir succeeded in carrying off the first prize (M 5 and a baton) at the Earlestown Eisteddfod on Saturday in the competition for male voice choirs. The test piece was "On the Ramparts " and the the three competing cb.oirs were placed .—Apollo Choir, Ist ; Warrington Male Voice Choir, 2nd ; and Makerfield Male Voice Choir, 3rd. Speaking of the winning choir, the Adjudicator said they possessed rich voices, and the purity of tone was sustained throughout. Their attack and release was very good, and their humming was exceptionally good. Theirs was throughout a very fine performance. The 'Warrington Male Voice Choir made a good opening, and had a good body of tone. Mr. Berry conducted the winning choir, and Mr. Nesbit the Warrington Male Voice Choir. At the same Eisteddfod the silver- street juvenile choir, trained Ind conducted by Mr. B. Wadsworth, carried off the prize in the children's choir competition. "PREMIER." CYCLES at 9 guineas for Best Grade, unsurpassed.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Orford Tannery Gutted. A disastrous fire occurred at Orford Tannery (Messrs. Mortimer and Co.) on Thursday morning. A. seven storey drying shed, the erection of which was ' only completed a month ago, being completely guttel . The outbreak was first noticed by workmen two miles off shortly after midnight, but the premises must have been burning for some time. The Warrington brigade were quickly on the scene and found the whole of the seven-storey building ablaze from end to end. It was at once seen that its destruction was inevitable, and the firemen directed their efforts to saving the boiler-house and adjoining premises, being ably assisted by the works fire brignile. After an hour the whole of the building was burnt out, only the bare walls and fireproof floor remaining. Fortunately the building stood apart and no further damage was done. It was used for drying sole leather and contained a large stock, machinery and electrical plant, the whole of which was destroyed. The damage, which is covered by insurance, has not yet been computed but it is roughly estimated at about .£lO,OOO. The business of the firm will not be interfered with in consequence of the fire.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLocx for Good BOOM—Advt. Wedding at Whitley. The marriage took place at Whitley Church on Tuesday afternoon of Mr. Mark Mounfield, of Pill Moss, Whitley, and Miss Nellie Frith, daughter of Mrs. Frith, of Brook Farm, Dutton. I3oth the bride and bridegroom being well-known and highly respected in the district. There was a large attendance at the Church. The Rev. J. C. Trampleasure, Vicar of Whitley, was the officiating minister. The bride, who was given away by her brother, Mr. Oswald Frith, looked very pretty, and was attired in a beauti- ful dress of white crepoline, trimmed with lace and orange blossoms, and she carried a shower bouquet of lilies. The bridesmaids were the Misses Jennie and Lilly Frith, sisters of the bride. They were attired in dresses of blue crepoline, with bats to match, wore gold brooches and carried bouquets of lilies of the valley and daffodils, the gifts of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were discharged by Mr. Charles H. Lougshaw, of Warrington, with Mr. Alec Frith as , second groomsman. At the conclusion of the ceremony a reception was held and wedding breakfast served at the residence of the bride's mother, and was numer- ously attended by relatives and friends of the con- tracting parties. Later in the afternoon the happy couple left for Llandudno. The bride's travelling dress was of brown cloth, with hat to match.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—IINDER, THE CLOCK FO n BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Bold-street Wesleyan Church. Special services were held at the above Church on Sunday, at which the preacher was the Rev. Thomas Ross (formerly of Warrington). Collections were taken on behalf of the funds of the Wesleyan Society. With the object of further assisting the funds of the Society a social gathering was held in the schoolroom on Monday evening, when over 100 persons sat down to. an excellent tea which was provided. The Rev. G. H. Carnson presided over the subsequent proceedings, commending in a brief speech the object of the gather • ing. Mr. George Bailey, senior society steward, said there was a somewhat large deficiency at Christmas, and they wanted to make some little effort whereby they could get a clear sheet. A deficit of 109 was certainly far too much. He wished to thank the ladies and gentlemen who had given trays. They were also indebted to the ladies and gentlemen who had given sub- scriptions, amounting altogether to £5 3s. —The Rev. Joseph Manning said he hoped the out- come of the, meeting would be the fostering, and deepening of the spiritual life of 'the church. The churches and chapels at the present time were not filled as they ought to be, and the problem of filling them was not one man's work, but the work of all. The church was not aggressive, and to succeed, energy and push were required. It was a very difficult thing to fill empty !chapels, and the co-operation of all was wanted to achieve that end. (Applause.)--Mr. J. W. Cannon, lay missionary, Widnes, also addressed the meeting, and said that men and women required the fire of God, and when they got it it would make a difference in their lives.—During the evening Miss Edith Prince rendered a solo in a pleasing manner.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 11, 1905</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LIBERAL ACTIVITY.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL ACTIVITY.</p>
        <p>ENTHUSIASTIC ANNUAL MEETING OF THE WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr. J. Clayton.</p>
        <p>The increasing interest which is being taken in the progress of Liberalism in Warrington was shown by the excepdonally large attendance at the annual meet- ing of the Liberal Association held at the club on Wednesday evening, the acommodation of the assembly room being severely taxed. Mr. (',E. Parker took the chair and was supported by Alderman Pier- point, Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, Mr. W. Furness, Coun- cillor James Evans, Mr. Joseph Howes, Mr. H. Roberts, Mr. E. A. Landers, Mr. F. R. Roberts, Coun- cillor P. Peocock Mr. J. Mounfield, Mr. W. Roberts and Mr. Joseph Howes jun.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>ABSENTEES.</head>
        <p>ABSENTEES.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. JACKSON HoLmEs read letters of apology for non-attendance. Mr. Thomas Birchall wrote to say he would have liked to be present but had been contlned to the house for three weeks. He hoped they would have a good meeting. Mr. HOLMES said he would only, he was sure, be voicing the opinion of the Association if he wrote to Mr. Birchall expressing their regret at his serious illness and at his inability to be present. (Hear, hear.) Letters of apology had also been received from the Rev. H. Dunnico, Mr. James Wilcock, the Rev. J. F. Matthews and a number of other gentlemen. PRESENTATION TO MR. CLAYTON.</p>
        <p>SERVICES TO THE ASSOCIATION RECOGNIZED.</p>
        <p>Mr. PAnxErt, said that before they proceeded with the ordinary business of the meeting he had a duty to perform which was at the same time pleasant and yet unpleasant in giving ton faithful servant of the Associa- tion some slight recognition of the good work which he had for many years done for the Association. He referred to Mr. Clayton. That duty was sorrowful because of the state of the health of their friend which had completely broken down. He would not be able to serve the Association any longer, and therefore they were doing their duty in recognizing in some small way their obligation to him. (Applause.) He would ask one who knew a great deal more of Mr. Clayton's work than he did (Alderman Pierpoint) to make the presentation. (Applause). Alderman PIERPOINT said he thought Mr. Parker had fairly well expressed the deep regret they felt at their severance from Mr. Clayton. He (Alderman Pierrint) as one of the oldest members of the Liberal Association of Warrington. and as one who for a great number of years came into very close contact with Mr. Clayton, could appreciate the value of his work, which had been constant and earnest on behalf of Liberalism. Mr. Clay ton never spared himself on behalf of the cause. He was enthusiastic in his work, and did as much as anyone was capable of doing to encourage young Liberals to walk steadily in the path of Liberalism. He deeply regretted Mr. Clayton had reached a time when, unfortunately as most of them would have to -do-sooner or later, he had to lay down his weapons and become a quiet spectator of the proceedings of the party in Warrington. On his own behalf and on behalf of the Liberals of Warrington who were closely associated with him during the time he gave his work to Warrington he gave Mr. Clayton his heartfelt thanks for what he had done for them and would never forget the splendid example he had given them but would hope that the Liberal party might grow greater, stronger and better in consequence of the lessons he had given them. What had been said he thought would servo to show they had a hearty regret at Mr. Clayton being severed from them. Mr. HOLMES then read the inscription on the purse which read : "Presented by Mr. A. H. Crosfleld t 5 Clayton in recognition of his faithful services to the Liberal Association of Warrington, March, 1905." Continuing, Alderman PIERPOINT said he thought the inscription expressed exactly what they meant and therefore without further words he would hand the pre- sent to Miss Clayton with the wish that she would take from that meeting to her father that they sent their kindly remembrance combined with the hope that he would be spared for many years to watch the victorious progress of the Liberal party in Warrington. (Applause). Miss Cf.A.YTON said her father deeply regretted not being able to be present as he was very unwell. He desired however to thank the subscribers for their kind- ness in giving him that present and also to thank the officers who had been so kind and courteous to him during the seventeen years in which he had worked for the Liberal Association. Mr. PARKER explained that the purse had been given by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, the gold it contained having been subscribed by the inembers of the Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>TIIE PRESIDENCY.</head>
        <p>TIIE PRESIDENCY.</p>
        <p>AIdermanPIRRPOINT said he did not think there could be any difference of opinion as to the man who was best fitted to be president of their association. Judging by the work he had done in the past and the work he was prepared to do in the future there was no man in War- rington more fitted to occupy the position of president of their association than Mr. C. E. Parker, and he had great pleasure in proposing his re-election. (Applause.) Mr. W. ROBERTS seconded. Mr. Parker had rendered valuable servicas to the Liberal Association in the past and they could look forward to hts giving valuable services in the future. (Applause.) The proposal was carried with acclamation. MR. PARKER AND THE UNITY OF THE LIBERAL PARTY.</p>
        <p>Mr. PARKER said he was very grateful for the honour they had again done him. It was an inspiration to sae so large and enthusiastic a gathering. With regard to Liberals and Liberalism in Warrington, he could say with respect to the leaders that they had been very actively engaged of late and were absolutely united. Anything which had divided them in the past had been altogether set aside. (At this point Mr. Henry Roberts entered the room, being received with cheers). Speakers, said Mr. Parker, were often at a loss for an illustration, but when an illustration walked into the room it saved him the trouble of seeking for one. (Applause). He did not intend to mention names but he must say that if the Liberal party in ‘Varrington had had to enter upon a campaign without the assistance and co-opera- tion of a family who for very many years in Warrington had been ever in the fore-front in the fight it would have been a sad misfortune. (Applause.) To have the co-operation of that family did not surprise him in the least because, in view of the great national crisis which was before the country be felt sure that duty would have made them rally round the grand old cause of Liberalisra—(applause)—and fight under the grand old flag. What he had said about the leaders of the party he was sure he could say with equal truth about the rank and file. Whatever separating influences there had been in the party, in view of the great national crisis, they had been put aside and leaders and rank and file stood, an absolutely united party, ready to stand shoulder to shoulder in the great fight in which they were about to engage. (Applause.) They had a I candidate who was worthy to fight for Liberalism in the town a man who was worthy of their rallying round him. (Applause.) He thanked the executive and workers of the Association for the kindly consideration they had shown to him in the year just ended. ( Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>OTHER ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>OTHER ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Messrs. A. H. Crosfleld, Z. Armitage. E. J. Guest, T. Birettall, H. Roberts, and Alderman Pierpoint were elected vice-treasurers of the Association. The hon. secretary and hon. treasurers, Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes and W. Furness were re-elected. The following gentlemen were elected co-optatin members of the General Council :—Messrs. S. Dale, J. J. Crosfield, P. Perris, J. Haines, G. Thompson, J. Critchley, F. Brown,' B. Few, D, Hill, G. Elolford, J. W. Turner, It. H. Pear- son, J. Povey. J. Strange, T. Birchall, P. C. Garnett, A. Rathbone, W. W. Bibby, E. V. Walker. W. Madden, Drs. Marken, Naden, Liston, Kisby, and Smith, the Revs. J. Yonge, tf. Dunnico, E. H. Cook, and J. Davies, and Captains Roberts and Crosfleld. The Rev. J. YONGE asked that he should not be appointed a co-optative member of the Execu- tive Committee. He had undertaken so much work of late that he could not properly attend to the duties. He would, however, have them to understand he was as thorough a Liberal as ever he was and was intensely anxious for the success of the party in Warrington at the next General election. He thought however, that when men became as old as he was they might be excused from executive work but he would have them thoroughly to understand that he would do whatever he could upon every opportunity to help forward the Liberal cause. (Applause). The following gentlemen were then elected co-optative members of the Executive Committee :—Messrs. J. Mountleld, E. V. Walker, A. Rathbone, and J. Bailey, and the Rev. C. H. Cook. The business of the meeting being o-er, Roberts delivered a spirited address. He referred to the unity which existed in all classes of the Liberal party and suggested that bye-gones should be bye-gones (Applause.) During the coming contest questions of great importance were involved and with such a worthy candidate there certainly was a strong inducement for the progressive Party to be united. (Applause). This was followed by Alderman Pierpoint who heartily endorsed Mr. Roberts remarks and said he was quite willing to throw himself heartily into the coming con- test. He recognized the unity and enthusiasm of the Liberal Party and declared himself to be delighted with the tone of the meeting. Mr. Howes also spoke and referred to the necessity of every attention being given to the detail work necessary for the coming General Election. Such a spirit of unity had been evidenced as might cheer the hearts of all true reformers and if the enthusiasm mani- fested at that gathering was maintained it would do much to destroy the Tory supremacy which had been so long upheld in the Borough, and would in all proba- bility bring about a glorious victory for the Liberal cause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>MAYOR HONOURED.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR HONOURED.</p>
        <p>Presentation of Portrait.</p>
        <p>The public services rendered by the Mayor (Aldemaa Bolton) as a member of the Town Council and more particularly during his Mayoralty were fittingly recog- nized on Wednesday morning. A three-quarter-length portrait of his Worship had been painted by Mr. James Charles, the well-known Warrington artist, at the request of members of the Council, officials and friends, and at an interesting gathering in the Council ' Chamber Alderman Bolton was presented with the painting which he afterwards formally handed over to the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Fairclough) as a ' gift to the town. It is the general opinion that the likeness is a faithful one, and fully maintains the high reputation which Mr. Charles has gained in the world of art. It will be hung in the Council Chamber where three of Mr. Charles's portraits of former Mayors already adorn the walls. Amongst those in attendance at the presentation were the Mayor and Mayoress, Alderman and Mrs. Evans, Alderman Francomb, Alderman Bennett, Councillors Tinniun, Brough, P. Peacock, Fulton Smith, Ellison and Wilkinson, Messrs. James Charles, Henry Roberts, T. Birtles, J. Longland, J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), J. Moore Murray (director of education), G. F. F. White (magistrates clerk), W. 11. Woodcock (assistant overseer), and.other officials.</p>
        <p>Speeches were made by the Deputy-Mayor, the Mayor, Alderman Bennett, Councillor Tinnion, and Mr. H. Roberts.</p>
        <p>A collection of Mr. Charles's works was on view in the Council Chamber, including portraits of the Mayoress, Freda, Mr. Stanley Wilson's little daughter and a number of paintings from the North of France.</p>
        <p>~.......*.i.........</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PROGRESSIVE INSURANCE COMPANY. Record Year for the Prudential.</head>
        <p>A PROGRESSIVE INSURANCE COMPANY. Record Year for the Prudential.</p>
        <p>The wonderful progress of the Prudential Assurance Company is once more brought to the notice of the public by the issuing of the annual report, a summary of which appears in our advertising columns. Fifty-six years of well-directed efforts closed with the end of last December, and the report shows that 1901 was in many respects a record year. 71,874 policies were issued in the ordinary branch alone, assuring £6,951,650 at an annual premium of £377,587. The total premiums received in this branch during the year amounted to £3,969,016 being £151,160 more than in 1903. This, we believe, is a record for all British life offices, but what perhaps is of more interest and importance is that claims amounting to £1,651.753 were paid off. In the industrial branch also, a record has been created, as the premium income now equals £1,579,335, an increase of £132,781 during the year. The claims totalled £2,230,180, which is not exceptionally heavy considering the vast premium income. it is a striking fact that if the Government took over the whole of the Indus- trial branch to-morrow, the Prudantial, with its ordin- ary branch alone, would still remain the largest British Insurance institution in existence. The total assets of the company in both branches amount to the magni- ficent sum of £55,359,505, ;having been increased by £1,142,128 during the year. The public will be better able to realize the enormous business transactions !of the concern when it is pointed out that the claims paid work out at £12,100 a day. It is one of the proud boasts of the company that more than one-third of the population of the United Kingdom is insured with it. The local Superintendent of the Company is Mr. J. T. Rowlands Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>LIBERAL WARD MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WARD MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Latchford Ward Liberal Committee was held in the Methodist schools, Knutsford-road, oa Monday evening, Mr. J. H. Hill presiding over a large attendance. Mr. Hill was re-appointed chairman of the ward committee, and heartily thanked for his past services, and the following gentlemen were appointed vice-chairmen: —Alderman Pierpoint, Councillor Ellison, the Rev. J. F. Matthews, and Messrs. William Roberts, C. Jackson Holmes, T. Barnes' and W. Bibby, rklessrs. John Bibby W. P. James, G. H. Mile and James Higham were appointed the hon. secretaries.—The formal business over, a meeting was held to discuss the arrange- ments to be made for contesting the borough at the forthcoming General Election, and it was decided to hold at an early date a public meeting in the ward to be addressed by Mr. Joseph Howes and other gentlemen. The Chairman said the prospects of the party were bright, and they had an excellent candidate In Mr. Arthur Crosfield.</p>
        <p>A well-attended meeting of the Fairfield Ward Liberal Committee was held in the Fairfield Schools, Manchester-road on Tuesday evening for the election of officers for the ensuing year. Mr. George Walker,of Springfield. Padgate-lane was unanimously elected chairman, and the following gentlemen were elected vice-chairmen—Councillor D. S. Duncan, Messrs. Wm. Jeffrey, Wilkinson Haigh, Benjamin Farrar, James Locker, James Miles. Messrs. W. Habberley and W. P. Ashton were appointed secretaries. The formal business over, a very instructive speech was made by the Chair- man. He remarked that it was extremely gratifying to see the organization and enthusiasm in the ward in such a splendid condition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Passive Resistence.</head>
        <p>Passive Resistence.</p>
        <p>We understand that the Revs. H. Dunnico and J. S. Hughes are summoned to appear at the Petty Sessions Daresbury on Tuesday morning to show cause why they refuse to pay that portion of the poor rate ' de- voted to Sectarian purposes. In view of this Mr. Dunnico's address on Sunday evening will have more than passing interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>JUST INTRODUCED</head>
