        <p>CRAWFORD'S GOLDEN BUTTER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>BISCUITS</head>
        <p>BISCUITS</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. (By " OVaist.";</p>
        <p>Warrington are now experimenting with a will in I order to discover the side best calculated to carry the club into the final, if not further. They might have begun a month or two earlier with a dvantage,but even now it is not too late. The great experiment last Saturday in the opening round of the cup-ties was the playing of Thomas at half back. As the result was a foregone conclusion there was no risk attached to the change, and it must be admitted that it did not work at all badly. Thomas is always energetic and in many ways skilful, and he was quite good enough for the Morecambe men at times he showed:a:disinclina- tion to keep on-side, but time will soon erase such a fault as this. He worked well with J. Davies, and Warrington won by the nice round total of 30 points to nil.</p>
        <p>The AI )recambe men did not leave their hearts in their dressing bags. They opened very determinedly and were never a demoralised side Warrington took matters rather easily, and they did not look like scor- ing until the Seasiders menaced their line. Then the team came away in a body, and a fine combined run the length of the field left Morecambe five points in arrear. At half time the total had grown to 9 points. In the second half, Warrington showed that they could play if they liked, and the Morecambe line was crossed five times as the result of clever combination, gener- ally initiated by J. Davies. Six goals, six tries was the final score, Harris and Dickenson getting two tries :each, Berry one try and four goals, Cook one try, and Fish and Preston a goal each.</p>
        <p>The most remarkable feature was the fact that Fish did not get a try. Somehow the Warrington halves nearly always went to the right, and Fish was stand- ing idle most of the time. He certainly got rather irapatient towards the close, but it was no use. Still, his dropped goal was a neat effort, and proves Fish to possess an extra qualification not suspected. But if Warrington are opposed to 3 serious opponent in the later rounds of the Cup tourney, they will have to play more to Fish's wing in order to win. Variety is essential, and both wings should be well-plied with work.</p>
        <p>There was little attacking ability displayed by the Morecambe men, and apparently not much pace behind. The forwards worked hard against a much stronger eight, and the backs all tackled well. Levi Jackson played a really sound game at full back, and without him the score must have been ranch larger. But a comparison between the teams is unnecessary. They were in quite a different class from one another.</p>
        <p>There is not much profit for the Warrington club out of the Moracambe match, in fact, people will be surprised to hear that when all expenses are paid the Club will actually be a few paunds to tho bad. The gate money totalled £79 18s. Ild, out of which Warrington had to pay £l6 4s. for members, leaving the treasurer a net gate of £63 14s. 11d. Of this the Morecambe Club received £32 133. 2d. and other expenses totalled up to £l4 12s. 6d. thus leaving Warrington the paltry balance of £l6 19s. 2d. for the payment of players wages, rent, rates, etc. The Cup- ties are by no means the gold-mine people often imagine, unless a club has a big match every round and runs into the final.</p>
        <p>There are other reas3ns however, for considering Warrington fortunate in the draw. The second round will see them opposed to Keighley, and though the latter defeated Salford they should prove fairly easy victims at Wildersp33l. Hops is already runninst high in the town. Warrington may after all have a voice once more iu the destination of the Cup. A third time in'the final—ifthey get there—will surely pay for all! The draw for the second round, to be played next Saturday, is as follows : Swinton v. Bradford.—Referee, G. F. Dickinson (Halifax). Bstley v. Oldham.—ReferAe, J. Bruckshavr (Stockport.) Huddersfield v. Wakefield Trinity.—Referee, ILEdniond- son (Bradford). Leigh v. Flalifax.—Referee, F. Renton (Hunslet). Broughton Rangers v. St. Helens.—Referee, E. H. Smirke (Preston.) Warrington v.Keighley.—Referee,J.Oakland (Barnsley). Hull Kingston Rovers v. Leeds.—Referee, J. H. Cromp- ton (Bradford). Hull v. Hunslet.—Referee,W. Robinson (111anninghara.) The draw is a good one for the Lancashire clubs for only one of them—Oldham—has to make the journey into Yorkshire. whereas Bradford, Halifax, and Keighley have all to travel into the County Palatine. A singular Mature of the draw is that both the Leeds clubs have to go to Hull.</p>
        <p>The great match of the first round was at Leigh, where Wigan received their quietus in the presence of a record crowd of 13,003, the receipts being £335. Wigan were superior in all departments but half back, wnere Hopkins and Thomas proved unequal to the rigours of a cup-tie. For such stern struggles, dash is more essential than cleverness behind the pack, and Warrington appear to have realised this sooner than the Wiganers.</p>
        <p>To-day the clubs return te the calmer atmos- phere of League football. Warrington receive a visit from Hull Kingston Rovers and ought to atone for the severe defeat sus- tained at Hull. Another bold experiment has been decided on. Berry having made his place secure at centre, Isherwood has been brought in at half back with Thomas, and I see no reason why he should not do well. It is rather odd, however, to see 'Warrington represented at half by not one of the regular half backs. Neverthless the back division is the strongest possible. Combination is alone required, and this the new halves will have to inaugurate.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team will be : Hallam, full back ; Fish, Berry, Dickenson, and Harris, three-quarters ; Isherwood and Thomas, half backs; Preston, Shngars, Belton, Cook, Swift, Jolley, Naylor, and Harmer, for- wards.</p>
        <p>The" A" team has a Combination match with Rochdale Hornets" A' away, and will be represented as follows : Knight, full back, Kenyon, Brooks, J. Davies, Jordan, three-quarters ; It. Bate and W. Bate, half backs ; Morrison, Coleman, Ashton, Forster, and Fairhurst, forwards.</p>
        <p>Next week Warrington "A " should have played Chadderton here, and unless the match can be arranged to follow the cup-tie Warrington will have to halve the points, which will put them out of the rurning for the Combination Championship.</p>
        <p>Thomas confirmed the best impressions in the memorial match with Cheshire County on Tuesday. He was the best back on the Warrington side and did excellent work in opening out the game. The contest was a very good one,and Warrington well earned their victory by 5 points to nil. The forwards played especially well, the work in the loose being at times really high-class. As a result of the match it is expected that' about .£2,5 will be available for the widow of the late Joe Richardson of Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Warrington have signed on Herbert Hall, of Flia- ton (half-back), and a new forward from the Wigan district named J. Thomas.</p>
        <p>There is a late change in the teara for to-day, Brooks corning in for Ihrris, who is raiting his knee .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Hockey.</head>
        <p>Hockey.</p>
        <p>[By R.G.P.] Last week the Stockton Heath first eleven were left without a fixture owing to an unfortunate mistake, bat the 2nd eleven managed to get up a scratch match with Lotchford 2nd string. The match was played on the Latchford ground, and as It. Pierpoint, S. Baxter, W. Richardson and G. Hatton appeared in the visitors, team, the home side could hardly hope to avert defeat. As it was after a game of a fairly even description, Stockton Heath were victorious by 34. The goals for Stockton Heath were scored by S. Baxter 2, A. FergusOn 1. A. Ferguson at outside right played a good game, and should develop into a first class wing forward.</p>
        <p>The game between Timperley and Sale proved a very level match, as games between these two clubs always do, and Sale just tetannged to claim the victory by 3-2. Northern had very little difficulty with South Niau- chaster and won by 5-0, the south have been travelling very badly of late. Bowdon visited Western and made a draw of two goals each. The Western defence played their best game this season.</p>
        <p>Ahlezley Edge visited Kersaland after an even game, the final whistle sounded with the snore 3 all. Latchford visited New Brighton Park, and although only playing</p>
        <p>5</p>
        <p>ten men, scored alood victory. by 2-1. New Brighton Wanderers were in great form, defeating West Kirby by 8-2.</p>
        <p>This week Stockton Heath play New Brighton Wan- deers, when the following will represent Stockton Heath :-Ist (at home) R. G. Pierpoint (goal), E.. E. Woods and E. Pierpoint (backs), J. Richardson, G. Hatton and L. Fairclough (halves), C. H. Bond, S. Baxter, R. Pierpoint, W. Richardson and A. Ferguson (Forwards). Referee, N. W. Bellamy. Bully off 2.4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WARRNGTON GROCERS ANNUAL DINNER. Councillor Wilkinson and Warrington's</head>
        <p>WARRNGTON GROCERS ANNUAL DINNER. Councillor Wilkinson and Warrington's</p>
        <p>Progress.</p>
        <p>Formed four years ago the Warrington and District Grocers' and Provision Dealers' Association has increased annually in numbers, and in its sphere of Use- fulness. On Thursday the members held their annual dinner at the Winmarleigh Cafe", when the flourishing condition of the Association was shown by the large number who sat down to an excellent repast, the cater- ing for which gave every possible satisfaction., Mr. Joiry GANDY (the president) was at the head of the table daring the subsequent proceedings, and upon his proposition the usual loyal toasts were heartily dnualz.</p>
        <p>THE FEDERATTON. Mr. GANDY proposed" The Federation " coupled with the names of Mr. Kellett, Liverpool, and, Mr. W. H. Lever, Port Sunlight. He remarked that the federation had done a good work although it was oal v in its infancy, and was calculated to continue to do a very good work on behalf of the Grocers Association through- out the country. (Applause.) The toast was heartily drunk. Mr. KELLETT in responding spoke of the valuable services rendered by Mr. Lever in the formations of the federation. With the alteration in many of the condi- tions tit conducting their business, with legislation trying to interfere, it was necessary they should com- bine and protect their interests. They had combined and now had the strongest organization in the country whose influence was felt in Parliament and in the law courts. (Applause). Mr. Kellett proceeded to deal with matters affecting grocers speaking of the necessity of having all invoices made legal guarantees. He pointed our that a retail invoice for butter was a guarantee whereas the wholesalers' invoices were not guarantees as to the quality of the articles sold. Deal- ing with the question of the adulteration of butter which is at present agitating the grocer world, Mr. Kellett advocated the procuration of a standard of 18 per cent. for moisture contained in butter. Grocers were agitat- ing for a lower standard which it was not possible could be maintained in home-made butter and which if adopted would seriously handicap English and Irish manufacturers who produced two thirds of the butter used in the country. The adoption of a standard of 18 per cent, would not interfere with home products and would prevent the wholesale adulteration of butter which was going on in various parts of the country. Mr. Kellett dealt with many other matters of interest to the trade.</p>
        <p>Mr. LEVER also responded to the toast, and spoke in enthusiastic terms of the Federation Benevolent fund, strongly advocating its claim for support from grocers. Grocers he said had a mistaken view of Co-operative society. He had been in many countries where there were similar societies and in countries where such societies did not exist. In the United States where there were no such societies the grocery business was infinitely worse than it was in England on account of the "cutting" which went on- There an article Was "cut' not to the smallest margin of profit but until it was sold for a direcc loss, and if the co-operative societies did not exist in England the grocers would have to face the more serious competi- tion of cutting prices. They had nothing to fear from Co-operative Societies and if they only looked after their business they would he able to hold their own, bat they most endeavour to conduct their business intelligently and look after the interests of the public. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>TRY: GUESTS.</head>
        <p>TRY: GUESTS.</p>
        <p>This toast was received with musical honours on the proposition of Mr. Monks, Messrs. 13.. Smith, Liverpool, Lightbody, Liverpool, and Cradleld, Manchester, briefly responding. TFIE TOWN AND TRADE OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR WILKINSON AND THE TOWN'S PROGRESS. Mr. Rictrintosoe proposed the toast of the town and trade of Warrington, coupled with the name of Coun- cillor Wilkinson. He would like to say how pleased he was at the harmonious conditions which existed between the grocers and the Town Council. (Hear, hear.) From time to time the grocers had made suggestions to the Town Council, which, he was pleased to say, had always been received in the right spirit. The past year from the point of view of trade had been one of the worst years they had had for a long time, but he thought the worst was over and they ware already beginning to have better times. (Hear, hear.) He was Pleased to see the development of local patriotism amongst the rising generation, and to note that young men were coming forward and taking their places in the social and municipal life of the Borough. They had been accused of apathy and indifierence in municipal affairs but he thought they were waking up to the fact Ithat the town required business-like management, and were introducing business-like qualities into their town life and were taking the first place amongst the rising. towns of Lancashire. (Applause). People in the past had however been apathetic and as there was a good deal of lee-way to make up and they were bearing the burden of the neglect of men in the past. Forty years ago men regarded the town as fit for themselves and were satisfied but a different feeling now- existed and the. town was being made fit for 2.5 years hence. There was a great deal of grumbling about the high rates and the spending of money but if they saw they were gotting value for their money he thought that grumbling would. not be heard. There had been depression in trade but in Warrington it had not been felt so keenly as it had in other towns and he thought they were in a position to congratulate themselves upon that acco un*., ( App I 1•113 The toast was received with musical honours. Councillor Witaresos, in response, said he would like to call their attention to the fact that during the last 25 years Warrington had doubled its population, and had doubled its assessable value, and he hoped that during the next 25 years the town would again double itself. (Hear, hear.) Warrington people were carrying the burden of their fathers' neglect, and were suffering from their lack of enterprise. Mr. Richardson had referred to the high rates, and there was no doubt War- rington people had-great cause for grumbling. But if they were paying high rates it was brought about by the enterprise which had been shown within the past ten Tears in the Council Chamber, and by the endeavour to bring the town up-to-date, and in some departments to ever take the lead. There was no doubt they were the first people in England to establish an isolation for poor children striken down with fever. A sanatorium had been built at Sankey to enable the town to deal with epidemics of small-pox; the town possessed a fine technical institute which he hoped would before long compare favourably with any in the land, they had their electricity and tramway system, gas works and water works, and there was one blessing they owned them and were not like many towns in which the provision of those things bad been left with private enterprise, and which would at some time have to take upon themselves the burden of an enormous expense as Warrington had done in the past and from which they were now suffering. lAs years went on there was no doubt they could ' forward to Warrington rates coming down much lower than they were at the present time. (Hear, hear.) He did not think there was any fear that they would get any higher. (Applause.) The town had had the education thrust upon them which had caused the rates to rise to the extent of 11d, in the L. lie was a heavy ratepaver himself and he had borne the high rates with- out a murmur because he felt that for 30 years they had staved off a school board which would have been a much greater thing to tackle than that which they ware having to tackle as the result of the Education Act. (Hear, hear.) War- rington was always spoken of as a town of many trades and he thought in that respect the town was blessed. He was pleased to look back and see how new industries had sprung up in the town, which in themselves, he thought, spoke well for the value of Warrington as a commercial centre. Even if the rates were high they would compere favourably with the rates in the whole of England. He thought the town was stillhandicapped as it was handicapped when the London and North- Western Railway wanted to bring their works here— by selfish landlordism. Warrington people could look back with pride upon the prosperous strides which they had made in the past and he only hoped that in the future those strides would be even greater. (Applause)</p>
        <p>OTHER TOAST&amp; The toast of kindred Asqociation was drank on the proposition of Hr. K.i,r, Mr. SHAW (Butcher's Associa- tion) briefly responding In proposing the healt. * hof the President, HW r. .H. Owen., Mr. T. S. Steel and Hr. W. Taylor spoke inhighly complimentary terms of the valuable services rendered. to the Association by Mr. Gandy. The toast was reeived with =deal honours, .llr GAisiDv briefly responding.</p>
        <p>SPREES! BY MR. H. ROBERTS. Mr. HENRY Ronal's, who arrived at a late stage in the proceedings, and who was accorded a hearty recap tion, said he had come at so late an hour for Mar that his absence should be misunderstood. He had, however, promised to preside at the Welsh concert, and an alteration in the date of that concert bad.caused his absence during the earlier proceed- ings. Re most cordially wished their Association prosperity, and the members of the Association personal prosperty. They were going through rather bad times andsome of them could unfortunately remember having gone through bad times before. It was however not cloudy all over the world at the same time. The snn was always shining somewhere, and in all probability it i would shine upon them before they anticipated t. He congratulated them upon having so successful a gather- ing and hoped that as years went by their association would increase in numbers and in its influence. (Ap- plause.) During the proceedings songs were given by Messrs W. H. Ireland, W. H. Owen, Princept, Griffiths (Liver- pool), and Lenton, Mr. T. Taylor aotod as accompanist.</p>
        <p>14- :` •</p>
      </div>
