      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (presiding), Aldermen Francomb and Smethurst, Councillors Tinnien and Wilkinson, and Messrs. J. W. Hayward, George Pill- ing, H. Thornton, W. Roberts and S. Wright. DRUNK, FIGHTING AND LOITERING.—AIfred Beckett, 37, Scotland-road. and William Coyne, Rose and Crown- street, were charged with having been drunk and fight- ing in Arpley-road on Saturday night. The Chief Con- stable said the prisoners were loafers, and never worked. The former had been convicted 16 times and the latter 32. They were sent to gaol for one month with hard labour.—Thomas Hennegan, of no settled abode, was sent to gaol for a month for loitering in Horsemarket- street at twelve o'clock on Sunday night with intent to commit a felony. THEFT OF SKIRTS.—Edwin Linaker, 2, Robinson's- yard, was charged with stealing from a draper's shop in buttermarker-street two ladies' skirts, value 116.10 d.— Sergeant Barnett said he saw the prisoner take down the skirts which were hung outside Messrs. Lee and Evans' shop, and hurry off with them. The officer overtook him and the prisoner said he wanted to go to Wigan. If the officer would let him go he would take the articles back.—He was fined 10s. A LOITRRER.—James Hennigan, a convicted thief, was sentenced to a month's hard labour for loitering in Buttermarket-street during the night with intent to commit a felony.—P.S. Barnett said the prisoner told him he intended 'to be in somewhere before morning." . .</p>
        <p>THIRTY-NINTH APPEARANCE.—Mary Broderick ma-de her thirty-ninth appearance charged with drunkenness and was fined ss._and.costs.</p>
        <p>DISORDER.—AIfred Holmes was fined in the costs (3s. 6d.) for disorderly behaviour in Ellesmere-street. STREET FOOTBALL—Frank Millington, 13, Thewlis- street ; Robert Ritchie, 17, Mill-lane; James Long, 185, Wellfleld-street : Wm. Broadhurst, 61, Bostock-steeet ; George Wm. Hunt, 50. Baxter-street, youths, were each ined 3s. 6d. for playing at football in Mill-lane.—Five small boys named Fred Risley, Walter Hall, Frank Fear, Alfred Gibbons, and John Wytherley, all of Catherine- street, were fined Is. each for a similar offence in Catherine-street. _ _ .</p>
        <p>UNFIT TO Wog.—Edward Marshall, Risley Moss, was summoned for causing a pony to be ill-treated by working it whilst in an unfit state.—lnspector Gibbons, R.S.P.C.A. proved the case, and said the animal was in good condition but suffered from lameness.—Defendant said the pony was "as amiiue. as a bell of brass and as fat as a pig."--He was fined in the costs (6a.) BRONCHITIS AND ASTHMA</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>SUFFERED MANY YEARS. CURED BY TENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</head>
        <p>SUFFERED MANY YEARS. CURED BY TENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. CLARK, 58, Nelson-street, Aberdeen, writes : "I have been suffering many years with chronic bron- chitis and asthma. In July last I commenced taking VENG'S LIGHTNING CocaH CURE, I ba4 then been off work six months. The first dose gave me relief and I rapidly improved. It has indeed made a remarkable change ; in fact, I am now cured, enjoying good health, quite a new man; everybody is surprised. For a long time I was in a low state having keen weaken( 1 by con- tinual vomiting of blood. Your Cough Cure graaually stopped the vomiting. I cannot realise, after so mach doctoring and suffering, how your Cough Cure has cured me so quickly." V&amp;No's LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is the most efficient remedy procurable for coughs, colds, btonchitis, asthma, catarrh, weak lungs, and children's coughs. 91d., is. I id., and 2s. 9d. at Chemists every where.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WORKINGMEN'S HELP FOR THE</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S HELP FOR THE</p>
        <p>INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Increased Contribution Despite Bad</p>
        <p>Trade</p>
        <p>Since its formatioL in 1830 the Warrington Working- men's Committee has been instrumental in raising over £13,000 for the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensaty. Another very satisfactory year's work was under review at the annual meeting of the Committee held at the Town Hall on Saturday afternoon. The Mayor (Alder- man Bolton)presided and was supported by the president of the Committee (Mr. J. Charlton Parr), the Chairman (Councillor John Roberts), the Hon. Secretary (Mr. Robert Higham) and the Secretary of the Infirmary (Mr. J. H. J. Hampson). There was a large attendance of representatives from the various works. The sc 3re- tary announced the receipt of letters regretting inability to attend from Dr. Bowden, the Rev. Father Whittle, Dr. Richmond, and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. Mr. Arthur Down had also written aeknowledging a letter of sym- pathy forwarded by the Committee on the death of his father. THE vAtur. 01' THE INSTITUTION TO THE WORKING</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>CLASSES.</head>
        <p>CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in a short address described the institu- tion as being probably the most useful which the working-classes had at their command. He was very i pleased to know that t was so much appreciated, as was shown by the fact that the subscriptions during the year had increased. The need for support was greater, and he would point out that 2,000 more patients had been treated at the Infirmary this year than in the previ- ous twelve months.</p>
        <p>B. Percival, Captain Higson (lay evangelis-t) and Mr. Webb. During the evening refreshments were handed THE FUNDS. round, and an enjoyable time was brought to a close Mr. HAMPSON, in presenting the financial statement, by the singing of the Doxology. said the Tear had been one of particular anxiety to all engaged in charitable work, but notwithstanding trade depression, he was pleased to announce that the con- tributions from the works showed an increase of £23. The Dewsbury Sanitary Inspector (Mr. Thackray) states that since his appointment, 15 years ago, he has The amount raised from the employs at the works, &amp;c., was £750 14s. 2d., as compared with £727 14s. last year. dealt with 1,394, cases of small-pox, a significant fact umerous other donations had been given to the considering that Dewsbury is a strong anti-vaccination N Infirmary through the COMMittl 3, and the total sum centre. handed over, after deducting expenses, was £872 12s. Id. Richard Hodgson, an inmate of Clitheroe Workhouse, as against £855 19s. last year, stated at Clitheroe on Thursday that he had paid so Councillor JOHN ROBERTS said he was informed that 4 much in rates that half the Clitheroe Workhouse since the inauguration of the committee in (1880 the belonged to him, if everyone had their own. He got amount raised for the institution had been £13,379 1 fourteen days for quitting it without leave.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>" I firmly believe that if the children were well fed, our workhouses, gaols and asylums would soon be empty." Sir John Gorst, M.P.</p>
      </div>
