      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <p>a</p>
        <p>SMOII</p>
        <p>Cocoa</p>
        <p>STRIMENT TO 4.8. BEEF OR TEN CUPS OF ' COCOA and is absolutely free from. chemicals. aGESTION, BRACES THE NERVES, INVIGORATES THE SYSTEM. IN TINS SD., 9D., 1/4 AND 1/6.</p>
        <p>14s. 11d. The speaker paid a tribute to the valuable services of the hon. secretary (Mr. R. Higham) and Mr. Hampson. He urged the members of the committee to do all they could to increase the anneal contribution to £l,OOO. He could assure them that there was no extravagance whatever in the working of the institu- tion, in fact the methods would be difficult to improve upon. The committee could rest satisfie 1 that all the money they collected was wisely spent. Mr. J. C. PARR moved the adoption of the balance sheet which, he said, was eminently satisfactory. It was gratifying that the workingn en of the town valuc I thp institution and had taken up its suppoi i, with so much vigour, as Englishmen always did when they had an institution which ney prized. The general desire to assist the institution was evidenced by the way in which subscriptions came in from time to time from various clubs, congregations of all denomin- ations, dramatic societies, the police force and other organizations. After the encouraging remarks of their chairman he was sure the efforts of that committee would not slacken, but that they would from year to year increase their subscription until the large figure he had mentioned was attained. As to the proposed enlargement of the Inffrmary ho hardly felt competent to speak about it, except to say that the matter was in the hanci f the executive committee and he knew they would do what they could to make the institution more useful, if DosSi ble, and of benefit to an even larger number of people than at present. The Rev. JOHN YONGE seconded the adoption of the report and congratulated the workingmen on their splendid efforts during a year which, he supposed, had been one of the most Awkward in their history from the point of view of raising money for charitable objects. The fact that so large a sum had been collected was a distinct triumph, and he hoped the members rejoiced in it and would be encantage 1 to go forward to even better things in the brighter times they hor 3d were coming. (Hear, hear.) , He did not think the time cou'd be very far distsnt when the Infirmary would have to be extended. The matter had already . .31 very conaieerably discussed by the Board of Management and the reason nothing had been done was not indisposition do ,it, but they had not yet quite hit upon the best wr of accomplishing it He was strongly of opinion that Warrington could do anything it liked in the direction of raising money and he trusted those present would inspire their friends and fellow-workmen to be as generous as they could in the support of an institution which lay very near to the interest and welfare of all classes. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. T. WrtarNsoN seconded by Mr. CROMULL, the delegates present were appointed a General Commitee with power to add to their number. Mr. G. THOILPSON said he had pleasure in proposing that for the twenty-fifth yenr in succession, Councillor Roberts be elected chairman of the committee. A better chai marl he thought it would be impossible to have. _</p>
        <p>Mr. T. WADSWORTH seconded and the motion was cordially agreed to, Councillor Roberts returning thanks.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. J. ELWELL, jun., seconded by Mr. J. Purrs, Councillor Barton was re-elected deputy-chairman. The following gentlemen were elected to represent the Committee on the Board of Management : Councillors J. Roberts and T. Burtor, Messrs. C. M. Watkin and T. A. Wilkinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>THANKS.'</head>
        <p>THANKS.'</p>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL CLUB'S SUBSCRIPTION. Mr. C. WHITE moved avote of thanks to the chairman deputy-chairman, secretary, treasurer, hon. auditor, (Mr. C. Jackson Holmes), and representative on the Education Committee(Mr. G. Walker) for truth services during the year. Mr. MASSEY seconded and the motion was cordially agreed to. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. A. C. Ilmrs proposed a vote of thanks to the Press, Chief Constable and Police, Warrington Rugby Football Club, the committee of the May Day pro- cession of Horses, and the members of Mr. T. J. Down's band for their help. He expressed the hope that the football club, which con- sisted mainly of working people,would see its way cicar not to decrease it subscription during the year but to raise it by a lump sum. The club, he considered,was doing an injustice to the people of Warrington by decreasing its subscription from £34 155., in 1903 to £2 Is. during the past year. ine club might, without injuring itself, subscribe £5O and he trusted an effort would be mutest° raise that amount.</p>
        <p>Mr. WALTERS _seeoncled the and it Vill4carried.</p>
        <p>The hon. medical officers, senior and junior house surgeons, matron, and nurses were thanked on the motion of Mr. T. SMITH seconded by Mr. F. TAYLOR. Mr. G. THOMPSON remarked that with a view to meeting the increased pressure on the time of the doctors at the Infirmary, it was proposed to appoint another hon. medical officer. Two were not sufficient to cope with the work. The MAYOR was cordially thanked for presiding and granting the use of the Council Chamber, and regret was expressed at the absence through illness of Mr. F. Monks.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. C. r AAR. was also thanked for showing his interest in the committee's work by attending.</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Church Army Mission. A successful social in connection with the above, was held in the Mission Room John-street on Tuesday evening. The musical part of the programme opened with the " Glory song" by the choir, followed by solos duets, recitations &amp;c., by the workers and friends. The gathering was addressed by the Rev. . . • . _</p>
      </div>
