        <p>SANCTION FOR LOANS. COUNCILLOR LORD AND TILE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD'S CENSURE.</p>
        <p>deposit schedule of prices with your officials for any contracts that they may be successful in obtaining." Councillor Plimugu'roN said the standing order already in existence covered that point. His resolution was _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Tom: CLERK, at a meeting of the Finance and Estate Committee, held on February 6th, laid before the 'meeting the following letter from the Local Govern- ment Board : "Local Government 'Board, Whitehall, S.W. 18th February, 1905. am directed by the Local Government Board to advert to your letter of the 21st ultimo, and to st.ite that in view of the explanation I contained therein, they have decided to approve of the borrowing by the Tow Council of Warrington of the sum of £492 in respect of expenditure incurred in connec- tion with the manure storeat their depot. The Board's 'formal approval is enclmed he ewith. lam to explain that in fix ng the period allowed for the repayment of the loan we Board have had regard to the fact that the works in respect, of which the loan is required were carried out about five years ago, and they have there- foal deduct Id five years from the r riod they would usually allow for repayment of a loan for the works in question. lam at the same time directed to state that in the event of tke Town Council again anticipating sanction, the Board will in addition to redncing the period allowed for repayment of the loan, also make a reduction in the amounts sanctioned. They will be pre- pared to sanction only such amount as would at the time be properly outstanding if a loan 0 mat the ex- penditure had been sanctioned prior to such expenditure being incurred.—l am, sir, your obedient servant, Not T. KERSHAW, assistant secretary. The Town Clerk, Warrington. The DEPUTY-31 AYOR moved, and Alderman Beriett seconded, the passing of the minutes. Councillor LORD alluded to that part of the letter of the Local 'Government Board commencing with the words : "I am to explain," and ending at ' expenditure being incurred." He contended that as a Conncil,they, were seriously censured. The Council ought to know who was to blame for the irregularity. The MAYOR: The Local Government Board like to say something. If they didn't complain and grumble people would think they had gone out of existence. Councillor Loan: And you think there is no justifi- cation? The MAYOR: That is a matter for then, The TOWN CLERIC: I have printed the letter in the , minutes to draw the attention of the Council to the matter. 1 The MAYOR: If you have done wrong you must not I do itagain. (Laughter.) There is a deal of red tap* about the Local Government Board. Councillor PEMBERTON replied that he did not think it was a question of red tape. It was a question of money having been spent before the Corporation had permission to borrow the money. One of the safe- guards of the ratepayers was that a Local Government Board inquiiy should be held before money was spent. It was a pity the Corporation should be censured in the way they had been, but he was afraid this wort not the only case. The DEPUTY-MAYOR: This is not a new thing and the Finance Committee ar not to blame, but the com- mittees who spend more than they ought. If we waited for the sanction of the Local Government Board before we made alterations the waterworks would be stopped and the cost to the ratepayers would be thousands of pounds. The minutes were approved. PHYSICAL INSTRUCTION FOR SCHOOL CHILDREN. At a meeting of the Gymnasium'Committee the Chair- man submitted the report of the Joint Sub-committees appointed to discuss the question of physical instruction to be given to children attending public elementary schools. _</p>
        <p>an extension of It. He witnted every member of the Council to have the right of access to all minutes or papers, or works of the Council at any time. Every member of the Council had been appointed to marege the town's affairs and he considered that every man ! was presumed to be honest. It did not follow that he should not be trusted with the papers of any Committee ke was not sitting on. i The MAYOR: If your way of looking at things was i right we should not need committees at all. I Councillor PexpereroN : I claim the right of every I member of the Council to have access to the minutes I and papers of any Committee. I was on one Committee ' and was distinctly refused access by the official and the Chairman. The matter was one ievolvine £29,000. He , wanted to see how the difference between the amount ' paid and the contract prices was made up. The St Ading I Orders gave him the right. The MAYOR: The reasonable right. Councillor PEMBERTON : I asked for the contract which had been in existence 2i years and the bills which had been sent in from time to time. I wanted to go down into the wells at Winwick and see how much of the edit had been driven as compared with the amount steted in the accounts. If the thing was above board there was no reason why he should not see the works ! and papers. The work had cost nearly double the amount i it should have done, and if it had been done properly there should be no objection to it being inspected. The MAYOR: Ido not see why you should see bills of quantities. Councillor PEMBERTON: Why not? The contract ' has been accepted. If the Council was going to refuse members the facility for checking work which was being ' done it was no use him being there at all. Councillor LORD Sc 3onded the motion. Conncillor Sean, said he thought Councillor Pember- ton had put the case very fairly. It was really question- ing the honesty of members of the Council if they were I confined to the committees they eet on. If 10 members I were to be trusted with certain information surely the others were? He claimed that the members of the , Council had the right if they thought fit of inspecting the works of any committee. I The TOWN CLERK: Would you claim the right of inspecting schedules of prices? I Councillor STOTT: Certainly, if the members of one committee had the right. The MAYOR: I should certainly refuse you any private correspondence 'elating to the dispute with the Ship Canal Company. Councillor STOTT : That would not be reqairt I. Councillor TINNTON said he thought it would be wise to rescind the exiating resolution awl not pace the other. If they carried the second there was no document in I possession of the Town Clerk which they would not have the right to inspect, He could conceive it possible ! for a man to have serious temptations if he had the right Ito inspect all documents. It would be a mistake to extend the standing orders. ICouncillor WILKINSON said if he was a builder he would refuse to tender to the Corporation under those conditions. Councillor JAMES EVA.Ns raid that with regard to I passing charges for work done, the bills for rtyments had to be revised by the head official of the department concerned. That was the crux of the situation. If they had an official they could not trust they would IKnow what to do, but ittlie- official certified that the work was done and the money due, he did not see how much farther they could get. He thought, however, that a measure of freedom should be extended to any I member of the Council to visit any department Councillor LORD said that head officials of the Cor- poration had given orders that members be refused . . .</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION moved the confirmation of the Gymnasium Committee'sproe,eedings, and remarked that the sub-committee of the Education Committee had . .</p>
        <p>admission to certain works unless on the respo-nii6ie. committee. Councillor PEMBERTON : If a member of the Council</p>
        <p>agreed to the suggestion of paying £2O-0 for drill instruction, and the matt3r had now been amicably settled. Councillor STOTT said that with regard to the new schools about to be built he hoped the question of pro- viding them with gymnasiums would not be lost sight of. The MAYOR said the Board of Education bound them down to certain lines, but they would do what they Zould in the matter. Councillor TONION said that while he agreed with Councillor Stott as to the advisability of providing schools with gymnasiums, and that it would be better to do that in the long run, at the present time it would be too exp.-wive an item, especially as they would have to build thrae other schools in addition to the two</p>
        <p>- is not to be allowed to inspect a schedule of prices how are you going to check £20,000 of extra work? The MAYOR : You employ an official for that. Councillor PEMBERTON then offered to withdraw the rst resolution. The MAYOR : You cannot do th%t ; they are bound together. Councillor PEMBERTONT: Then I withdraw both of them. The discussion thus eime to an end summarily.</p>
        <p>Brick-street Mission Band of Hope. In connection with the above, the sth entertainment of the season was held in the schoolroom on Tuesday evening last. The meeting was presided over by Mr. James Shaw. Recititaiions were given by Nally Brooks, Jane Riley, Florrie Starkey, Annie Bailey, Martha Riley, Alice Starkey, Ethel Wainwright and John Whittle. Solos were rendered by Eva Shaw and Ethel Lightfoot. Duets were sung by Miss Bowyer, and Miss Leigh. Selections on the mandoline and violin were given by Miss Broomhead and Miss Ashbrook. A dialogue entitled, "What Susan did with her birthday sovereign," was given by Elizabeth Appleton and Alice Heesom. The enter. tainment was concluded by an amusing It3m entitled "McNamara's Band" given by a numb3r of boys from Ebenezer Mission.</p>
        <p>decided upon and they would cost £16,000 each. How- ever the new schools would have lateral halls which</p>
        <p>would serve somewhat the same purpose and would prove very useful. The minutes were approved. RESIGNING OFFICIALS. RECOGNITION OF VALUABLE SERVICES. The MAYOR read the following letter from Mr. Stanley Wilson :—" Town Clerk's office, Warrington, 27th February, 1905. Dear Mr. Mayor,—Having been appointed Town Clerk of Leigh, I beg to tender nay resignation asjDeputy Town Clerk of your borough. In severing my service with the Corporation, I need hardly say that Ido so with feal Ings of profound regret. I desire to thank you and all the members of the Council for the kindness and good !Ealing shown to me during the 22 years that I have been connected with the , municipal work of my native town. I shall always remember with gratitude and pleasure the support and encouragement that I have received from your esteemed and able Town Clerk, and I am glad of this opuortuuity of publicly thanking him and all the officials of the Corporation for the uniform kindnees they have at all times shown to me." (Applause.) Cont,nuing, the Mayor said that seeing that Mr. Wilson had secured the post of Town Clerk of Leigh, he had also done War- rington credit by st owing that we had ra official whom [other boroughs appreciated. As a public servant Mr. ' Wilson had_ done his best and served the town well and</p>
        <p>faithfully all the time he had been connected with it. (Hear, hear.) He bad watched him rising st3p by step, and he hoped he would have every success in his new position. (Hear, hear). Councillor P. PEACOCK said that while they were on that topic he might express the hope that Mr. Wilson's successor would be appointed from their pre ent staff. The committee might bear that in mind. The Mayon 1: The Committee has to inquire into the duties of the office and report on what is required.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>No Breakfast Table complete without</head>
        <p>No Breakfast Table complete without</p>
        <p>EPPS'S</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>The Most Nutritious and EconomicaL</p>
        <p>ZAM-BUK STOPS AN EPIDEMIC. Family of Six Children Cured. DOCTORS ASTOUNDED -PARENTS GRATEFUL.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Wycliffe P.S.A. Society Anniversary.</p>
        <p>Past Work Reviewed.</p>
        <p>The twelth anniversary of the Wycliffe P.S.A, Society was celebrated on Sunday by the holding of a special service, at which there was a large congregation. The speaker was the president, the Rev. John Yonee who spoke on, "Looking backward, loeking upward, looking onward." He said he wished the Wycliffe P.S.A. Many happy returns of the day !" Those who had been in this P.S.A. from the very beginning would doubtless be looking back to-day to the sth of March, 1893, when the society came into existence, and remem- ber somewhat of the thoughts and feelings with which they regarded that ever" men,orable Sunday afternoon. It cost an outlay of £5O to make the pre- liminary arrangements; and for that money Ithey were indebted to very generous con- tributions from a good many friends. Then came the appointed day, twelve years ago that very identical day. March sth. The church was opened at 2.30, and a company of stewards were in anxious smiling readiness Ito welcome men, and enrol them if they desired. For twenty minutes they came but very tardily and scantily, so that at ten minutes to three the church held so few that their anxious, eager hearts sank almost into their shoes! It really looked as if the P.S.A.—despite all our toil and pains and outlay—was not going to 'catch on!" But working men were so trained by habit to being on the spot just at the "nick of time," and never any too soon, that they followed their week-day habit on that Sunday afternoon. About seven minutes to three they began to enter in rapidly growing numbers, 'until at the exact stroke of three—as the Chairman (Mr. Robinson, ofithe Great George-street, Liverpool, P.S.A.) rose to give out the opening hymn—he faced an audience of 700jmen—which in a few more minutes increased to 800. The actual enrolment of that day was 300. He wendered how many of that 300 were present in that meeting. Of course they knew that very many of them had removed from town. Also not a few of them had been translated to the " glorious home on high." Look- ing at the P.S.A. report for 1894, he noticed among the list of officers and committee the names of E, Jewkes, J. Irish, J. S. Garland, E. Pickavance, all of whom were now on the "other side of the river." And from the ranks of the membership had passed men too numerous to mention, many of whom blessed God that ever they joined the society, for they regarded it as having been the means to them of the greatest moral social and spiritual good. But they knew, also, that a. goodly number of that first 300 still were in town, although they had ceased membership. If there were any of them present it would afford them the greatest possible delight to again enrol them as co-operators in that good enterprise. He invited them back, and hoped they would accept their invitation. So much, then, kr that first historic day. But their second Sunday increased the roll very considerably, and so did all , following Sundays, until at the end of the first three I months they had a membership of 594; at the ' end of six momths, of 700; at the end of twelve months 1,150, with an average weekly attendance of 6301 One thousand eight hundred and ninety-three and four was the year of the coal strike. Many of the men and their families were in great extremities, as the works were stopped. The P.S.A. Benevolent Fund then became a great means of assistance, and, under the energetic guidance of Mr. Hickman, many of the P.S.A. families were greatly helped. Their second year's roll was yet larger than the first; being 1,203, with an average attendance of 829. During those two years no less than 480 certificates of merit were given for regular attendance. Other figures were given,and the President said that, looking back over the 12 years,they could record a membership of nearly 9,C...1 men. Among these members nearly 11,030 books had been received as prizes. And the members' roll foithe yearjust com- pleted reached the total of 590—xvh h was larger than it had been for four years. For 'the last six months ' almost each Sunday had welcomed additions to the ranks What about the asults—the moral and spiritual results ? Well, they were more and far streater, and more wisely -spread, than they could possibly think or say ! It was certain that men had been reclaimed from evil habits and evil lives ; that homes had been transformed, and made happy and peaceful; that wives had blessed the day when husbands were brought to the P.S.A. ; that parents had rejoiced that their sons came in there; drunkards had becoma sober; swearers had learned a purer speech ; and Sabbath breakers had learned a better use of Sunday. (Applause.) In conclusion the rev, gentleman said they must be more than ever a progressive P.S.A. ; and to be progressive they roust be aggressive. He wanted to see the church filled on Sunday afternoons ; that was a sight he longed to see before his time came to its end. Let every marine a recruiting sergeant and enlist men for the Lord's army with some of that persistence with which men wore enlisted in times of war for the King's army. If they would do so they would be able before the next anniversary to double the number of P.S.A. members. _ .</p>
        <p>A special feature of the proceedings was the singing of the Warrington Male Voice Choir which gave several items in an efficient manner. Mr. T. F. Wallington presided at the organ,and Mr. R. Atkinson and Mr. B. Farrar also took part in the service. NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
      </div>
