        <p>Warrington v. Morecambe. :ravelling forward with strong reverse rotation, the The first round draw favoured most of the leading friction of the cloth has destroyed this re- clubs with easy fixtures, and Warrington were among verse rotation, and for one fleeting moment the those thus fortunately placed. Morecambe, the wooden pall has glided along the cloth with no rotation spoonists of the second division were rightly looked it all (neither backward nor forward), and then upon as very small fryand not likely to offer any serious' korward rctation has begun to be developed, but opposition. It was quite surprising to find 4,000 people just at this moment the object ball has been assembled to witness the match, which was played in struck and the cue ball stops dead, or perhaps beautiful weather. Warrington had an experimental rolls on just an inch or two. side, Thomas making his-debut as a half back, with In a like manner shoutd the cue ball at the every intention of making a brave show. Mr. Cross of p contact with an object ball not be Preston, was referee and the teams lined out as moment of travelling with perfect' rotation, the cue ball wil follows: nall half ball strokes be deflected considerably Warrington :—Hallam, full back; Fish, Berry, Dickenson and Harris three-quarters; Thomas and J. more than is the case with normal half ball Davies, half backs ; Preston, Shugars, Jolley, Belton, strokes. Naylor, Cook, Harmer and Swift, forwards. Morecambe :—Fnll back, Levi Jackson; threequarters, T. A. Lamb, D. Bell (captain), W. Longmire and E. Flaxington; halves S. Thornton, and W. Threlfall ; forwards, T. Shaw;G. Halton, F. Jackson, Toulmin, Balmforth, Woodhouse, Cornthwaite and Hadwen. The opening staues_ were evenly contested, Warring- ton not displaying any great eagerness to open the scoring, while Morecambe were very energetic. They tackled excellently and kept play for some time near the centre. Eventually a series of penalties took them ' right to the home line, where they sustained somewhat of a shock. Warrington got possession, J. Davies, Hallam, and Harris coming clear away from the scrum, and the wing man outpaced everyone to score in good position. Berry placed an easy goal. The homesters easily held the upper hand, but showed indifferent com- bination. Fish ran in from touch and dropped a clever goal, while Berry placed a penalty goal, and at half- time Morecambe were nine points in arrears. Warrington re-started in a more business-like style and at once began to put on points. Thomas and J. Davies opened out the game, and fine combination among the backs led to tries being scored by Dickinson (two) Harris, Berry, Cook, and goals by Berry (two) and Preston, one. Morcambe never got into the War- rington half and were quite outclassed, but they never- theless played with plenty of pluck. Levi Jackson at full back was very reliable, while Bell and Flaxington also defended well. A curious feature of the game was that Fish never got a try, the ball rarely coming in his direction, while when it did he was collared by T. Lamb. The final score was: Warrington, 6 goals, 6 tries (30 points); Morecambe, nil.</p>
        <p>DIAGRAM 67.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. CHESHIRE COUNTY. THE J. RICHARDSON MEMORIAL.</p>
        <p>The match in aid of the widow of the late Joe Richardson of Runcorn drew a crowd of about 4,000 people to the Wilderspool ground on Tuesday evening, when Warrington opposed a strong team representing Cheshire County. There were several departures from the original selections on the Cheshire side, and the more notable absentees included Houghton (Runcorn), Dun bavin ( Bradford), and Johnson (Leigh). Warrington Played the same team as on Saturday. The Cheshire men were as follows : _ _</p>
        <p>bi Position A: -28 inches frOrir the side ci # inches from the baulk cushion. and I, at the back of the D On the central "able. Object white in hot position brom She side cushion ans., hee frr aushion. Red on "</p>
        <p>Position A in Diagram 67 shows a cannon oftet missed by the ordinary player. The angle looks the same and in fact, is practically the same as in any ordinary half-ball stroke with the cue ball farther away from the first: object' ball. Owing, however, to the cue ball being so near the first object ball, the throw-off angle, should the ball be struck at the centre, will be. considerably greater than that of ordinary half-ball strokes. This cannon hould be played by striking the cue ball well above the centre and taking the object ball half-ball. In this way forward rotation is given to the cue ball, and .iis prevents it being thrown off at a wider angle than the normal one. It is far better to play cannons in this manner when they can be got by a etroke than to play them is a somewhat thin shot, for when playing to match the object ball rather lese than half-ball, it is very east, to make the mistake of catching it too thin and so miss the cannon the other way. With the two object balls in the position shown on the diagram (or in any similar posi- tion) a player should if in hand place his ball as far away from the first object ball as possible (as shown in Position It), instead f placing it near it. In this way the cue ball is given time to develop perfect rotation, thus ensuring the throw-off angle being the natural half-ball angle. Also, by putting as much space as possible lk- tween the cue ball and object ball, the correct angle for the stroke is more easily gauged, and as the cue ball has such a long way to travel after striking the first object ball it can be easily understood that not much latitude for error exists.</p>
        <p>_ Cheshire County: Harrison, (Runcorn), full back; Watkins (Warrington), J. Butterworth, H. Price and Padbury (Runcorn), three-quarters; Jolley (Run- corn), and H. Dunbavin (Leigh) half backs ; S. Walker, A. Moulton, A. Richardson Farmer, Dixon (Runcorn), McDonald (Widnes), Jone4Birkenhead), I. Tomlinson and Wood (Runcorn), forwards. Referee : Mr. J. H. Smith (Widnes). A high wind prevailed, and Warrington winning the toss soon took advantage of the elements to attack hotly. Jolley and Harrison defended strongly, but ten minutes after the commencement Preston kicked a clever goal for Warrington. The latter generally had the upper hand, and once Dickenson got clean aaay only to give Berry a forward pass which spoiled a beautiful opening. Cheshire retaliated, Dunbavin running trickily to the Warrington line where Thomas brought him down. Half-time arrived with Warrington leading by a goal to nil.</p>
        <p>Turning round, although playing against the wind,the Warsington forwards performed excellently, and capital rushes gradually ended in Elarrisons charge being assailed. Frequent penalties against Thomas and Davies negatived and the game was well spent before Dicken- son, Berry, and Fish indulged in a passing bout which ended in the last named scoring at the corner. This Was the only tangible record of this half, and the final score read as follows :—Warrington, one goal, one try (five points) ; Cheshire County, nil.</p>
