      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>WAY TO SCREW.</head>
        <p>THE WAY TO SCREW.</p>
        <p>As the merest novice at billiards knows, the way to impart screw on reverse rotation to the cue ball is by striking the ball low down, but many ordinary players, when playing screw' backs, always quickly draw back the cue after striking the ball, as though the sharp backward movement of the cue could in some mysterious way imparts a similar action to the Cue ball. Under exceptional circumstances, which will be referred to later on, the cue has to be pulled hack quickly, but generally speaking not only should the cue not be pulled back when playing screw backS, but it should instead actually go *lean through the ball, that is to say it should follow on some considerable distance after strik- ing the ball just in the same way that the sus follows on when playi.ng. run-through strokes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>DIAGRAM 68.</head>
        <p>DIAGRAM 68.</p>
        <p>The —orsected lih should go clean throuL lag screw-back strokes.</p>
        <p>how the cue Al when play-,</p>
        <p>Diagram 68 shows very clearly the correct action a the cue when playing screw-backs that rlsquire a considerable amount of strength, and so screw-backs can be played without strength unless the three balls are all very near to each ether. _ _ .</p>
        <p>' Beginners always find a great difficulty in learning to play. screw-backs, and many players who can regularly make their thirties and forties often enough fail dismally at a screw-back. The stroke certainly requires considerable practice if a fair mastery of it is to be obtained, but there is really nothing very difficult about it. Clean, in- cisive hitting below the centre, letting the cu., go' right through the ball, muet'impart reverse rota- tion to the ball, the amount depending to a very great extent on the strength of the stroke, and this reverse rotation must cause the cue ball to secoil after striking a near object ball full or nearly full in the fare. . The beet way to practise screwing is to place two balls-it varying distances; begian.ing with the balls about six inches apart and increasing the distance between them by an inch or two at tim" magram 69 shows a series of such strokes: The one ball should be made to come back well i-to baulk each time, and in a straight-or ne..,rly straight line. 2•The player should take -Saxe to get full on to the object ball and to drive his cue Steen through the cue ball. The farther the bails are apart the gr,eater the strength at which the stroke should be played. There is no'necessity to strike the cue ball very low down. Half-way between the centre of the ball and the level of the table is low enough. Very low hitting of the cue ball eauses,mis-cues, and beginners are very apt to tear the *loth in this way, especially as they almcst always elevate the butt of the cue. .The cue should be held as horizontally as the low bit- ting of the cue ball will allow, and the tip should be chalked before every stroke.</p>
        <p>into] ines illustrate .gh the cue '</p>
