      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS.</head>
        <p>ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Amateur (Manchester).—Lovejoy is now a profes- sional, and consequently cannot take part in the amateur championships. (Leeds).—Stevenson's break of 802 stands as the record, as Roberts's break of 821 WS not passed by the Billiard Association, owing to the ia,le on which it was made not having been tested pr". .uus to the break. 1 "Screwback " (Soutnamptou),—Possibly the reason that you miss-cue so often when pin) ing screw-back cannons is that you strike your ball too low. Try strik- ing the cue ball only about half-way below the centre. This is as low as necessary. H. E. J. (Cricklewood).—l don't think your argu- ment that ivory balls must be better than crystalates, because all the leading professional matches are played with ivories, is a good one. In the first place many important matches are played with crystalate balls, and it was in a match with these balls against Haverson, last season, that Stevenson made a break of 703, which at the time stood as his record. Prejudice dies hard,!but if you were to make a tour of the billiard rooms of London, I think you would be surprised to find how fast ivory balls are disappearing. In my opinion, in another ten years ivory billiard balls will be ' looked upon almost as curiosities. - One of the articles of agreement between Roberts and Stevenson re their coming great match, stipulates that each player shall pro ride three carefully-selected sets of balls. It is a ' well-known fact that scarcely a professional match with ivory balls takes place, without the balls being changed at least once, owing to one or other of the balls not running true. Sometimes they are changed two or three times during the course of the game, but in the course of the forthcoming big match provision for con- stant changes is made by having a stock of no less than Isix different sets of carefully-selected balls. Not only this, but by the articles of agreement the spot balls are not to have some black substance (generally ebony) inserted into the ivory to mark the spots,but instead are to be marked with two small ink spots: Does this not prove conclusively that the very best ivory balls cannot be relied upon to run perfectly true, and is it not a well- known fact that ivory balls which have been in use for a long time are almost always totally unfit for good I play? What is the case with crystalates? Such a thing as changing a set of balls during a match is quite 'unknown, also there is no such thing as a carefully ; selected set of crystalates; one set is just the same as another, and more than one set is never provided for a match. The set with which Stevenion made his 703 break was just an ordinary set taken from stock. I shall be glad to hear from you again if you can reconcile these facts with your present belief, and in the meantime;you ought to try a few games with crystalates, and then yob</p>
      </div>
