        <p>tundruni " (Blackpool).—Thirty-four points is of course the most that, theoretically, could be made in four consecutive strokes; three eight shots, followed by a ten shot, but of course such scoring could never happen in practice. T once saw a very ordinary player score"2o in three successive strokes, two seven shots and a six shot, but all three strokes were very lucky ones. J. Goldstein ( Yarmouth).—l am not aware of any official rules governing the game of skittle pool, but if there are any you will be able to get them from the Billiard Association, 18, Bouverie-street, London. J. Lewis (Cardiff).—lf you will send me a stamped envelope, I will give you my opinion as to the best make of billiard tables. Constant Reader.—l hope to have Volume II of "The Strokes of the Game" ready early next September. RISO LEVI..</p>
        <p>upon the proceedings at the P,oor Law Conference held on February 21st and 22nd. They pointed out that the following system for the treatment of indoor and out- door poor was advocated by the chairman of the Brad- ford Board of Guardians (1) the removal of children from an atmosphere of adult pauperism and their train- ing in cottage homes or similar institutions; (2) the treatment of aged poor who have lived decent lives apart from the foul-mouthed and ill-mannered inmates of the Workhouse; (3) the separate administration of the Workhouse hospital with a staff of qualified nurses and sufficient medical attendance, and the abolition of pauper service ' • (4) the Work- house to be for the able-bodied what its name implied—a place for work—the work while not repres- sive to be sufficiently deterrent to be a test of destitu- tion. Out-door relief ; thorough investigation by the relieving officers so that out-door relief may only be given to those who will use it for the proper purpose; (2) joint committees of Beards of Guardians, local bodies and charitable agencies to be appointed for dealing with distress due to unemployment so that overlapping may be avoided, pauperism prevented, work found, labour registers kept and all information respect- ing the state of labour obtained. It was the unanimous feeling of the conference that tramps should be dealt with by the State and that persons going from town to town in search of work should be given tickets by the police for admission to Workhouses. The cost of the deputation was .£l2 ins. In moving the adoption of the report, Mr. Antwis said it was suggested at a conference that children travelling with tramps should be taken and educated by the State. It was felt that the only possible way to reduce the number of tramps wae to rescue the children from lives of vagrancy. The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS The Boiler at the Workhouse. The fortnightly meeting of the RunOorn Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on Tuesday when there were present—Messrs.C.R.Norman (presiding), C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw, H. Eves, G. Egerton-Warburton,A. Broome, T. Baxter, ELM. 'Davies, C. E. Linaker, J. H. Davies J. Smith, T. Wright, H. Mawdesley, J. T. Collier, S. Woodward, T. Guest, S. Harrison, G. Pilling, J. L. Jones, E. Bretherton, J. Fryer, J. Spruce, W. M. Morris, J. Douglas. J. W. Temperley, J. Rutter, J. Hallows, R. Billington, C. E. Linaker, jun., J. Mounfleld, T. Antwis, A.G. Guest, G.F. Ashton (clerk), and W. E. Hough (assistaht clerk).</p>
