      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR,</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR,</p>
        <p>--- - NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENT. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases sent their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matters intended for publication must be written on tide only of the paper and sent in, if possible, not ater than Thursday. Mn. C. E. PARKER and Mn. A. D. RATHBONE.—We -have been reluctantly compelled to hold -your letters over owing to the great pressure on our space this week.—ED. "Ex."</p>
        <p>Re-Rachel Barton, Deceased. Sir,—Referring to the lamentable dent bhp drowning of the above-named, concerning which an inquest was held here on the 3rd inst, permit me (having our Asteemed Joroner's sanction) to give the exact text of the note produced to Mr. Brighouse and the jury, who alone had official right or concern in it. The document is an ordinary envelope across which are scribbled the words: I have drowned myself !, I cannot live ! I am So wicked." This and no more. The, last four words are written separately and distinctly from the others; evidently the result of an after-thought. To my mind they convey only a dim consciousness that self-destruc- tion would violate a spiritual law--for Miss Barton was controlled by such considerations in every phase of thought and action. In fact, she often carried this peculiarity to extremes. Personally I have known the girl for years; she was a domestic at the old Sessions House for half a decade. The most of her life since then was spent in the service of a prominent and highly-esteemed townsman whose written description of her to me I endorse most fully. Thus he writes :—"Rachel was a girl of exemplary life, a good and:faithful servant." Which of us will deserve a better epitaph than that sentence? The real cause, lam confident, of the tragedy, waA the poor girl's haunting horror of total blindness. One eye was almost totally obscured, the other was weakening slowly. The heavy dread of the living darkness that comes at noonday bowed and bent the frail sweet mentality until it snapped. -Somewhat of the rest we know—the remainder is with Him Who says " Well done "to even such as Rachel Barton, who wasfa " good and faithful servant." Trusting that you will he so kind as to give this letter publicity in the columns of your valuable paper, I am, air, faithfully yours, LUKE TALBOT, Chief Constable.</p>
        <p>March Bth, 1905. Coancinor Stott and the Next Election. Sir,—Councillor Stott has explained in his letter to the" Examiner" of Saturday last the position which he and the other members of the Independent Labour Party intend to occupy at the coming General Election. "If there is no Labour Candidate in Warrington at the next General Election, I shall have no interest in the Election as far as actual work is concerned, and intend to devote all the time I possibly can to try and secure the return of Mr, J. H. Seddon for Newton Division." If, by any chance, Mr. A. H. Crosfield should be returned without his help he will, no doubt, reserve to himself the right to criticize his actions. It would be far more straightforward if he and his party were to put certain questions to the respective candidates, and then work whole-hearted]y for the one who was prepared to support the measures for the good of the community they repre- sent, rather than adopt • such an absurd dog-in-the- manger policy. It would only be doing locally what the Party will be compelled to do imperially. One can readily understand their anxiety to build up a labour party, but one cannot understand such an attitude as Councillor Stott's. For instance,what would Councillor Stott have to say if at the coming November municipal elections all the Liberals and progressives refused to work for him in his ward because he was a socialist? Again, if there was a fire in Warrington would Councillor Stott refuse to assist in putting it out because he was a friend of the Captain of the Newton Division Fire Brigade and had promised that gentleman his support ?His sympathy no doubt, would be extended to the sufferers. But of what use is that without his help? There is such a thing as co-operation. What would Mr. Seddon say if the Liberals refused their help to him in his fight at Newton? Dropping metaphor ; the issue at the next election is too serious to be trifled with for the sake of party tactics. And why? We were wont to pride ourselves on being an eminently practical, energetic people, making common sense our guide, and always carefully adapting the means employed to the ends proposed. Now is not the time to exploit a party for the sake of numerical gain but we should be eager to work for any party which will help to put an end to such miserable and contemptible government. By refusing to help one side they give support to the other. As to the intended reflection upon the Rev. H. Minnie°, well, it is hardly worth noticing. It may be his fun! But, I should like to say here that it appears to me that because the rev, gentleman is honest enough to abstain from flattering the Independent Labour Party by prophesying smooth things and glossing over mean and petty differences, and is desirous of promoting the social as well as the spiritual welfare of the people, some of the leaders of the I.L.P. leave no stone unturned in their hurry to denounce him. He is far too clever and earnest for them, and, naturally, of course, they are jealous—nothing more, nothing less. We do not blame them but we do pity them in their littleness. When their minds become sufficiently broad to believe that there are others quite as desirous of promoting the welfare of the workers as they are, and will allow the fresh air of fair- ness to enter their political lives, some lasting good may then be done. Until then, well, they must, of ,course, resort to other devices.—Yours truly. NUNQVALM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Barremman, lloylake. "Wire Drawing in Warrington." Honour to Whom Honour is Due.</head>
        <p>Barremman, lloylake. "Wire Drawing in Warrington." Honour to Whom Honour is Due.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield's Last Word. An Archaic Ship and Its Crew. The following is a continuation of the letter from Mr. A. H. Crosfield in last week's paper. Owing to pressure on our space we were unable to give the whole of Mr. Crosfield's argument in reply to " Observer." The letter thus continues "I now come to " Observer's " great discovery that there is actually one grocer's shop in Warrington where American soap is to the fore. I may re-assure him. It is very kind of him to be solicitous for the future of Joseph Crosfield and Sons, and the prospects of our workmen. If only the Tariff Reform League will cease to spread damaging reports in foreign markets, reports directly calculated to weaken prestige and damage trade, if this ill starred league will just keep quiet I can assure " Observer " we shall do very well indeed. Ours is not a decaying industry, neither are foreign competitors by any means cutting us out. One American firm, well up-to-date in advertisine, did manage a few years since to make head- way in the English maiket. But what is the total per- centage, not only of American soap, but of all foreign soap shipped to England. I am not able to give exact figures, but I should probably over-estimate the total soap imported from abroad if I put it down at 3 per cent of the home production the amount America sends us is a mere bagatelle, and although attempts have fre- quently been made to capture the British market for soap they have utterly failed, and always will fail as long as our cost of production, thanks to Free Trade remains below that of America, Germany, France and other countries. "One swallow" Mr. Editor, "does nat make a summer," and yeu may rest assured that American soap to the fore in one shop will not spoil the Warrington market, or a pitiful 3 per cent, of importation the British market. Yon no doubt object to what lam estimating at 3 per cent. as "dumped" manufacturers. But I am impressed by the remark a witty friend of mine made the other day, he said " ' Dumping ' is like matrimony ; it's a useful irritatant." So much for the home market, but what about foreign markets. Why is it that in countries such as China, India, Straits Settlements, South Africa, West Africa, the Canary Islands, and Egypt, which are some of the chief markets to which English non-advertised soap is shipped—why in these places have we been able to beat American competition hollow, while Germany is almost out of running. Freight facilities will not explain this, because in the West Indies where freights are lower from the United States than from Liverpool, England does the bulk of the trade in such markets as•Jamstica, Trinidad, Baradoes, Demerara, itc., where we compete on even terms with America; and this in spite of prolonged efforts by manu- facturers in New Orleans and elsewhere to secure the trade. For the future the outlook is even brighter. The consumption of soap is a measure of civilisation, and as these semi-civilized countries advance they will take more and more of what we manufacture at Bank Quay and in Liverpool. And so in spite of the shop window in Bridge-street there is not the slightest prob- ability of diminished employment at either of our works. England gets raw materials free of duty and can build her mills relatively cheaply, and thanks to free food living is cheaper here than in the States, so that both in home and foreign neutral markets we can beat our rivals in the soap trade. " Observer" cannot take exception to this reply as regards the home trade; but if he argues that the ship- ments of English soap abroad are relatively a small factor in our over-the-sea trade, then let us take the largest branch of it the great cotton industry of Lancashire, which is second only to agriculture in the number of people for whom it finds employment. How stand the fortunes of British cotton in competition with French or German cotton in a neutral market? Will "Observer" consider the case of India for a moment as an example. He will find that while our total exports to India reached £3,1,000,000 in 1903, Germany and Belgium could only muster a poor total of £1,000,000. If he looks into that £34,000,000, he will find it includes many other things besides cotton, but if he seeks the reason for Britain's enormous preponderance in the cotton trade he will find that in France (I have not the figures for Germany) it costs just about twice as much to build a , mill as it costs in Lancashire. The protectionist manu- facturer is handicapped from the start. He can do business behind a tariff wall in his own country but he Liberal majority is utterly worsted when he ventures into the open. Let ; Conservative majority him look into the conditions of labour. He will find that labour is cheap in Lancsshire. Why? Not because</p>
        <p>Sir,—To all Warringtonians the report published in a recent issue of your paper on "The Rylands Centenary" furnishes most interesting reading. There is not a man amongst us who is not proud of the justly earned and world wide reputation for excellence of manufacture and honourable .business conduct which belongs to ! Rylands Bros. Ltd. I cannot however allow the claim put forward in the following sent- ence to pass unchallenged, viz : "A generation later in the year 1805 Mr. John Rylands of this firm 1 which was then known as John Rylands kit Son, intro- duced the manufacture of wire into Warrington." To ' do so would be to tacitly assent to what I feel sure is an unintentional injustice to the memory of a man who, in his day and generation lived a useful and honoured life amongst us, and to whom and to whose sons and grandsons our community owes much gratitude for sub- stantial, public services, and whose family name for three generations had stood and to-day stands equally with the names of the best of our townsmen for both commercial and personal integrity. In the year 1799 Nathaniel Greening came to War- rington from The Tintern Abbey Wire Mills. He was only 19 to 20 years of age at the time but he had a thoroughly sound and practical knowledge of wire drawing, and he !commenced in business as a wiredrawer on his own account in the year mentioned in premises in Bridge-street, somewhere near the Lion Hotel. A few years later, about 1807 Mr. Nathaniel Greening took into partnership Mr. John Rylands (the grandfather of the present Mr. W. P. Rylands) who was at that time a manufacturer of sail cloth, cart sheets and similar fabrics, and the business of wire-drawing was carried on by Nathaniel Greening and John Rylands under the style of Messrs. N. Greening and Co. The firm took larger premises, which formed part of the Bridge-street Foundry on the side of the river and remained there for some little time, afterwards remov- ing to an empty cotton or silk mill at the top of Ch rch-street, which previously belonged to the Percival family. The same firm began the wire weaving trade about this time in Cloth Hall-Yard behind Butter- market-street and off Bank-street in premises belong- ing to one of the partners—Mr. John Rylands. Mr. Nathaniel Greening then lived in Buttermarket- street where, in a large room which extended over the next house he established a Museum of Curiosities. In Baines' History of Lancashire kpublished in the year 1825) at page 587, the following interesting reference to Mr. Greening may be found. "An institution originating in the monthly meeting of a few literary and scientific gentlemen in the town and neighbourhood of Warrington was formed in 1812 for the cultivation of Science, Literature and ;the Arts. The meetings took place in the large room of the Museum of Natural History then recently established in Warrington by Mr. Greening, and lectures were delivered every alternate Friday." In passing it is worthy of notice how closely and honourably identified with the progress and enrichment of our Museum, the name of Greening has been, not only in the person of Nathaniel Greening but also in that of his son—the late Mr. Noah Greening—and of his grandson Mr. Lini:mus Greening. The dual business of wire-drawing and wire-weaving was carried on, the former in Church-street and the latter at Cloth Hall Yard premises' by N. Greening Co. (Nathaniel Greening and John I;ylands) for several years. The name of the firm was subsequently changed to that of Rylands and Greening and in 1843 the partnership between Nathaniel Greening and John Rylands was dissolved. Mr. Rylands' three sons, John, Thomas Glazebrook aid Peter continued the wire- drawing business taking the name of Messrs. Rylands Bros. and Mr. Greening with his three sons Timothy, Noah and John carried on the Wire Weavirr, industry under the name of Messrs. N. Greening and Sons, first in a warehouse in Winwick- street from 1843 to 1847, then for many years in the building since occupied by the Farmers Stores in Bewsey- street, afterwards in Crown-street in the premises formerly known as Mottram's-yard, also in Scotland- road (Kitchens' old Weighing Machine Works) and to- day they occupy 26 acres of land in Bewsey-road and Lovely-lane upon which they have erected some of the finest workshops (if not the finest of the kind) in the country, equipped with the most modern machinery, finding employment for a largely increased number of workpeople and are able to successfully compete with all comers for the world's requirements in every de- partment of wire weaving and metal perforating. It is also worthy of note that Messrs. Greening and Sons, Limited to this day (as they have done continuously since 1799) " draw" the wire used in wire weaving on their own premises. All that has been said and most truthfully said as to the enterprise, excellence of work and honourable methods in business conduct which have characterised the lives and built up the untarnished reputation of the Rylands' family, apply with equal accuracy and justice Ito every member of the firm of N. Greening and Sons, Ltd., to its founder, Nathaniel Greening, (who was the , pioneer of the Wire Weaving Industry in Warrington) , to his sons Timothy,Noah and John and to his grandsons Nathaniel and Linnkeus, who we trust will for many years continue to personally control with increasing , success a concern of which they and all Warrington , may well be proud. " HISTORIOUS."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB v. CONSERVATIVE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The return match between teams of 14 aside repre- senting the Liberal and Conservative Clubs was played at the Liberal Club, Bewsey-street, on Thursday even- ing. A week ago the Conservatives gained a majority of 475 at Sankey-street, and a big effort was made by the Liberals to wipe off the defl^,it. At one time, when nearly 300 ahead, the Liberals looked like accomplish- ing the feat, but some of the concluding games were lost by largt margins. The close of the match left the Liberals with a majority of 103 points on the right, the Conservatives retaining an advantage on the home and homes games of 3'12 points. _</p>
        <p>' LIBERALS. CONSERVATIVES. H. V. Mike' 2OO S. Cooper R. Woods E. Pierpoint J. McKenzie B. Smith 2OO T. Sangster J. Jennings 195 J. Devenport ow J. Davies 2OO H. Collin G.Darton 2OO R. Straw S.J. Beaman ' 230 G. H. Smith J. Halliwell 2OO J. Keatinge E. J. Mitchell W. Woods (Hanover at.) 2:00 H. Hughes 129 E. Wilson J. Jeffrey 97 W. Podmore 2560 2466 2460</p>
        <p>103 on both matches 372.</p>
