        <p>•</p>
        <p>- I</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 11, 1905.</p>
        <p>it earns lower wages. On the contrary it is very much better paid. But it enjoys a higher standard of phy- THE FREE CHURCHES. sical comfort and is therefore more efficient and more skilful. It is this alliance of efficient labour based on cheap food and free capital based on the cheapness of all the materials of manufacture which gives to English MASS MEETING IN THE PARR HALL. trade its superiority in every neutral market. We are by far the greatest exporting power in the world. Man for man, taking the triennial period 1900-2 England Addresses by the Revs. F. B. Meyer, exports £0 17s. 2d., Germany £4 Is., and the United States £3 16s. 6d. We are, actually increasing our lead, ; Dr. D. Brook and Frank Ballard. for while Great Britain and the United States have each added 13s. to their exports per head of the popula- tion' Germany has added only 14s. lid. THE CHURCHES' OPPORTUNITY. Before I bid "Observer " farewell—for this letter , inds the correspondence so far as I am concerned—let I me conclude with a little tribute of admiration to his I Members of the Free Churches mustered in strong staunchness in a losing cause. I admire his courage, I fiscal contro- force at the Parr Hall on Thursday evening on the remember the brisk days when the great occasion of a mass meeting promoted in connection versy began. The ship of "Fiscal Reform," a somewhat , archaic vessel, built on the three decker model, and with the National Council of Evangelical Free fresh painted after her long repose in a museum of , Churches which has been meeting in Manchester this nautical curiosities set sail with the tornado of the , week. The local Council had been fortunate in scour- " raging tearing propaganda" to bear her on. There ing the services of such powerful speakers as the were a few heroic figures on her deck. I could discern the features of some of them.. There was Sir Howard 1 Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., of London, the Rev. David Brook, MA., D.C.L. (Oxon), of Southport (suc- Vincent on the poop, Mr. Pierpoint on the Quarter , Deck, and" Observer " somewhere in the rigging nail- cessor to the Rev. Silas Hocking) and the Rev. ing the flag of Protection to the mast. At the helm I , Frank Ballard, of Newcastle on-Tyne, and their recognised the weather wise and sagacious features of i addresses on various phases of Free Church work my old friend Mr. John White looking somewhat were listened to with great interest and pleasure. dubiously at the horizon. (And may I interrupt this . The chair was occupied by Mr. John E. Sears, L.C.C., little allegory to say how sincerely glad we all are to i hear Mr. White is again in good health and ready for , of London, and in addition to the speakers already mentioned there were present on the platform :—Mr. the fray. I hope he may continue in good fighting trim , i C. E. Parker (president of the Free Church Council), for many an election to come) On the deck of the vessel I am describing the cargo i the Revs. G. H. Carnson, J. Manning, J. Yonge, lay piled in profusion and confusion. There were pearl 1 Arthur Back, of Lyrnm, F. Vaughan, J. S. Hughes, H. buttons, and Gainsborough chairs with bottoms "Made I Wall, J. L. Milne, C. L. Stowe, and J. F. Matthews, in Russia," and many other things strewn about in Mr. R. Bridge, Councillor L. Ellison Captain Searles admired disorder. But what struck me as strange in this vessel were the internal decorations, which were (Salvation Army), Mr. E. Wynne 'Jones, Mr. W. i Beswick, and Mr. R. Pierce Williams. There were quite to exhibition of arts and crafts. Pictures I saw of shadowy pastures cropped by phantom cows, and resent in the audience :—Alderman Bennett, Mr. E. misty studies on the fiscal question lit by mellifluous I - G 31 Guest, r. Councillor roil,. , an B 0.11 d the Rev. C. moonshine, and fancy-illuminated finance. Only to i Harvey Cook. mention one or two more samples, I noticed a harrow- I In a few introductory remarks, the Rev. J. F. ing picture of British workers in African goldmines I MAT'PHEWS announced that the Free Church Hall at suffering great hardshiips on wages of a pounda day,and 1 Winwick would be opened on Tuesday, April 4th. He a life sized figure of a mine owner moving British specu- I extended a hearty welcome to the Chairman and lators to tears and indignation by his sufferings—and 1 speakers. moving the wires for the purposed also caught a glimpse THE CHAIRMAN AND HOPEFUL SIGNS. of some curious studies (or experiments in Yellow and THE LESSON OF THE WELSH REVIVAL. White) though there are many of these works of art I Th —ir The t, AIRMaN said that meeting was representative could not sea because the fiscal fog which obscured of many denominations united in one faith. The great them. Then the weather began to freshen, and the unity among the Free Churches was to him one of the waves began to rise. A flood from Bury was the ant most remarkable features of the Free Church to beat upon the vessel's decks, and then fast and movement and one of the most hopeful signs furious the breakers came from all the quarters at once, of the times. The Free Churches were to-day Orkney and Woolwich, Stalybridge and Bute. I noticed minimising their differences of organization and creed, that Mr. Pierpoint went below and Mr. White gazed and were emphaising the great fundamental principals anxiously at the rocks ahead. Looking a little into the .on which they were agreed and on which they stood. future (can just discern" Observer" and Sir Howard All denominations of the Free Churches were joining Vincent still at their posts, stiff and unbending in an hands in aggressive spiritual and social work. The attitude they have learned from Sir Edward Poynter's Church of Christ had too long lost sight of the social Roman soldier in the famous picture of Pompeii entitled side of Christian work. The fact was that " Faithful unto death." lam proud to think they will THE CHURCH HAD BEEN ASLEEP go down with colours flying.--I am, Sir, yours &amp;c., for eighteen centuries and was only just beginning to A. H. CROSFIELD. awaken to its responsibilities and opportunities and to exert its true influence onthe life a the nation. They were also united for defensive purposes, to defend and consolidate their civil and religious rights and liberties and especially to secure a reversal of the iniquitous Education Act, in connection with the latter question their position was a clear one. They had nothing to compromise, they were claiming no privilege for them- selves, but their claim was equal rights. (Applause). They wanted to sae a truly national system of educa- tion under public control. They wanted to see all sectarian teaching excluded from school hours. (Hea?, hear.) They protested against any sectarian teaching being given to the children with the authority of the State behind it, and they must seek as far as it was possible to render PROSELYTISING IN THE PUBLIC SCHOOLS of England impossible. (Applause). It seemed to him that the Free Churches to-day were taking their stand in order to secure to the people of the nation a true representation of the Church of Christ. There were two views of the Church of Christ. There was the sacerdotal or Roman view that the church was an ecclesiastical organization and that authority resided in the Bishops and Priests. It was an exclusive claim for their church and they sought to shut out Free Churches. The position of Free Churchmen was that the Church of Christ was a spiritual body and belonged to all those who were true followers of Christ,by whatever name they were called. If this view were held universally, if there was the recognition by Christians of members of other churches there would be an end to strife. Was not God giving them a great lesson to-day in Wales. (Applause). There had been a wonderful revival ; the whole character of the Principality? was changed in a few weeks. It was not changed by the influence of the church claiming the authority but as the result of the efforts of the spiritually-minded men, humble men, almost uneducated men who were outside the pale of that church. It seemed to him that God was teaching this nation a great lesson at a most opportune time. It was</p>
