        <p>a sub-mission to the ideal of Jestis Ch-rist, it seemed to him ho must have more optimism than sense. He made no apology for</p>
        <p>usua, _ling in take over the duties until the appointment had been com- firmed by the Council.fle thought that was a ridiculous piece of jobbery and he hoped the Council would do the right thing without humbug. (Hear, hear.) Councillor CLEGG said he felt it his duty to second the amendment. It was generally known throughout the town that the appointment was decided upon long before the gentlemen mentioned appeared before the committee. IR fact, he had been told that the com- mittee had decided definitely to appoint Mr. Williams, whoever came against him. (Cries of "Nonsense.") Nonsense or no nonsense, that was what he had been told. A VOICE: You may have been told but it is not true. The MAYOR: That would not be evidence in a Court of law. Councillor CLEGG said he made it his duty after the appointment was made to go through the applications and to examine the testimonials. Having done so, he was of the opinion that the appointment had not been made in the best interests of the Corporation. Councillor ROBERTS said that when he proposed the apointment of Mr. Williams at the committee meeting he thought he was the best man, and he was still of that opinion, although he must admit that Mr. Williams had been painted as black that night as he possibly could be. (Hear, hear, and No, no.) Why, he could not understand, but he had a very good idea. He (Councillor Roberts)had the pleasure of meeting Mr. Walker,who gave Mr. Williams a testimonial two years ago, and that gentleman assured him that the testimonial was as applicable to-day as when it was written. In fact, if he (Mr. Walker) had to give him another one, it would be better. Mr. Walker added that he knew Mr. Williams's qualifications, he understood his work, and the Corporation would do well to engage him. He considered that was quite equal to any other testi- monials which the other candidates presented. Mr. Williams had also a testimonial from ' Messrs. Owen, and surely a testimonial from them was good enough to put before any Council. Councillor TINSION : They only say Mr. Williams is a stone mason. Councillor Rosgras said he must admit there was not much between Messrs. Williams and Wareing,but he considered Mr. Williams was the better man. When- ever he thought one applicant was equal to another he would give his vote in favour of the local man. (Hear, hear.) That, he considered, ought to be practically a Standing Order of the Council. He hoped there would be a majority in favour of Mr. Williams. Councillor STOTT supported the amendment. He was very sorry to have to take that course, but he did so on a matter of principle, because he personally considered the appointment a most glaring piece of favouritism. If they allowed that to pass, what guarantee had they that they were going to have the most suitable men selected for higher appointments ?—None whatever. In regard to Councillor Roberts's remarks, he would point out that Mr. Walker refused to give Mr. Williams a testimonial on the present occasion, and the one written two years ago was presented. Mr. Walker said: "I have given one to Brownbill. He is the more suit- able man for the appointment. He has had experience in the kind of work he will have to undertake if he gets the appointment." Ho (Councillor Stott) had nothing to say against Mr. Williams personally and he was very sorry things had been said in the way they had been. He did not know Mr. Williams, but he had made inquiries and he believed he was an excellent stone mason and a man of very good character ; in fact there was everything good to be said about him, except that he was not qualified for the appointment for which he had been selected by a majority of the committee. Having regard to the particulars given by Councillor Tinnion,he thought the committee should have had no hesitation wh tever hi selecting Waring. He was of the same opinion as Councillor Roberts—that when qualifications were equal• a local man should be selected. But when men who lived outside the borough were much better qualified it was an entirely wrong principle to favour local appointments. If every other Corporation took that standpoint, what chance would our officials have had of obtaining such I excellent appointments as several of them had gained recently? The committee had acted very wrongly indeed and he did not think qualifications influenced the majority of members. He must protest against any outside influence being brought to bear in regard to any Corporation appointment. If that principle were adopted by the Corporation any man, however qualified, who held his (Councillor Stott's) views politically and socially would not have the slightest possible chance of obtaining Corporation positions. Councillor WRIGHT supported the amendment. Councillor Roberts had asserted that there was practi- cally no difference between the respective qualifications of Messrs. Wareing and Williams. It should be borne in mind that whereas Mr. Williams had had practically no experience in roa.dmaking, Mr. Wareing had held an assistant foremanship at Southport for Vmrteen years, a general foremanship at Litherland for three years, and a highways surveyorship at Bradford for the past eighteen months. How any gentleman could say that a man who had had no experience at all. in roadmaking could beat an applicant of that discription, he coul i d not possibly tell. To his mind t was a piece of political trickery. (Cries ot "Oh " and "Order.") The M.tyort : Come, come. You have been very careful up to now. Councillor WRIGHT : It appears that way to me, at any rate. Continuing he said Mr. Williams's applica- tion had been carried through from beginning to end when similar ones had been cast out, just because Mr. Williams happened to be a Warrington man and the</p>
