        <p>Let them disabuse their minds about any outside feeling having been introduced. Councillor Tinnion read the testimonials given to Mr. Williams by Mr." Walker who stated he was a practical mason and had been employed in the masonry department of the Cheshire Lines Com- mittee for six and a 1-.111 0,1 work of a varied kind. Mr. Williams, said CuwiilurTinnion,was certainly an intelligent man. As far as his (the speaker's) memory went, all bricksetters and masons were struck out but Mr. Williams with similar qnalifications was left in. As to the questions themselves he was not responsible for them as they were submitted by the Borough Surveyor. As to the reference to betting, he did say jocularly that his would lay ten to one, bar one. (Laughter). He might say that the Council's macadam road foreman had just died, but owing to changed con- ditions now,---the Tramways Committee having taken over the repair of some thorougfares,—they would nott need to appoint anyone in his place. He would sugges as a way out of the present difficulty tht t they should refer the whole matter back to the committee and see if they could appoint someone capable of fulfilling this latter office and the position which they were discuss- ing. This course met with the approval of the majority and on the amendment being put to the meeting it was carried. __</p>
        <p>Councillor Peacock had given notice of the following motions:—That Richard Wareing, of Bradford, be appointed ro id foreman vice Sanderson resigned. That Edward Brownbill, of Warrington, be appointed road foreman vice Sanderson resigned. He explained that his reason for having two motions was that failing the selection of Wareing he desired the best local applicant to receive the appointment. Councillor Peacock at first desired to press the motions but Councillor Tinnion objected to the Council being asked to over-ride the committee. Councillor PEACOCK said he had heard it was the intention of the committee to again propose Mr. Williams. (Cries of "No.") Eventually the motions were withdrawn and the matter therefore rests with the committee.</p>
        <p>Councillor 'WILKINSON; Their intentions were good till they came to the letter "W." Alderman SMETHURST said they all regretted the spirit which had been introduced into the discussion. He flatly denied that he knew of any arrangement for appointing Mr. Williams. Councillor Ron Eras : There was no arrangement. Alderman SMETHURST said he had not seen Mr. Williams himself or anyone representing him. He believed the committee made the appointment in the same manner as other appointments were made. In his opinion Mr. Williams was the best man. Considering the number of questions asked the appointment might have been worth £l,OOO a year. As for the contemptible insinuation that the matter was settled in advance, he denied it before the God who made him. Councillor EVANS expressed regret that politics had been introduced; it should be purely a question of electing the best man. (Hear, hear.) It was evident that the majority of the committee—and he gave them credit for exercising their judgment—did so in the interest of Mr. Williams. Councillor Pemberton had said no line of procedure was laid down, but he (Coun- cillor Evans) emphatically said it was understood that persons who had not the requisite practical knowledge should be struck out. Councillor Pemberton was not in the room when Mr. Wareing was examined, and it was significant that when he arrived the Chairman offered to supply the information given by Mr. Wareing but Councillor Pemberton said" It did not matter." Councillor PEMBERTON replied that he was supplied by Mr. Forshay.- with the answers. Councillor EVANS said other masons and intelligent and capable men were struck out and on that ground alone he should support the amendment. If they had been selecting a stone-mason he should certainly have voted for Mr. Williams, and he had no personal feeling in the matter. Councillor FORSHAW said that when Councillor Pemberton came into the room he supplied him with a copy of the answers taken by himself, and that was no doubt the reason Councillor Pemberton said "It did not matter." He thought from the intelligent manner in which Mr. 'Williams answered the questions that he was equally qualified with anyone else. Alderman HUTCHINSON said the statements that it was well known in advance that Mr. Williams would receive the appointment were erroneous, misleading and unjust. Up to the time of the selection he had not promised directly or indirectly to vote for any candi- date. He was quite satisfied that Mr. Williams would make a good assistant to the Borough Surveyor. Before the meeting began, Councillor Tinnion in a jocular manner said : " I will lay four to one on one." (Laughter.) That probably had given rise to the 11.1111 Ours. Councillor CRAIK : What position shall we be in if the amendment is carried ? Will it throw out Mr. Williams altogether ? The MAYOR: The matter will go back to the com- mittee to reconsider whether Mr. Williams is better than the others or whether someone else shall be appoint d in his place. Councillor MAGINN did not feel it to be gracious on his part to say anything against the qualifications of Mr. Williams, or anyone else, but he was afraid that in this case the friends and supporters of Mr. Williams had made out a bad case for him. (Hear, hear.) His quali- fications were not as good as two of the others, namely, Mr. Browbill and Mr. Wareing. Accusations bad been made that night—truthful or otherwise—that this was a political matter. It was known by the members of the Council and the town, and it would be known to the public that politics— The MAYOR : You might keep politics out. Councillor MAGINN : Tam doing so as fares possible. I have never introduced politics since I became a mem- bbr of the Council, and I have been pleased to notice that no politics have been introduced by others until now, but it has gone forth that this is a question of politics. (" No, no.") It will go forth that it is a political vote. However good Mr. Williams may be, we could have got a better man for £2 a week. Continu- ing, he contended that the testimonial given to Mr. Brownbill by Mr. Walker was far superior to Mr. Williams's for the position for which he was required. Councillor Roberts had taken a great interest in the matter, and appeared to have gone so far as to try to solicit or coax another testimonial in favour of Mr. Williams. Councillor ROBERTS I did not. I only asked him a straightforward question and he gave a straightforward answer. I simply wanted to know whether I was doing right or wrong in supporting Mr. Williams, Councillor MAGINIst said the committee appealed to the Borough Surveyor as'to whom he considered the most suitable man and he decided in favour of Mr.Ware- ing. Yet in face of that recommendation the commit- tee appointed a man who was not experienced as a road- maker. Personally he had nothing against Mr. Williams, but he hoped the excuse for an accusation of political influence would never arise again whilst he was a mem- ber or the Council. Councillor TINNION, in reply, said he had an entirely clear conscience in this matter. He had stated the facts plainly, and he had not given his opinion. fie had not endeavoured to influence the Council. He took no part in the matter at all, and at the close of the com- mittee meeting when the appointment was made, a vote of thanks was accorded to him for his impartiality, but he declined to accept it, as it would have been a vote of censure on his past conduct in the chair. (Laughter.) He expressed his regret that so much heat had been in- troduced into the discussion of this question, which, after all was not a very large matter. Certainly a. good deal of unnecessary heat had been introduced</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>DEALING.WITH FACTS</head>
        <p>DEALING.WITH FACTS</p>
        <p>as he found them and he found them to be of such a kind as to send them away from the Free Church Council meetings, at all events, with sobriety, and he hoped with something else, He would ask them to consider that what they found throughout the country was that after all their missions and work, they had at least five up-gro am people outside their churches for everyone who was inside. He would ask them to look that fact in the face and see whether it wasa cause for superficial optimism or whether they were warranted in singing a hymn as if it were true that very soon everything difficult would be solved and all the evil would melt away into delightful good. The man who was either an optimist or a pessimist to-day was equally foolish and un- warranted. There had of late years been a process of spiritual evolution, and now things were said and done in the names of the Free Churches to-day which would have been simply counsels of quixotic perfection a quarter of a century ago. In spite of everything they were making progress. They had shifted their emphasis in preaching from creed to character, from pietism to socialism. They remembered that the individual was only worth saving,so far as he became himself a part of the saving of others. They were leaving eternity to take care of itself, feeling sure that if they took care of time the Lord whom they served was quite able to take care of eternity. flow was it there were to-day more men and women living without the Christian faith than there had ever been before? He began by saying frankly and absolutely that the present state of affairs was not God's fault. They could see how many things there were outside the churches which were 'fighting against all their ideals of right, truth and hope. But let them look at their own divisions. He did not much mind their Free Church divisions—the individuality of the churches was a precious and helpful thing. It was almost impossible to have Free Church meetings without making reference to the Anglican church. In regard to the Education Act they had sworn Christians manifestly and before all the world at daggers drawn with one another. Was it likely that the Kingdom of Christ would come so long as two great bodies of Christendom were thus engaged in what must almost be characterised as A DEADLY STRUGGLE. In their churches themselves they had men and women with scarcely any definite convictions for which they were prepared to live or die. In every church it was the few who did the work and the many looked on and smiled or frowned. It was unmitigated cant to say that it was not the place of the churches to concern themselves in social work. The reason they hadnot made as much progress as they ought to have done was because they had been ttlrapped up in a psuedo-spirituality, a pietism which had lacked the sympathy of Jesus Christ. No one could possibly deny the magnitude and enormity of the devilry wrought in this land through drink. And yet the Free Churches were not at one en that subject. If the Churches were united they could sweep nearly into the sea the infamies of the drink traffic. Continuing, the speaker said that the thing which, more than any- thing else, was hindering the comin., of Christ in this land was the substitution on the part of some people of their superfluities for sacrifice. (Applause.) In con- nection with self-denial, the speaker alluded to smoking. Considering the state of the young lads and men in our land, was there any doubt as to which side the influence of the Christian men and Churches should be thrown in that matter? In his judgment there was not. Mr. , Ballard concluded by asking his hearers tube deter- mined before God, that by His grace, they would give Him not their superfluities but their sacrifice, and only thus would they hasten on the day for which they were praying when the schemes and systems, kingdoms and republics should fall, and there would be something kindlier, higher, and holier. (Applause). A collection was taken and the Rev. .T. F. A.I.A.TTHEWS announced that the amount realized would more than cover the expenses of the meeting.</p>
        <p>Band of Hope Concert. The Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Schoolroom was filled almost to overflowing on Monday evening when the second concert of the season in connection with the Band of Hope was held. Mrs. Simpson presided and gave a very interesting address, advising all the children present never to start drinking intoxicating liquors. The programme which proved highly enjoy- able consisted of the following :—Duets by the Misses K. Bowyer and E. Lee, songs by the Misses E. Plumpton and May Isherwood and Messrs. Fred Lowe and Archie Cox. The. Penketh Tannery Prize Band Quartet should have given two quartettes, but owing to one of the bandsmen being indisposed, trios were substituted. Recitations were also given by Annie Sankey, May Butler, Maggie Woodward, Lily Welding and Harold Carr. The concluding item was a humorous dialogue entitled "Seaside Lodgings," the following taking part :—The Misses L. Hardy and E Sudlow and Messrs. E. G. R.ickers, W. Wilkes, C. Powney, P. Jackson, Chas. Chadwick, Arthur Jones and Joseph Gough. On the motion of Mr. F. Hardy seconded by Mr. W. Miles, a vote of thanks was passed to all who had taken part in the proceedings. The singing of the Doxology brought a most successful gathering to a close. Mr. E. G. Bickers ably acted as accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
