      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>A- GREAT REBUKE</head>
        <p>A- GREAT REBUKE</p>
        <p>to the attempt which had been made during the latter days of last century and which was being so strenuously driven home to-day, to reassert the authority of the priest and of the Church. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>POLITICS A SECONDARY CONSIDERATION.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. BROOK, of Southport, addressed the meeting.ThelFree Church Counci I,he said,had done a great deal for all the denominations, but it had perhaps done most of all in that department of its services for the smaller denominations. Whilst they utilized their Free Church Councils forl the carrying out of &amp;cer- tain great objects through the medium of politics, do not let them forget that the political side was not the first nor the principal side of their Free Church Council work. (Hear, hear). Do not let their methods be such that their enemies could say there was rather too much of the shriek about Free Church Council work. He was in perfect harmony with the platform of the Free Church Council and it was simply a brotherly word of warning to keep to the front TII E SPIRITUAL SIDE of their work. (Applause.) They would not really succeed in their greet objects of a secondary class un- less they were very ernest in regard to the first objects. As an instance of that, he did not believe the abolition of tests for the universities of Oxford ami. Cambridge, which was a political measure, had strmgthened Non- conformists because a large number of men from homes which were often talked about as their best homes had gone to the University, but the atmosphere of the place had been so thoroughly Anglican that almost uncon- sciously their Nonconformity had been sucked out of them and at the end of their term they had become practically Anglicans or Agnostics, or at all events they had ceased to be anything like strong and robust Nonconformists. He considered that Nonconformity had lost by the opening of the Universities. There was a large number of young men who, but for the abolition of the religious tests, would probably have bscome workers in the Nonconformist churches of the country, or at any rate their names would have always been identified with Nonconformity. All that meant that political reform would not do them much good unless they could keep up a great Nonconformist fire which would remain with the young men when they went to the Universities. Dr. Brook went on to urge a greater zeal for the uplifting of the masses and in the cause of foreign missions. The rich folks who went to the suburbs ought to take a lot of the burden for the foreign mission work upon them, till it really hurt them mid took a substantial part of their means in order to show Christ thatithey really loved Him and cared about His work. (Applause.) THE REV. F. B. 'MEYER'S ADDRESS. WHAT THE CHURCHES HAVE TO FIGHT AGAINST.</p>
        <p>The Rev. F. B. llgvErt, who had a very cordial recep- tion, said he would be much more happy in a midnight march in raiding public-houses or in giving breakfasts to discharged prisoners, and dealing with criminals generally : he thought he must have come from a reclaimed poacher. (Laughter.) There was that strain in his nature that seemed to understand such people. (Laughter.) But to talk to cultivated people of War- rington in that fine hall—well, he could only do that second best. They had certainly come from a great conference,he should think one of the greatest they, had held in the:thirteen years of their history, one the flame of which would be scattered right through the country, for as the express trains that night were bear- ing to every part of our land men and women who had met at the conference, wherever they went they would carry not only the fire of a new enthusiasm for Christ, but the fire of a new enthusiasm for men. (Hear, hear.) He was no party politician : he was sure they would have to live a long time with him before they knew which side he voted on. (Laughter.) But he was a poli- tician in the deepest and best sense. He could not live in this world without doing his level best to be its salt if not its sugar, and to leave the city life, the social and municipal life of his time a purer and healthier thing than it would have been had he not been born. 1 (Applause). In this remote part of the eountry— (Laughter)—his fame as a Councillor of the municipal- ity of Lambeth did not seem to have arrived. (Laughter). He deliberately undertook that municipal office in order that he might show by his example how greatly he valued those great English institutions which had come down to us from the past, and howhe thought every Christian man was bound to permeate them with the principles of the gospel of Jesus Christ which was the crowning thing of our municipal and social as well as our national and religious life. It was a beautiful thing to realize what had been done within the last thirteen yeers. They had lived once with each denomination surrounded by its high walls. Dr. MacLaren told them that on those walls was glass which would cut a man's fingers were he to attempt to scale them. But the glass had gone, and THE WALLS HAD. GONE,</p>
        <p>and instead they had only a light Pallisading over which woodbine and honeysuckle were now growing —,laughter and applause)—and each one of them could stand in his garden and talk across to his neighbour, reaching his hand in brotherly sympathy. They had learnt that the success of one lay in the success of all, and that not one reache</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>..,..,,.„,... ...,.....,,..,,.:•..",..-47;q4,47.....-t-:;?..;;;.,t;.',..•,-;1.:.:!...`,.?...f..;x. .., ... . „. . . .. .. .</head>
        <p>..,..,,.„,... ...,.....,,..,,.:•..",..-47;q4,47.....-t-:;?..;;;.,t;.',..•,-;1.:.:!...`,.?...f..;x. .., ... . „. . . .. .. .</p>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING</p>
        <p>SOUSA AND HIS BAND.</p>
        <p>On Saturday we went to see and hear Sousa and his band. Usually one is content to hear a band, even at a distance, but in this case Sousa and his band possess joint attractions which must not be missed, and which cannot be enjoyed separately. If you want to listen to the band you must needs also watch Sousa,. and if you are merely anxious to watch the conductor you will have difficulty in ignoring the presence of the instrumentalists. The best plan is to pay your money and then see and hear all you can for the outlay. In combination Sousa and his band are excellent. What either would be without the other we shudder to contemplate, but, probably the loss of the con- ductor would be a less serions_mattPr than .the loss of</p>
        <p>...._ .._ the band. Sousa without his instrumentalists might pall upon one after a time ; as it is he provides an appreciable diversion. This is the second visit to Warrington, and good audiences again testified to the popularity of the "March King" and his merry men. They gave two performances—afternoon and evening—with a different programme of music each time, so that those enthusiasts with case-hardened ear- drums who went twice got some enjoyment out of both concerts. We hardly need go into detail about the selections which formed the repertoire for the day. They were extensive, varied, and served to bring out the striking capabilities of both Sousa and his band. The former is no ordinary conductor, and he ought really to wield a magic wand or a be-jewelled baton instead of the plain white wood instrument which does duty. It is due to the Americanism which permeates the whole show that Sousa should avoid the conventional in this par- ticular as in others and we make the sug- gestion for what it is worth. To see Sousa extracting the most delicate nitancts of ex- pression with prehensile kid-gloved fingers, and opening his arms to a tornado of sound from the brasses, is a sight not to be forgotten in a moment. Anon he may interpret Chopin by a sort of chopping motion with the baton which suggests slaughter amid the wheat stalks. In any pose or posture Sousa is, well—Sousa, and he is meant to be seen. The band is a fine company of talented musicians, and when it , is in full blast on some soul-inspiring march or descrip- tive suit, it produces an effect comparable with nothing in the realm of music. But the performance is not 1 all noise. Frequently the bandsmen subdue their i efforts till the faintest whisper only is proceeding from the instruments, and they are as capable of t the sweet and low as they are of the crash and bang 't which is more especially their fort.. A word should be added with regard to their matchless "time " and i the military precision with which they swing off into ; the most brilliant selection without a moment's notice. 1 High training and magnificent instrumentation are as obvious as is the striving after effect which is not an objectionable characteristic. It was typical of Sousa and his band to give as an encore the popular panto- ~ 1 mine song, " Blue-bell." Most bands would have dis- dained anythine, so un-classical, but not so Sousa's. They played '''' Blue-bell" •e suite, in sections, and -.2 with every conceivable variation except backwards, and nothing pleased he generality of the audience msre. But Sousa and his band set out to please, and they succeeded admirably. As on the previous visit the band programme was interspersed by songs from Miss Estelle Liebling, a soprano of remarkable quality and power, and Miss Maud Powell, violinist. The latter gave a fine interpre- tation of " Zigeunerweisen," which was also the principal violin solo by Mr. Alfred Ross at the Musical Society's concert earlier in the week, so that many members of the audience were afforded an opportunity of an interesting comparison. A word should finally be said to the credit of Messrs. R. H. Pearson, Limited, who arranged the welcome re-visit of Sousa and his band. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-03-11_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>Memoriam.</head>
        <p>Memoriam.</p>
        <p>In loving memory of my dear husband, George Savage who departed this life March 11th, 1902. WelMeld Street, Warrington. A loved one from our house is gone A footstep so well known, His willing hands will toil no more For those he loved at home. ANDERTON—In affectionate remembrance of Robert Anderton, who passed away March 12, 1904. From his WIFE and CHILDREN. Cross Keys Hotel, Warrington.</p>
