        <p>THE GEORGE HOTEL WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS LICENCE New Railway Arrangement. __— The Warrington Board of Guardians met at the Workhouse on Wednesday morning. Mr. A. Brooks presided, and there were also present :--Mrs. Clay, Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Revs. Dr. Mercer and T. R. Matthews, Dr. Patterson, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, J. W. Knowles, W. Hickman, J. Billinge, S. Davies, J. Payton, W. Houghton, P. Longton, E. L. Bent, T. H. Ashcroft, W. Flannagan' G. Thompson, T. Ritchie, R. Bolton, R. ' ' Forshaw, M. W. Seton S. Suttan (deputy clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of Padgate schools). Messrs. William and Segar Owen, architects, were also in attendance. THE SCHOOLS. On the recommendation of the School Committee the Board accepted the tender of Mr. Stringer, Stockton Heath, to repair the asphelt in the school yard for £4O, and that of Mr. J. Banks to supply 224 quilts at 3s. 6d. each. NEW RAILWAY SIDING—IMPORTANT ARRANGEMENT. The House Committee recommended the acceptance of the tender of Messrs. Clare and Ridgway for 100 tons of granite at 6s. 4d. per ton. The Master sug - gested the desirability of approaching the Cheshire Lines Committee with a view to having a special siding for the delivery of fuel and materials in bulk to the Workhouse and thus avoiding charges for carting. The master was directed to unoffi- cially approach the Cheshire Lines Committee and report to the committee. At a subsequent meeting a letter was received from the Cheshire Lines Committee stating that the manager would recommend the com- mittee to incur the cost of putting in a siding near the Workhouse if the Boardwould provide chutes which must be erected to the satisfaction of the engineer who said they could be easily erected at a cost of about £2O each. The House Committee recommended the Board to agree to the terms and undertake to provide three chutes. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY moved the adoption of the minutes He thought the Cheshire Lines Committee had treated the Board very well indeed. (Hear, hear). Some discussion followed, Mr. IeNOWLES expressing doubt as to whether much would be gained by having the siding. Certain classes of material would no don bt have to be carted to different parts of the establishment after they had been delivered by means of the chute. He moved an amendment that the matter be further considered by the committee. Mr. THOMPSON seconded. On a vote being taken, the amendment was defeated and the minutes were approved. ROMAN CATHOLIC RELIGIOUS INSTRUCTOR. A letter was read from the secretary to the Roman Catholic Bishop of Liverpool stating that his lordship suggested the name of the Rev. Thomas Butler, priest in charge of St. Peter's Woolston, for the office of religious instructor to the Catholic children in Padgate Cottage Homes. The appointment of the Rev. T. Butler was agreed to by the Board. A discussion on the proposed Workhouse extension is reported in another column.</p>
