        <p>McDOUGALL'S Pategt"SELF—SELRAISING FLOURThe Examiner Saturday, March 11, 1905. WOMAN'S REALM. • DRESS—FASHION—HOME. 4.VETAHTs WITH OCCULT DESIGNS." According to a German spiritist newspaper, a certain medium, a lady, who has shewn remarkable powers in drawing singularly beautiful designs while in a state of trance, has been engaged by a firm of plush and velvet manufacturers as one of their designers. Several of her weird designs have already been stamped in furniture velvets for cover- ing sofas, Sm. The firm in question advertises the lady's work as "velvets with occult designs." WOMEN TRACERS AT CHATHAM DOCKYARD. Six women tracers have, it is stated, been appointed for duty in the drawing-office at Chatham Dockyard, and six for the drawing-office at Sheer- ness Yard. A chargewoman has also been appointed to each yard to take the oversight of these new employees. Other women tracers will be selected later on, as they are required, and corresponding reductions will be made in the number of male tracers employed. WALKING COSTUME. Our sketch shews a neat-looking walking costume carried out in the ever-useful blue serge. The skirt is well shaped and many gored, and round the bottom a series of short straps are placed up each seam. The short, closely-fitting coat is laid in narrow pleats, is double-breasted, and fastens with small silver buttons, which also appear on the skirt strappings. A small collar, cut off square in front, finishes off the neck, and is composed of</p>
        <p>chocolate, and jam industries made such progress that two or three years ago we practically controlled the export trade of the world under those three ' keads. We had some sugar planters in the West Indies, many of whom were disgracefully idle and lived upon the produces of black labour, who never applied any modern inventions to their industries, who had friends in court. Under pressure from those friends we went to Germany and France and persuaded them to stop giving bounties on sugar, for the benefit of the West Indian planters, upon our agreeing to refuse to allow bounty fed sugar from any country to enter England. The result has been the stoppage of the supply of cheap sugar from Russia and Argentine, and as a consequence the price of sugar had gone up and had had a serious effect upon those indus- tries which depended for their existence and prosperity upon cheap sugar. Ten thousand workpeople had been thrown out of work and 25,000 had been placed upon short time and our German and French competi- tors were erecting works and putting down machinery as fast as they could to compete for the export trade in confectionery, jam, AC, which formerly almost exclu- stvely belonged to our manufacturers. it would have been thought that a Government which entered into such a convention and which professed to believe in retaliation would have seen that there was fair treat- ment as the result of the convention, yet though we refused bounty-fed sugar we allowed German and French manufactures, made from bounty-fed suer to enter the country by only paying duty in proportion to the sup" used in their making, while British jams, &amp;Q., had to pay duty upon entering Germany or France, on the whole article. That showed how utterly incapable as business men were the members of the present Government. (Applause). The vote was heartily accorded. Mr. Lever briefly responded, and in doing so moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Longland for his services in the chair. Mr. Mounfleld seconded the vote which was equally heartily carried, Mr. Longland briefly returning thanks.</p>
