        <p>THE EX.A MINER. MARCH Mb. 1905.</p>
        <p>The distressing sewer fatality which created a sensation in the town on Monday was probably the first of the kind in the history of the Borough. Saddening as the details are, one cannot resist a feeling of admiration for the heroism which brightens somewhat the tragic record. The deceased man, Baxter, went to his doom in an endeavour to rescue two comrades who had been over-powered by the fumes, and when three men lay unconscious in the man-hole a carter named Stockton, at the risk of losing his own life,bravely entered and extricated them one by one. His conduct is certainly deserving of that recognition on the part of the Royal Humane Society which the jury was unanimous in recommending. FOUND THAT FOR QUALITY MID VALUS IN IVINBS AND SPIRITS FULTON BROS. CANNOT fll BBATIIN. FULTON BROS. 20. RIINSHAW STRRET, uveapooL. ALPO AT FULTON'S BUILDINGS, ROCEIDAIB. Telephone 2261 Royal, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>bPRING NOVELTIES NOW SHOWING. W. T. vki ILLIAMS' NWN' COSTUMES, MILLI THRY, DRESSES, COT. &amp;c., &amp;c. 48, SANKEY STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS. Index to Inside Pages. • PAGE 2. Liberal Meeting at Stockton Heath. • The Band of Festival. • The George Hotel Licence. Fortune-Teller Fined. 3. •"The Ghost of Bridan's Fire-Line " by Bertram Mitford. Warrington Men Gassed. Science Notes. 0. Workhouse Extension Scheme. Town Council. Police News. Wycliffe P.S.A. Annual Meeting. 7. Football. Billiards. H. M. Asmos's LIFE: TONIC is the XING or TONICS.-63. SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Coining Events. In place of the usual sermon to-morrow (Sunday) Inorning at Bewsey-road the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees Will give a description of a wonderful Welsh Revival Bervice that he recently attended. Mr. Rees has lately had an exceptional opportunity of seeing the Work of the great services in South Wales and of form- ing an opinion as to its effects. The exhibition of paintings, portraits of the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Bolton), by Mr, James Charles, the well-known Warrington artist, will be on view at the Town Hall, to-day. On Friday evening Professor S. J. Chapman, M.A., • iilllecture in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street on Social Questions: Co-operatlon." The annual general meeting of the Warrington and District Bowling League will be held at the Brook- . lands Hotel on Tuesday, March 21st. A brass band contest arranged by Messrs. Cros- fields' Band, will be held on the Football Ground on kay 13th. A meeting in aid of the Uganda Medical Mission ' of the C.M.S. will be held in the Parr Hall on Monday evening. An open meeting of the Warrington P.S.A. will be held tomorrow afternoon. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER ` CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. • Women's Liberal Association. A meeting of the Women's Liberal Association will be held at the Liberal Club on Monday, the 20th,when an address will be given by Mr. J. Howes on the corn- ing General Election. a, , Licensing Change. At the Borough Police Court, on Monday, Mr. Steel aPplied on be'ialf of Mr. William Moore, for teinpor- rY to sell at the Mason's Arms Mill-street, , IA lieu of Mrs. Reynolds, deceased. The application Was granted. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY ST., WARRINGTON.—Advt. Social at Messrs. Crosfield's. A very pleasant evening was spent in the assembly tc3ora of Messrs. Crosfield's works on Saturday when a Programme of vocal and instrumental music and Tee:nations was given by the workpeople to an audience , of over 300, which included the directors, managers, I Toren:ten, workpeople, and friends. The latter part of the programme was a selection from the opeia "Merrie ' ng and," which was very ably given. At the con- . tlusion of the concert dancing to music supplied by d' 4tesals. Crosfield's band was indulged in. PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST °°l's IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Concert in the Co-operative Hall. There was a large attendance on Thursday evening, attheCo-operative Hall, Sankey-street when a concert arranged by Mr. T. Brough was given. The principal , contributors to an excellent programme were the _ .srrington Harmonic Juvenile choir, whose singing c-retieeted the greatest credit upon the training and vonductorship of Mr. T. Brough. The choir, which is ory nicely balanced, gave a number of choruses with 4,3cellent taste and expression, and was rewarded by rile heartily expressed approval of the audience. The proe.eedings were pleasingly varied by selections given 54 the excellent Apollo Male quartette. Instrumental items were introduced by Mr. F. Bates aud the humour c,of the evening was capably sustained by Mr. E. A. Joanders.</p>
        <p>A PROSPEROUS LOCAL INDUSTRY. The present state of the trade was satisfactory under all circumstances, and he believed they would have no I reason to be ashamed when they inet again at their f annual meeting. He would like to thank the whole of nnual Meeting of the Richmond Gas the staff for their loyal services—from the office boy to the heads of the departments, for he felt the success of Stove and Meter Co., Ltd. the company had been the result of that loyalty.(Hear, hear). The report was unanimously adopted. AN ORDER FOR THE VATICAN. THE DIVIDEND. A highly satisfactory year's working was under review The Citairtmas proposed that a dividend at the rate of 10 per cent. per annum free of income tax (less five at the fourteenth annual meeting of the Richmond Gas per cent, already paid) and a bonus of 4 per cent. for Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., held at the registered office the 12 months ended December 31st, 1934 be now of the Company, Academy-street, Warrington, on Thurs- declared anti paid. day. Mr. Thomas Dwerryhouse (chairman of directors) Mr. JOHN CoxoN seconded : He thought the share- presided Hand the other directors present were t holders were to be congratulated upon the balance sheet , : . I Messrs. oce M. Thornton (vice-chairman and manag- which had been placed before them. He would like to ing, m director), John Coxon (Warrington,) J. F. Atkins, take that op of the officers of the companyportunity, of saying that if it had not been .M .8., (Birmingham), J. A. Ransoe (managing direc- for the zeal chairman downwards, the result could not ' have been from the tor), and J. W. Glover (Chelsea). The attendance also , included :—Alderman Smethurst, Messrs. Duff, Booth, , attained. He thought all who read the balance sheet would admit that the financial position of the concern E. J. Hall (Messrs. Henry Greenall and Co.), W. H. Sherburn, Barlow, Newcombe, L. A. Voisey (auditor), was unassailable and as a director he could assure the mad J. W. Slack (secretary). shareholders that the whole of the company's affairs THE REPORT. were in admirable order. (Applause.) In their report the directors stated that the balance The resolution was agreed to. standing to the credit of profit and loss account (after deducting debenture interest) including the amount THE AUDITOR. brought forward as per balance sheet was £31,080 14s 9d, , The CHAIRMAN explained that hitherto they had had which the directors recommended be appropriated as two auditors but now that the registered office was at follows :—Depreciation on buildings, patterns, plant, Warrington, it would only be necessary to appoint one. showroom fixtures, &amp;c., £4,335 165.; dividend on prefer- Mr. L. A. Voisey was recommended for the appoint- ence capital at the rate of 6 per cent, per anuum, for ment. His father took part in the formation of the year ended December 31st, 1901, £5,743 6s, Bd. ; divi- concern and Mr. Voisey had given great satisfaction by dend (free of income tax) at the rate of 10 per cent. per the way in which he had discharged the duties. annum on the ordinary capital for the year ended Mr. BOOTH moved : "That Mr. L. A. Voisey be December 31st, 1904, £8,034 14s. ; bonus (free of income re-appointed auditor of the accounts of the company at tax) at the rate of 21 per cent, per annum, £2,008 13s 6d; a remuneration of £l5O for the year 1905." He looked reserve fund (bringing up this fund and the branch upon the staff of the company as being second to none reserve fund to £28,158 95.), £5,000 •, carrying forward a and he trusted that success would attend their efforts balance to next account, subject to directors' remunera- in the future. tion, of £5,958 4s. 7d. The business of the Company Mr. E. J. HALL seconded. He had been associated continued to be satisfactory, and it was gratifying to with Mr. Voisey in many companies and he knew that the directors that they were able, after placing entire his interest in the concerns to which he was appointed cost of advertising as in former years against revenue, I auditor was not merely a professional one. (Hear, to recommend a dividend at the rate of 10 per cent., hear.) He expressed the hops that the accounts Mr. and a bonus of 2,1 per cent, in addition. The Company's ' Voisey would have to audit would always be as works and various branches were in good condition, and pleasant as those which were presented that day. had been maintained out of Revenue. During the year The motion was carried and Mr. VOISEY briefly the Directors had invested the sum of £4,992 7s. Gd.out returned thanks. It gave him very great pleasure to be of the reserve fund. They recommended the transfer connected with such a successful and prosperous com- a the branch reserve fund to general reserve fund. pany and the balance sheet submitted was a striking The Directors regretted to record the death of Mr. pnoof of the excellent management on the part of the James Atkins, one of the directors of the,company directors and officials. , The retiring directors were Mr. Thomas Dwerryhouse REMUNERATION FOR DIRECTORS. and Mr. H. NI. Thornton. Their re-election by the Mr. DUFF moved: That the sum of £650 be placed Board was reported in accordance with the Company's at year the disposal of the directors for their services during Articles of Association. The Auditor Mr. L. A. Voisey, Chartered accountant, retired and was recommended the Alderman SMETHURST seconded. He was ORS of the for re-appointment, original shareholders in the company and it was highly THE CHAIRMAN'S SPEECH. gratifying to findthat the balance sheet was one of the The CHAIRMAN moved : "That the directors' report best hehad seen in his life. What pleased him most of and statement of accounts for the year ended December all was to see that the reserve fund was being invested 31st, 1904, as certified by the auditors, be received and in stocks and consols. adopted." He said that no doubt the shareholders had The resolution was passed. derived as much satisfaction in perusing the report as A vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman on the directors had in issuing it. The reports and accounts, the motion of Mr. HALL who congratulated Mr.Dwerry- he considered, showed the sound state of the company. house upon his improved state of health. (Hear, hear). They had had several advantages,amongst them good seasons—a warm summer and a cold winter which suited the business very well. The staff had worked it for what it was worth,as the accounts showed. In the middle of the year a gas exhibition was held in London and the directors were asked to join the project when it was in a very nebulous state. They could see that by doing so they would be helping the company very much so they i fell n with the scheme and secured a very good site. Mr. Thornton attended to the arrangements and the firm had a capital show. Considering that the visitors were gas engineers, managers, and officials the company had to do their very best to show what they were capable of producing. They had a very plea-ting assort- ment of goods on view, and although the outlay was large the exhibition had helped them very considerably and would do so for some time to come. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Dwerryhouse sympathetically alluded to the death of Mr. J. Atkins, a director, and said it had been decided not to fill his place on the Board. Hitherto, he continued, the company had had offices in London and Warrington, but it was thought it would be an advantage to have one office. The whole of the books had therefore been removed to Warrington where it had been decided the registered office should be, and the result would be an economy of money and time by having everything under one supervision. Mr. Dwerryhouse proceeded to explain the various items of the accounts in detail. He pointed out that the amount due to creditors was very stuall—£9,ooo as compared with £7,000 last year, and that showed the company was in a much better position. The trade stocks at Warrington, Stratford, London and branches had been reduced and that, he considered, was a good thing. The smaller the stock they could carry on the business with the better, because they made a profit at once. The total stock was valued at £35.698 2s. 4d. as against £42,000 last year. The amount due from sundry debtors—£46,273 4s. 9d.—looked large but they did a big business and therefore he did not think the sum was out of the way. It might be said that a lot of debts which were not quite good were included, but he would point out that their average bad debts for the last three years were only £llB per year. This year the amount was £237, but that was nothing to make a fuss about. Mr. Dwerryhouse referred to the fact that about £5,000 of the reserve fund had been invested during the year, and that, he thought, showed a healthy state of affairs. The money had been invested as follows :—The Gas Light and Coke Co., Ltd, 3 per cent. Debenture Stock, £995 14s. 6d. ;L. and N.-W. Railway Co., Con- solidated Stock £1,998 12s. 6d. ; South Metropolitan Gas Co., 3 per cent. Debenture Stock, £998 Os. Gd. ; and 2; per cent. Consols, £l,OOO. In regard to the disposal of the balance, he would not like to think that the bonus of 21 per cent, would be the last, but that depended upon a great many things. They might congratu- late themselves on having a very good staff who would do their utmost to maintain the present prosperous position, but the continuance of the bonus depended in a great measure upon their friends Mr. Thornton and Mr. Ransome. In adopting the report the shareholders would be asked to approve the merging of the branch reserve fund into the general reserve fund, which would then total .£28,158 a very respectable sum, considering theshort time they had been in business. Everything seemed to be going on well, and if they continued to prosper as they had done during the past year he would be very happy and he thought they, as shareholders, would be happpy too. (Applause). Mr. H. M. TflormoNr said he had the greatest possible pleasure in seconding. That was a unique occasion for three reasons; firstly because they had to report the largest year, as regarded returns, that the company had ever experienced; secondly, the profit was the largest the company had ever made; and in the third place,they had paid back the original shareholders their original capital and rather more, in dividends. In looking at previous balance sheets he found the company had not paid less than 10 per cent, dividend on ordinary shares since 1895, and on two occasions, including the present one, they had paid a bonus of 21 per cent. He was sure that in the gas industry the name of Richmond stood for progress. (Hear, hear.) Not only was it progress in business but progress in regard to profits. As a rule, he found those two things went together, and if they were not progressive they were not likely to see their profits increase. Two or three other things particularly struck him in looking through the balance sheet—the persist- ent and steady advance in the profits of the company, in the company's reserve, and in the depreciation allow- ance for the buildings and plant. Another very notice- able feature was the steady decrease in the stocks. In looking through the profits, he found that in 1901 £lB,OOO were made, in 1902 £23,000, in 1903 £27,000 and in 1904 £31,000, inclusive of the amounts brought forward each year. That showed an increase during the four years of £13,000 which, he thought, substanti- ated his statement that the firm was progressing. (Hear, hear.) The reserve fund in 1901 was £15,000, in 1902 £lB,OOO, in 1903 £23,000 and in 1904 £28,000. The depreciation allowance for the four years amounted to £15,500 which, of course, could be considered a sort of reserve, inasmuch as they maintained out of revenue their buildings, plant, and everything that should be maintained. One of the most satisfactory features of the balance sheet was that the trade stocks which in 1901 stood at £54,000 were to-day only £35,000.Yet, with that'distinctly lower stock they had a larger turn- over. All that had not been accomplished without the greatest anxiety on the part of the directors and the management. They had had to face bad trade in the country generally, as well as the severest competition in their own business. They bad faced that competition more particularly by carefully going into their manu- facturing costs, and they were daily doing something towards meeting the decline in prices here and there. Of course, they had also taken advantage of the very low prices of raw materials and their contracts had been made with the greatest care. Their general trade during the year had been an improving one, and they had had the largest turnover they had ever experienced. Mr. Thornton briefly alluded to the Earl's Court ex- hibition and remarked that although they could not exactly say it had brought them in large amount of business they felt sure that if they had not been repre- sented they would have lost something by it. It was interesting to know that they renewed last year their contract with the Dublin Gas Company—which had been one of their most successful contracts. They had also arranged with the Gas, Light and Coke Company, the largest company in the world, a most important contract since the last annual meeting, and it would last another eighteen months. They had been fortunate too, in installing in the Vatican at Rome for his Holiness the Pope a complete cooking plant. They had cause to feel gratified at that achievement as it was not usual perhaps for people of continental nations to go outside their own countries for the purpose of obtaining anything of the kind. They felt honoured and proud to think that the Italians had considered their goods so perfect that they wanted English-made articles in the Vatican itself. They had also been privileged to supply his Majesty the Ring with a complete set of gas apparatus for one of the palaces and they felt there was hardly any portion of work in the country that the firm did not get some interest in. About 500,000 of their stoves were in daily use in the country, and he thought that was a remarkable achievement. (Hear, hear.)</p>
