        <p>Wkoa a ihayer can easily screw back into twat favm any of the positions shown on Diagram 60, he should place the object ball farther and far- ther away, 'and in fact not untit_a player can easily screw back into baulk frcm a bill placed on the centre spot (cue . ball on the baulk lire) can he be said to be really proficient in screw backs. It may be argued that it can seldom or never be the game tOttempt screw-backs with tliN.ocue and first object ball at anything like this distance apart, and therefore it is quite unnecessary to be DIAGiAM 69.</p>
        <p>• , 1111111 A serk. f ura.etioe screw-backs. ante to play such big screw-backs. Abdit, how- ever, to screw back from a ball a long way off ren- ders moderate range screw-backs far easier of ac- complishment than when a player at his best can- not screw back from a ball more than 18 inches or so away. • The centre spot by no means marks the limit from which it is possible to screw straight back ' into baulk, and at the ehd of this chapter refer- ence will be made to some big screw strokes which have been accomplished as fancy strokes and which illustrate what is Possible in the yrs, of a screw stroke. If a player wishes to become proficient ia screw strokes he must not only practis• screwing back from a ball a long way off, but also 'screwing back a long way from a near object ball., One who excels at the one stroke will, howevers generally excel at the other, for the strokes ar4 practically the same. Hand, clean hitting II essential in both. DIAGRAM 70. •</p>
        <p>eti • g • 0. se • f tip ,r 40, A stroke in which the cue ball screws back tan full length of the table. Red ball half an inch from the side cushion and 4 inches from the baulk cushion. Cue ball about 6or 8 inches from the red and 2 inches or so from the side cushion.</p>
        <p>Diagram 70 shows a good position for practie• lug screwing back a long way. By potting the red there is no kiss to avoid, and players with plenty of power cf cue should easily screw up the table half-way past the centre pocket, and at their beet attempts even ,right up to the top cushion, and some distance down again. A very, interesting contest can be had between two good players by seeing who can screw back the farthest from such a position. I have seen an amateur using balls 'of. absolutely the same weight, screw back from the position given in Diagram 69 to the cushion and down the table again to a point quite four feet from the top cushion, (To be Continued.) Vol. I. of "Billiards: The Strokes of the Game." 100 illustrations. By iIISO LEVI. Ie now ready, la. net. Mr. Riso Levi will be pleased to answer in these columns all questions relative to the article appearing weekly, and as far as possible an questions on other points ia connection with the: game of Billiards. Envelopes should be marked' "Billiards."</p>
